INSERT INTO _s463b VALUES
(4268,'T',46,1,46,1,$n$*And it came to pass that after Jacob died the children of Yashar’el (Israel) multiplied in the land of Egypt, and they became a great nation, and they were of one accord in heart, so that brother loved brother and every man helped his brother, and they increased abundantly and multiplied exceedingly, ten weeks of years, all the days of the life of Joseph.* (Jubilees 46:1). *When he arose, he took the young child and his mother by night, and departed into Egypt:* (Matthew 2:14). Jubilees opens its account of Egypt with a people at rest there. After Jacob died the children of Yashar'el (Israel) multiplied, became a great nation, and loved one another all the days of Joseph; Egypt was a shelter before it was a prison. Matthew's Joseph takes the child down the same road by night, and Egypt shelters him too, until Herod is dead. The child walks the path his people walked, a sojourn in Egypt and a calling out of it, so that what happened to the nation happens again in the one who came for the nation.$n$,$n$And it came to pass that after Jacob died the children of Yashar’el (Israel) multiplied in the land of Egypt, and they became a great nation, and they were of one accord in heart, so that brother loved brother and every man helped his brother, and they increased abundantly and multiplied exceedingly, ten weeks of years, all the days of the life of Joseph.$n$,true),
(4279,'T',2,20,2,20,$n$*And I have chosen the seed of Jacob from amongst all that I have seen, and have written him down as My firstborn son, and have sanctified him to Myself for ever and ever; and I will teach them the Sabbath day, that they may keep Sabbath thereon from all work.”* (Jubilees 2:20). *And was there until the death of Herod: that it might be fulfilled which was spoken of Yahuah (Lord) by the prophet, saying, Out of Egypt have I called my son.* (Matthew 2:15). On the day the Sabbath is made, before Abraham, before Egypt, Yahuah (God) says He has chosen the seed of Jacob and written him down as His firstborn son. That writing is older than every event that follows it. When Matthew quotes the prophet, *Out of Egypt have I called my son*, the son in the prophet's mouth is the firstborn son named here at creation, the seed of Jacob brought up out of Egypt. Matthew says the child in Egypt fulfilled that word. The one who came for the firstborn walked the firstborn's road, and *my son* was written over the people before it was spoken over the child.$n$,$n$And I have chosen the seed of Jacob from amongst all that I have seen, and have written him down as My firstborn son, and have sanctified him to Myself for ever and ever; and I will teach them the Sabbath day, that they may keep Sabbath thereon from all work.”$n$,true),
(4284,'T',32,34,32,34,$n$*And Rachel died there and she was buried in the land of Ephrath, the same is Bethlehem, and Jacob built a pillar on the grave of Rachel, on the road above her grave.* (Jubilees 32:34). *In Rama was there a voice heard, lamentation, and weeping, and great mourning, Rachel weeping for her children, and would not be comforted, because they are not.* (Matthew 2:18). Jubilees places Rachel's grave where Genesis does, in the land of Ephrath, which is Bethlehem, with Jacob's pillar on the road above it. That is the road Herod's soldiers came down. Matthew reaches for Jeremiah's picture of Rachel weeping for her children because the mother of Joseph and Benjamin lies at the door of the town where the children were killed. The weeping is not a figure chosen at random; it is the voice of the one buried there, and the children slain at Bethlehem fell within sight of her pillar.$n$,$n$And Rachel died there and she was buried in the land of Ephrath, the same is Bethlehem, and Jacob built a pillar on the grave of Rachel, on the road above her grave.$n$,true),
(4306,'T',10,1,10,1,$n$*And in the third week of this jubilee the unclean demons began to lead astray the children of the sons of Noah; and to make to err and destroy them.* (Jubilees 10:1). *But when he saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees come to his baptism, he said unto them, O generation of vipers, who hath warned you to flee from the wrath to come?* (Matthew 3:7). The flood did not drown the spirits. Jubilees dates their return to the third week after it, leading astray the grandchildren of Noah and making them err. When John looks at the religious leaders coming down to his baptism and calls them a generation of vipers, he is naming a brood, a seed, and the same spirits that led Noah's grandchildren astray had by then made a religion out of it. The wrath to come that John warns them of is the wrath Noah's generation did not believe in either.$n$,$n$And in the third week of this jubilee the unclean demons began to lead astray the children of the sons of Noah; and to make to err and destroy them.$n$,true),
(4308,'T',10,8,10,8,$n$*And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say to them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men; for these are for corruption and leading astray before my judgment, for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.”* (Jubilees 10:8). *Then was Yahusha (Jesus) led up of the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted of the devil.* (Matthew 4:1). Mastema does not seize his place; he asks for it. He stands before Yahuah (God) and asks that some of the spirits be left to him so that he can lead the sons of men astray before the judgment, and the answer is *Let the tenth part of them remain before him* (Jubilees 10:9). So when Matthew says Yahusha (Jesus) was led up of the Spirit to be tempted of the devil, the tempter is not an intruder working outside the will of Yahuah (God). He is the one who asked for room to test the sons of men and was given it. The Spirit led the Son of Adam straight into that permitted testing, and he came out of it whole.$n$,$n$And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say to them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men; for these are for corruption and leading astray before my judgment, for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.”$n$,true),
(4309,'T',11,5,11,11,$n$*And the prince Mastêmâ sent ravens and birds to devour the seed which was sown in the land, in order to destroy the land, and rob the children of men of their labours. Before they could plough in the seed, the ravens picked (it) from the surface of the ground.* (Jubilees 11:11). *And when the tempter came to him, he said, If thou be the Son of Elohim (God), command that these stones be made bread.* (Matthew 4:3). Before Abraham was born, Mastema was already at war with bread. He sent the ravens to take the seed off the ground before it could be ploughed in, so that men's labour fed nothing and the land went hungry, and Terah's name carries the memory of it. In the wilderness the tempter comes to a hungry man with the same subject. He does not offer Yahusha (Jesus) a kingdom first; he offers bread, and tells him to make it himself rather than wait on the Father who feeds. The one who stole the bread from Terah's fields is the one who held it out in the wilderness, and he was refused.$n$,$n$And the prince Mastêmâ sent ravens and birds to devour the seed which was sown in the land, in order to destroy the land, and rob the children of men of their labours. Before they could plough in the seed, the ravens picked (it) from the surface of the ground.$n$,true),
(4310,'T',17,16,17,16,$n$*And the prince Mastêmâ came and said before Elohim (God), “Behold, Abraham loves Isaac his son, and he delights in him above all things else; bid him offer him as a burnt-offering on the altar, and You will see if he will do this command, and You will know if he is faithful in everything wherein You do try him.”* (Jubilees 17:16). *Then the devil taketh him up into the holy city, and setteth him on a pinnacle of the temple,* (Matthew 4:5). Jubilees lets the reader hear what Genesis leaves silent: the testing of Abraham began with Mastema standing before Elohim (God) and asking for it. His argument is that love must be proven by a command obeyed, and he names the dearest thing. The devil on the pinnacle makes the same move, pressing the Son to prove his sonship by a visible act. Abraham was tested with Isaac; Yahusha (Jesus) was tested with himself. In both the accuser proposed the trial, Yahuah (God) allowed it, and the faithful one came through it without giving the accuser what he asked for.$n$,$n$And the prince Mastêmâ came and said before Elohim (God), “Behold, Abraham loves Isaac his son, and he delights in him above all things else; bid him offer him as a burnt-offering on the altar, and You will see if he will do this command, and You will know if he is faithful in everything wherein You do try him.”$n$,true),
(4463,'T',23,27,23,27,$n$*And the days will begin to grow many and increase amongst those children of men, Till their days draw nigh to one thousand years, And to a greater number of years than (before) was the number of the days.* (Jubilees 23:27). *The blind receive their sight, and the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the poor have the gospel preached to them.* (Matthew 11:5). Jubilees looks ahead to the days of men growing long again, back toward a thousand years, and the verses that follow fill it in: no old man, no Satan, no evil destroyer, days of blessing and healing (Jubilees 23:28–29). When John sends from prison to ask whether Yahusha (Jesus) is the one, the answer he gets is a list of bodies being mended, the blind seeing, the lame walking, the dead raised. Those are the first drops of the healing Jubilees puts at the end of the age. The King showed the signs of that restoration in his first coming and held back the rest; in Nazareth he had read Isaiah's *acceptable year of Yahuah (LORD)* and stopped before *the day of vengeance of our Elohim (God)* (Isaiah 61:2). The healing came first; the vengeance waits for his return.$n$,$n$And the days will begin to grow many and increase amongst those children of men, Till their days draw nigh to one thousand years, And to a greater number of years than (before) was the number of the days.$n$,true),
(4466,'T',1,23,1,23,$n$*And after this they will turn to Me in all uprightness and with all (their) heart and with all (their) soul, and I shall circumcise the foreskin of their heart and the foreskin of the heart of their seed, and I shall create in them a holy spirit, and I shall cleanse them so that they shall not turn away from Me from that day to eternity.* (Jubilees 1:23). *Verily I say unto you, Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist: notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.* (Matthew 11:11). John was the greatest born of women, and yet the least in the kingdom is greater than he, not because of anything the least has done but because of what has been done in him. Jubilees names it in the Father's own words: a circumcised heart, a holy spirit created in them, a cleansing that holds from that day to eternity. Jeremiah heard the same promise as *I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts* (Jeremiah 31:33), and Ezekiel as *I will put my spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes* (Ezekiel 36:27). John stood at the door of that promise and pointed at the one who brings it; the ones who walk through carry it inside them. That is the whole difference between the prophet and the least in the kingdom.$n$,$n$And after this they will turn to Me in all uprightness and with all (their) heart and with all (their) soul, and I shall circumcise the foreskin of their heart and the foreskin of the heart of their seed, and I shall create in them a holy spirit, and I shall cleanse them so that they shall not turn away from Me from that day to eternity.$n$,true),
(4467,'T',1,24,1,24,$n$*And their souls will cleave to Me and to all My commandments, and they will fulfil My commandments, and I shall be their Father and they will be My children.* (Jubilees 1:24). *Verily I say unto you, Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist: notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.* (Matthew 11:11). Jubilees gives the order plainly. First the heart is circumcised and a holy spirit created in them (Jubilees 1:23), then their souls cleave to the commandments and fulfil them, and then He is their Father and they are His children. The keeping flows out of the new heart; it does not buy the sonship. That is what makes the least in the kingdom greater than John, not a higher rung on a ladder of works but a bond between Father and child, worked from the inside out. The commandments are not the price of the door. They are what the children of this Father do once they are in.$n$,$n$And their souls will cleave to Me and to all My commandments, and they will fulfil My commandments, and I shall be their Father and they will be My children.$n$,true),
(4468,'T',1,25,1,25,$n$*And they will all be called children of the living Elohim (God), and every angel and every spirit will know, yea, they will know that these are My children, and that I am their Father in uprightness and righteousness, and that I love them.* (Jubilees 1:25). *Verily I say unto you, Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist: notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.* (Matthew 11:11). *Children of the living Elohim (God)* is Hosea's name for the house that had been called Not My People: *in the place where it was said unto them, Ye are not my people, there it shall be said unto them, Ye are the sons of the living Elohim (God)* (Hosea 1:10), and Paul takes the same words up for the same gathering (Romans 9:26). Jubilees has the Father saying it of the ones He cleanses and circumcises in heart, and making every angel and every spirit know it. John announced that gathering; he was the voice at the edge of it. The ones inside the kingdom are the gathered themselves, known in heaven as His children, and that is why the least of them is greater than the one who pointed the way.$n$,$n$And they will all be called children of the living Elohim (God), and every angel and every spirit will know, yea, they will know that these are My children, and that I am their Father in uprightness and righteousness, and that I love them.$n$,true),
(4478,'T',16,5,16,5,$n$*And in this month Yahuah (God) executed his judgments on Sodom, and Gomorrah, and Zeboim, and all the region of the Jordan, and He burned them with fire and brimstone, and destroyed them until this day, even as lo I have declared to you all their works, that they are wicked and sinners exceedingly, and that they defile themselves and commit fornication in their flesh, and work uncleanness on the earth.* (Jubilees 16:5). *And thou, Capernaum, which art exalted unto heaven, shalt be brought down to hell: for if the mighty works, which have been done in thee, had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.* (Matthew 11:23). Jubilees records what Sodom was burned for and that it stays burned until this day. Yahusha (Jesus) reaches for the same city as the measuring rod and says Capernaum will fare worse. Sodom never saw the mighty works Capernaum saw, and had it seen them it would have remained until this day; the city that watched the blind see and the dead rise and did not repent carries a heavier weight than the city whose sins were open. Sight raises the account. The judgment of Sodom is not softened here, it is the floor, and Capernaum is being told it stands below it.$n$,$n$And in this month Yahuah (God) executed his judgments on Sodom, and Gomorrah, and Zeboim, and all the region of the Jordan, and He burned them with fire and brimstone, and destroyed them until this day, even as lo I have declared to you all their works, that they are wicked and sinners exceedingly, and that they defile themselves and commit fornication in their flesh, and work uncleanness on the earth.$n$,true),
(4479,'T',16,6,16,6,$n$*And, in like manner, Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites, like to the judgment of Sodom.* (Jubilees 16:6). *And thou, Capernaum, which art exalted unto heaven, shalt be brought down to hell: for if the mighty works, which have been done in thee, had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.* (Matthew 11:23). Jubilees says the judgment of Sodom is not finished history but a pattern: Elohim (God) will execute it again wherever people do as Sodom did. Yahusha (Jesus) lays that pattern over Capernaum and then sharpens it. It will go harder with the city that saw the mighty works than with the city whose uncleanness earned the fire, because Sodom sinned without ever seeing the King, and Capernaum refused him to his face. The template Jubilees names is still in use, and the one who holds it has told us which cities fall under it.$n$,$n$And, in like manner, Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites, like to the judgment of Sodom.$n$,true),
(4493,'T',2,21,2,21,$n$*And thus He created therein a sign in accordance with which they should keep Sabbath with us on the seventh day, to eat and to drink, and to bless Him who has created all things as He has blessed and sanctified to Himself a peculiar people above all peoples, and that they should keep Sabbath together with us.* (Jubilees 2:21). *For the Son of Adam is Adonai (Lord) even of the sabbath day.* (Matthew 12:8). Jubilees gives the Sabbath as a sign and says what the day is for: to eat, to drink, and to bless the One who created all things. The disciples walking through the grain, plucking ears and eating on the Sabbath, were doing exactly what the day was made for; the ones objecting had hedged the day with rules until eating became a crime. When Yahusha (Jesus) says the Son of Adam is Adonai (Lord) of the Sabbath he is not loosening the day. He is its owner telling what it is, a day for a peculiar people to eat and bless Him, not to go hungry. Jubilees was written long before those fences went up, and it already knew.$n$,$n$And thus He created therein a sign in accordance with which they should keep Sabbath with us on the seventh day, to eat and to drink, and to bless Him who has created all things as He has blessed and sanctified to Himself a peculiar people above all peoples, and that they should keep Sabbath together with us.$n$,true),
(4494,'T',50,12,50,12,$n$*And every man who does any work thereon, or goes a journey, or tills (his) farm, whether in his house or any other place, and whoever lights a fire, or rides on any beast, or travels by ship on the sea, and whoever strikes or kills anything, or slaughters a beast or a bird, or whoever catches an animal or a bird or a fish, or whoever fasts or makes war on the Sabbaths:* (Jubilees 50:12). *How much then is a man better than a sheep? Wherefore it is lawful to do well on the sabbath days.* (Matthew 12:12). This is the strictest Sabbath list in Jubilees, and it is worth reading what is on it: work, journeys, tilling, fire, riding, sailing, killing, hunting, fasting, war. Yahusha (Jesus) does not strike one item off the list. He points out what was never on it. Lifting a sheep out of a pit and healing a withered hand are doing well, not working, and *it is lawful to do well on the sabbath days*. The fence that had grown up around the day read *do no work* as *do no good*, and that is what he answers. Notice that Jubilees itself forbids fasting on the Sabbath; the day was for gladness and mercy from the start, and the Sabbath stands as it always has.$n$,$n$And every man who does any work thereon, or goes a journey, or tills (his) farm, whether in his house or any other place, and whoever lights a fire, or rides on any beast, or travels by ship on the sea, and whoever strikes or kills anything, or slaughters a beast or a bird, or whoever catches an animal or a bird or a fish, or whoever fasts or makes war on the Sabbaths:$n$,true),
(4510,'T',10,11,10,11,$n$*And we did according to all His words: all the malignant evil ones we bound in the place of condemnation, and a tenth part of them we left that they might be subject before Satan on the earth.* (Jubilees 10:11). *Or else how can one enter into a strong man’s house, and spoil his goods, except he first bind the strong man? and then he will spoil his house.* (Matthew 12:29). At Noah's prayer the angels bound nine parts of the evil spirits in the place of condemnation and left a tenth loose on the earth under Satan. Binding is how heaven has dealt with these spirits from the beginning. When Yahusha (Jesus) says no one spoils the strong man's house without first binding the strong man, he is describing the work he was doing in front of them: the tenth that was left is being bound too, and every devil cast out is the house being spoiled. The full binding waits for the thousand years, when the angel lays hold on the dragon and binds him (Revelation 20:2), but it had already begun in Galilee.$n$,$n$And we did according to all His words: all the malignant evil ones we bound in the place of condemnation, and a tenth part of them we left that they might be subject before Satan on the earth.$n$,true),
(4511,'T',15,33,15,34,$n$*And there will be great wrath from Yahuah (God) against the children of Yashar’el (Israel), because they have forsaken His covenant and turned aside from His word, and provoked and blasphemed, inasmuch as they do not observe the ordinance of this law; for they have treated their members like the nations, so that they may be removed and rooted out of the land. And there will no more be pardon or forgiveness to them so that there should be forgiveness and pardon for all the sin of this eternal error.* (Jubilees 15:34). *Wherefore I say unto you, All manner of sin and blasphemy shall be forgiven unto men: but the blasphemy against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) shall not be forgiven unto men.* (Matthew 12:31). Jubilees says a thing most readers do not expect from the book of the covenant: there is a sin for which there will no more be pardon. It is not a slip or a weakness. It is the children of Yashar'el (Israel) knowing the ordinance, forsaking the covenant on purpose, and treating the sign in their own flesh the way the nations do. Yahusha (Jesus) draws the same line in Matthew: every manner of sin and blasphemy can be forgiven, but the one who has watched the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) at work and called it something else has stepped past the place where forgiveness reaches. Both texts describe the same posture, a deliberate turning aside from what one has plainly seen, and both say the same thing about where it ends.$n$,$n$And there will be great wrath from Yahuah (God) against the children of Yashar’el (Israel), because they have forsaken His covenant and turned aside from His word, and provoked and blasphemed, inasmuch as they do not observe the ordinance of this law; for they have treated their members like the nations, so that they may be removed and rooted out of the land. And there will no more be pardon or forgiveness to them so that there should be forgiveness and pardon for all the sin of this eternal error.$n$,true),
(4519,'T',23,30,23,30,$n$*And at that time Yahuah (God) will heal His servants, And they will rise up and see great peace, And drive out their adversaries. And the righteous will see and be thankful, And rejoice with joy for ever and ever, And will see all their judgments and all their curses on their enemies.* (Jubilees 23:30). *For as Jonas was three days and three nights in the whale’s belly; so shall the Son of Adam be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.* (Matthew 12:40). Jubilees says the servants of Yahuah (God) will be healed and will rise up and see great peace. Yahusha (Jesus) gave that generation one sign: three days and three nights in the heart of the earth, as Jonah was in the whale, and then up. His rising is the first of the rising Jubilees promises, the firstfruits of it, and it is the reason the rest can be counted on. The servants rise because their Master rose first, and the adversaries driven out and the peace seen afterward follow him out of the ground.$n$,$n$And at that time Yahuah (God) will heal His servants, And they will rise up and see great peace, And drive out their adversaries. And the righteous will see and be thankful, And rejoice with joy for ever and ever, And will see all their judgments and all their curses on their enemies.$n$,true),
(4521,'T',1,23,1,23,$n$*And after this they will turn to Me in all uprightness and with all (their) heart and with all (their) soul, and I shall circumcise the foreskin of their heart and the foreskin of the heart of their seed, and I shall create in them a holy spirit, and I shall cleanse them so that they shall not turn away from Me from that day to eternity.* (Jubilees 1:23). *Then goeth he, and taketh with himself seven other spirits more wicked than himself, and they enter in and dwell there: and the last state of that man is worse than the first. Even so shall it be also unto this wicked generation.* (Matthew 12:45). A house swept and empty is the picture of a man delivered with nothing put inside him. Jubilees names what must go in: a heart circumcised, a holy spirit created in them, a cleansing that keeps them from turning away. Casting out is half the work; the other half is the Father making the house His own so there is no room when the seven come back. That generation had the devils cast out in front of them and would not take the new heart, and Yahusha (Jesus) says its last state will be worse than the first. Deliverance without the Father moving in is only a vacancy.$n$,$n$And after this they will turn to Me in all uprightness and with all (their) heart and with all (their) soul, and I shall circumcise the foreskin of their heart and the foreskin of the heart of their seed, and I shall create in them a holy spirit, and I shall cleanse them so that they shall not turn away from Me from that day to eternity.$n$,true),
(4525,'T',1,24,1,24,$n$*And their souls will cleave to Me and to all My commandments, and they will fulfil My commandments, and I shall be their Father and they will be My children.* (Jubilees 1:24). *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* (Matthew 12:50). Yahusha (Jesus) names his family by one mark: whoever does the will of my Father. Jubilees had already given the Father's own words for the same bond: their souls cleave to Me and to all My commandments, they fulfil My commandments, I am their Father and they are My children. The will of the Father is not a mystery hovering somewhere above the commandments; it is the commandments. John says it the same way later, *hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments* (1 John 2:3). The kingdom family is known by what it does, and what it does is keep the Father's Torah from a cleaving heart.$n$,$n$And their souls will cleave to Me and to all My commandments, and they will fulfil My commandments, and I shall be their Father and they will be My children.$n$,true),
(4530,'T',11,11,11,11,$n$*And the prince Mastêmâ sent ravens and birds to devour the seed which was sown in the land, in order to destroy the land, and rob the children of men of their labours. Before they could plough in the seed, the ravens picked (it) from the surface of the ground.* (Jubilees 11:11). *And when he sowed, some seeds fell by the way side, and the fowls came and devoured them up:* (Matthew 13:4). Jubilees shows the ravens as no accident of nature: the prince Mastema sent them to eat the seed before it could be ploughed in, to rob men of their labour, and the boy Abram drove them off seventy times in a day (Jubilees 11:19–20). When Yahusha (Jesus) explains his own parable he says the fowls are no accident either: *then cometh the wicked one, and catcheth away that which was sown in his heart* (Matthew 13:19). The birds on the way side are the same birds, sent by the same prince, after the same seed. The parable is not borrowing a farming picture; it is describing a war Jubilees had already named.$n$,$n$And the prince Mastêmâ sent ravens and birds to devour the seed which was sown in the land, in order to destroy the land, and rob the children of men of their labours. Before they could plough in the seed, the ravens picked (it) from the surface of the ground.$n$,true),
(4538,'T',5,1,5,1,$n$*And it came to pass when the children of men began to multiply on the face of the earth and daughters were born to them, that the angels of Elohim (God) saw them on a certain year of this jubilee, that they were beautiful to look upon; and they took themselves wives of all whom they chose, and they bare to them sons and they were giants.* (Jubilees 5:1). *But while men slept, his enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat, and went his way.* (Matthew 13:25). The enemy sowed while men slept, and the first sowing was in the days when the angels of Elohim (God) took wives of the daughters of men and bred giants. Jubilees names it here, 1 Enoch tells it at length (1 Enoch 6), and Genesis 6 keeps it. The tares are not a figure for bad ideas; the parable says they are the children of the wicked one (Matthew 13:38), and this is where that seed first entered the field. No one saw it coming. Men slept, and woke to giants.$n$,$n$And it came to pass when the children of men began to multiply on the face of the earth and daughters were born to them, that the angels of Elohim (God) saw them on a certain year of this jubilee, that they were beautiful to look upon; and they took themselves wives of all whom they chose, and they bare to them sons and they were giants.$n$,true),
(4539,'T',7,21,7,21,$n$*For owing to these three things came the flood upon the earth, namely, owing to the fornication wherein the Watchers against the law of their ordinances went a whoring after the daughters of men, and took themselves wives of all which they chose: and they made the beginning of uncleanness.* (Jubilees 7:21). *The field is the world; the good seed are the children of the kingdom; but the tares are the children of the wicked one;* (Matthew 13:38). Noah tells his sons why the flood came: the Watchers went whoring after the daughters of men against the law of their ordinances, and from that uncleanness came the giants who devoured one another (Jubilees 7:22). Yahusha (Jesus) tells his disciples what the tares are: the children of the wicked one, planted in the same field as the children of the kingdom. Noah's account shows a seed that was never the Father's, growing beside the sons of Adam and devouring them. The flood cut it down once; the parable says it grows again in the field of the world, and the reapers wait for the harvest.$n$,$n$For owing to these three things came the flood upon the earth, namely, owing to the fornication wherein the Watchers against the law of their ordinances went a whoring after the daughters of men, and took themselves wives of all which they chose: and they made the beginning of uncleanness.$n$,true),
(4558,'T',4,2,4,2,$n$*And in the first (year) of the third jubilee, Cain slew Abel because Elohim (God) accepted the sacrifice of Abel, and did not accept the offering of Cain.* (Jubilees 4:2). *The field is the world; the good seed are the children of the kingdom; but the tares are the children of the wicked one;* (Matthew 13:38). The first tare in the field was Cain. Jubilees says plainly why he killed: Elohim (God) accepted Abel's sacrifice and not his offering. John writes the same thing in the parable's own words, *not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother* (1 John 3:12). When Yahusha (Jesus) says the tares are the children of the wicked one, he is not inventing a category; he is naming a line that begins in the first family and runs through the field of the world to the harvest. The two seeds were there from the beginning, and the first field they shared was a literal one.$n$,$n$And in the first (year) of the third jubilee, Cain slew Abel because Elohim (God) accepted the sacrifice of Abel, and did not accept the offering of Cain.$n$,true),
(4590,'T',15,30,15,30,$n$*For Ishmael and his sons and his brothers and Esau, Yahuah (God) did not cause to approach Him, and he chose them not because they are the children of Abraham, because He knew them, but He chose Yashar’el (Israel) to be His people.* (Jubilees 15:30). *And, behold, a woman of Canaan came out of the same coasts, and cried unto him, saying, Have mercy on me, O Adonai (Lord), thou Son of David; my daughter is grievously vexed with a devil.* (Matthew 15:22). Not every child of Abraham is the chosen seed. Jubilees says it flat: Ishmael, his sons, his brothers, Esau, Yahuah (God) did not bring them near, though they were Abraham's children; He chose Yashar'el (Israel) to be His people. The woman crying *Son of David* on the coast of Tyre and Sidon is not standing outside that choice. Yahusha (Jesus) says *I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Matthew 15:24) and then heals her daughter, which means she is one of those lost sheep. Canaan was the ground of her exile, the merchant coast where the divorced northern house was swallowed up among the Gentiles (Hosea 8:8), not a verdict on her blood. She knew the Shepherd's voice and called him by David's name because she was a sheep before she ever heard him. The line Jubilees draws is kept; what the story shows is that the chosen seed had been scattered to places their brothers would never have looked.$n$,$n$For Ishmael and his sons and his brothers and Esau, Yahuah (God) did not cause to approach Him, and he chose them not because they are the children of Abraham, because He knew them, but He chose Yashar’el (Israel) to be His people.$n$,true),
(4591,'T',15,31,15,31,$n$*And He sanctified it, and gathered it from amongst all the children of men ; for there are many nations and many peoples, and all are His, and over all has He placed spirits in authority to lead them astray from Him.* (Jubilees 15:31). *But he answered and said, It is not meet to take the children’s bread, and to cast it to dogs.* (Matthew 15:26). Jubilees explains the difference between the children and the dogs. Many nations and many peoples are all His, but over them He set spirits in authority, and those spirits lead them astray; Yashar'el (Israel) He sanctified and gathered out from among all the children of men to rule Himself. Moses sang the same thing: *when he separated the sons of Adam, he set the bounds of the people according to the number of the children of Yashar'el (Israel). For the LORD’S portion is his people* (Deuteronomy 32:8–9). The woman on the coast of Canaan lives among the people under those spirits, and her daughter is vexed with a devil, but when she calls him *Son of David* and keeps asking, she shows she is not one of theirs. Yahusha (Jesus) says the bread is the children's, and then gives it to her, because she is a child of the household the Father rules directly, a lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) scattered into the territory of the spirits (Matthew 15:24, 28).$n$,$n$And He sanctified it, and gathered it from amongst all the children of men ; for there are many nations and many peoples, and all are His, and over all has He placed spirits in authority to lead them astray from Him.$n$,true),
(4592,'T',15,32,15,32,$n$*But over Yashar’el (Israel) He did not appoint any angel or spirit, for He alone is their ruler, and He will preserve them and require them at the hand of His angels and His spirits, and at the hand of all His powers in order that He may preserve them and bless them, and that they may be His and He may be theirs from henceforth for ever.* (Jubilees 15:32). *But he answered and said, It is not meet to take the children’s bread, and to cast it to dogs.* (Matthew 15:26). Over Yashar'el (Israel) no angel and no spirit: He alone is their ruler, and He keeps them Himself and requires them at the hand of every power. That is why Yahusha (Jesus) says he was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) (Matthew 15:24). The Shepherd who said through Ezekiel *I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out* (Ezekiel 34:11) had come to do it in person. The woman on the coast of Canaan is of that flock, a daughter of the divorced northern house living in the land of her scattering, and the Shepherd who was sent only to the lost sheep found one of them there. He says the bread is for the children, she answers as a child who knows whose table it is, and he gives it to her (Matthew 15:28). The line is kept; she was on the inside of it all along, and the hearing revealed it.$n$,$n$But over Yashar’el (Israel) He did not appoint any angel or spirit, for He alone is their ruler, and He will preserve them and require them at the hand of His angels and His spirits, and at the hand of all His powers in order that He may preserve them and bless them, and that they may be His and He may be theirs from henceforth for ever.$n$,true),
(4593,'T',16,17,16,17,$n$*And (that) all the seed of his sons should be nations, and be reckoned with the nations; but from the sons of Isaac one should become a holy seed, and should not be reckoned among the nations.* (Jubilees 16:17). *But he answered and said, It is not meet to take the children’s bread, and to cast it to dogs.* (Matthew 15:26). Abraham's other sons became nations and are reckoned with the nations; from Isaac comes one holy seed that is not reckoned among them. Paul says it the same way: *they are not all Yashar'el (Israel), which are of Yashar'el (Israel) ... In Isaac shall thy seed be called* (Romans 9:6–7). Children and dogs at the table is the same distinction. The seed of promise has a name and a line, and Yahusha (Jesus) does not pretend otherwise; he tells her the bread is the children's. But he was sent to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel) (Matthew 15:24), and the coast of Tyre and Sidon was where the divorced northern house had been swallowed up among the Gentiles (Hosea 8:8). When she answers as one who knows the Master's table and he gives her the bread, he is not crossing the line; he is finding a child of Isaac's holy seed on the far side of her exile.$n$,$n$And (that) all the seed of his sons should be nations, and be reckoned with the nations; but from the sons of Isaac one should become a holy seed, and should not be reckoned among the nations.$n$,true),
(4594,'T',50,1,50,1,$n$*And after this law I made known to you the days of the Sabbaths in the desert of Sinai, which is between Elim and Sinai.* (Jubilees 50:1). *And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the broken meat that was left seven baskets full.* (Matthew 15:37). The seven baskets are not a random count. Jubilees closes with the Sabbaths made known in the desert of Sinai, days and years and jubilees all built on seven. The first feeding left twelve baskets, a basket for every tribe (Matthew 14:20); the second leaves seven, the number of the Sabbath and the finished week. The King who fed the crowds counts the way Sinai counts, in twelves for the tribes and in sevens for rest. A people raised on that count would have noticed what was left over.$n$,$n$And after this law I made known to you the days of the Sabbaths in the desert of Sinai, which is between Elim and Sinai.$n$,true),
(4595,'T',50,2,50,2,$n$*And I told you of the Sabbaths of the land on Mount Sinai, and I told you of the jubilee years in the sabbaths of years: but the year thereof have I not told you till you enter the land which you are to possess.* (Jubilees 50:2). *And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the broken meat that was left seven baskets full.* (Matthew 15:37). Jubilees counts the land's rest in sevens: Sabbaths of years, and the jubilee at the end of seven sevens, as Moses laid it out, *seven sabbaths of years unto thee, seven times seven years* (Leviticus 25:8). The two feedings count in the two numbers of Yashar'el (Israel): twelve baskets for the twelve tribes (Matthew 14:20), seven baskets for the Sabbath and the jubilee. A crowd fed and filled with seven baskets over is a picture of the rest the land was promised: enough, more than enough, and the count of the week on top. The bread was real, and so was the number.$n$,$n$And I told you of the Sabbaths of the land on Mount Sinai, and I told you of the jubilee years in the sabbaths of years: but the year thereof have I not told you till you enter the land which you are to possess.$n$,true),
(4596,'T',50,4,50,4,$n$*Wherefore I have ordained for you the year-weeks and the years and the jubilees: there are forty-nine jubilees from the days of Adam until this day, and one week and two years and there are yet forty years to come (lit. "distant for learning the commandments of Yahuah (God), until they pass over into the land of Canaan, crossing the Jordan to the west.* (Jubilees 50:4). *And they did all eat, and were filled: and they took up of the broken meat that was left seven baskets full.* (Matthew 15:37). Jubilees counts forty-nine jubilees from Adam to the border of the land, seven sevens of jubilees, with the fiftieth opening as Yashar'el (Israel) crosses Jordan. Seven is the number this book counts the world's history in. When Yahusha (Jesus) feeds the crowd and seven baskets remain, the number lands on ears trained by that count. He had already stood in Nazareth and read *the acceptable year of Yahuah (Lord)* and told them *this day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears* (Luke 4:19, 21); the jubilee had a face. The seven baskets are the full measure left over when the jubilee is proclaimed.$n$,$n$Wherefore I have ordained for you the year-weeks and the years and the jubilees: there are forty-nine jubilees from the days of Adam until this day, and one week and two years and there are yet forty years to come (lit. "distant for learning the commandments of Yahuah (God), until they pass over into the land of Canaan, crossing the Jordan to the west.$n$,true),
(4699,'T',3,4,3,4,$n$*And Yahuah (God) said to us: “It is not good that the man should be alone: let us make a helpmeet for him.”* (Jubilees 3:4). *And he answered and said unto them, Have ye not read, that he which made them at the beginning made them male and female,* (Matthew 19:4). When the Pharisees ask about divorce, Yahusha (Jesus) does not go to Moses first. He goes behind Moses to the beginning, and Jubilees tells that beginning in the Father's own words to the angels: it is not good that the man should be alone, let us make a helpmeet for him. The one who made them male and female made them for each other, and that is the standard; what Moses allowed *because of the hardness of your hearts* (Matthew 19:8) sat under it, not over it. Both are Torah, and neither cancels the other. The King reads Torah from its first page and the hard hearts from the page where Moses met them.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) said to us: “It is not good that the man should be alone: let us make a helpmeet for him.”$n$,true),
(4700,'T',3,6,3,6,$n$*And He awaked Adam out of his sleep and on awaking he rose on the sixth day, and He brought her to him, and he knew her, and said to her: “This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she will be called my wife; because she was taken from her husband.”* (Jubilees 3:6). *And said, For this cause shall a man leave father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife: and they twain shall be one flesh?* (Matthew 19:5). Adam wakes, she is brought to him, and he says it: bone of my bones, flesh of my flesh, taken from her husband. That is why there is a *for this cause*. A man cleaves to his wife and the two are one flesh because she was one flesh with him before she was ever separate from him. Yahusha (Jesus) quotes Genesis to the Pharisees, and Jubilees carries the same scene in the same words. Marriage is a return to one body that was divided; what Elohim (God) joined on the sixth day no court was made to split.$n$,$n$And He awaked Adam out of his sleep and on awaking he rose on the sixth day, and He brought her to him, and he knew her, and said to her: “This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she will be called my wife; because she was taken from her husband.”$n$,true),
(4716,'T',1,15,1,15,$n$*And after this they will turn to Me from amongst the nations with all their heart and with all their soul and with all their strength, and I shall gather them from amongst all the nations, and they will seek Me, so that I shall be found of them, when they seek Me with all their heart and with all their soul.* (Jubilees 1:15). *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That ye which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28). Twelve thrones judging twelve tribes means twelve tribes will be there to be judged. Jubilees says how they get there: they turn to Him from among the nations with all their heart, and He gathers them from among all the nations. That is Ezekiel's two sticks made one, and Isaiah's *outcasts of Yashar'el (Israel)* and *dispersed of Yahudah (Judah)* gathered *from the four corners of the earth* (Isaiah 11:12). The regeneration Yahusha (Jesus) speaks of is a gathered people of Jacob's blood seated under Jacob's sons' names, not a church that has renamed itself Yashar'el (Israel). The scattering was real, the gathering is real, and the thrones are for real tribes.$n$,$n$And after this they will turn to Me from amongst the nations with all their heart and with all their soul and with all their strength, and I shall gather them from amongst all the nations, and they will seek Me, so that I shall be found of them, when they seek Me with all their heart and with all their soul.$n$,true),
(4717,'T',31,15,31,15,$n$*And they will be princes and judges, and chiefs of all the seed of the sons of Jacob; They will speak the word of Yahuah (God) in righteousness, And they will judge all His judgments in righteousness. And they will declare My ways to Jacob And My paths to Yashar’el (Israel). The blessing of Yahuah (God) will be given in their mouths To bless all the seed of the beloved.* (Jubilees 31:15). *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That ye which have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Adam shall sit in the throne of his glory, ye also shall sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel).* (Matthew 19:28). Isaac's blessing on Levi at Bethel is a blessing of judgeship: princes and judges and chiefs over all the seed of Jacob's sons, speaking the word of Yahuah (God) and declaring His ways to Jacob. Yahusha (Jesus) seats twelve men on twelve thrones over the same seed. Judging in Yashar'el (Israel) was never only a courtroom; *Yahuah (LORD) raised up judges, which delivered them out of the hand of those that spoiled them* (Judges 2:16). What Levi was blessed to carry at the altar, the twelve are given to carry at the throne, and the people under them are still the seed of the sons of Jacob.$n$,$n$And they will be princes and judges, and chiefs of all the seed of the sons of Jacob; They will speak the word of Yahuah (God) in righteousness, And they will judge all His judgments in righteousness. And they will declare My ways to Jacob And My paths to Yashar’el (Israel). The blessing of Yahuah (God) will be given in their mouths To bless all the seed of the beloved.$n$,true),
(4809,'T',5,2,5,2,$n$*And lawlessness increased on the earth and all flesh corrupted its way, alike men and cattle and beasts and birds and everything that walks on the earth-all of them corrupted their ways and their orders, and they began to devour each other, and lawlessness increased on the earth and every imagination of the thoughts of all men (was) thus evil continually.* (Jubilees 5:2). *But as the days of Noe were, so shall also the coming of the Son of Adam be.* (Matthew 24:37). Yahusha (Jesus) says his coming will look like Noah's days, and Jubilees says what Noah's days looked like: lawlessness increased on the earth, and all flesh corrupted its way, down to the cattle and the birds, every order out of its order. The word is lawlessness, Torah thrown off, and it is the same word the King uses for the end, *because iniquity shall abound, the love of many shall wax cold* (Matthew 24:12). The sign of the end is not a world that never heard the law. It is a world that had it and corrupted its way, and a religion that teaches its people the law is done away with is not a bystander to that sign.$n$,$n$And lawlessness increased on the earth and all flesh corrupted its way, alike men and cattle and beasts and birds and everything that walks on the earth-all of them corrupted their ways and their orders, and they began to devour each other, and lawlessness increased on the earth and every imagination of the thoughts of all men (was) thus evil continually.$n$,true),
(4810,'T',5,19,5,19,$n$*And as for all those who corrupted their ways and their thoughts before the flood, no man’s person was accepted save that of Noah alone; for his person was accepted in behalf of his sons, whom Elohim (God) saved from the waters of the flood on his account; for his heart was righteous in all his ways, according as it was commanded regarding him, and he had not departed from aught that was ordained for him.* (Jubilees 5:19). *For as in the days that were before the flood they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day that Noe entered into the ark,* (Matthew 24:38). Before the flood no one's person was accepted but Noah's, and his sons were saved on his account. Everyone else was eating, drinking, marrying, ordinary life, *and knew not until the flood came, and took them all away* (Matthew 24:39). The ones the flood took are the ones taken; Noah was the one left. So when Yahusha (Jesus) goes on to say one shall be taken and the other left, the pattern he has just set up is the flood, and when the disciples ask where the taken go, the answer is *wheresoever the body is, thither will the eagles be gathered together* (Luke 17:37). Being taken is not the rescue. The accepted one is the one still standing when the water goes down.$n$,$n$And as for all those who corrupted their ways and their thoughts before the flood, no man’s person was accepted save that of Noah alone; for his person was accepted in behalf of his sons, whom Elohim (God) saved from the waters of the flood on his account; for his heart was righteous in all his ways, according as it was commanded regarding him, and he had not departed from aught that was ordained for him.$n$,true),
(4905,'T',1,15,1,15,$n$*And after this they will turn to Me from amongst the nations with all their heart and with all their soul and with all their strength, and I shall gather them from amongst all the nations, and they will seek Me, so that I shall be found of them, when they seek Me with all their heart and with all their soul.* (Jubilees 1:15). *Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit):* (Matthew 28:19). The Father says He will gather them from among all the nations when they turn with all their heart and all their soul and all their strength, the words of the Shema (Deuteronomy 6:5) and of Jeremiah's promise, *ye shall seek me, and find me, when ye shall search for me with all your heart* (Jeremiah 29:13). Then the Son sends his disciples to all nations. The two fit together. The sheep are scattered through every nation, so the shepherds are sent through every nation; *teach all nations* is not a change of subject from the gathering of Yashar'el (Israel), it is how the gathering is carried out, because that is where the lost sheep are. The door is wide because the scattering was wide.$n$,$n$And after this they will turn to Me from amongst the nations with all their heart and with all their soul and with all their strength, and I shall gather them from amongst all the nations, and they will seek Me, so that I shall be found of them, when they seek Me with all their heart and with all their soul.$n$,true),
(4906,'T',1,23,1,23,$n$*And after this they will turn to Me in all uprightness and with all (their) heart and with all (their) soul, and I shall circumcise the foreskin of their heart and the foreskin of the heart of their seed, and I shall create in them a holy spirit, and I shall cleanse them so that they shall not turn away from Me from that day to eternity.* (Jubilees 1:23). *Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world. Amen.* (Matthew 28:20). The last words of Matthew are a command to teach commandments. Jubilees tells how a gathered people will be able to keep them: a circumcised heart, a holy spirit created in them, a cleansing that holds. Jeremiah calls it *I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts* (Jeremiah 31:33); Ezekiel calls it *I will put my spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes* (Ezekiel 36:27). The commission is that same work of the heart going out: baptize them, then teach them to observe everything he commanded. He opened his teaching by saying *one jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law* (Matthew 5:18) and closed it by sending men to teach the keeping of it. Grace is not the end of the commandments; it is the heart that can finally keep them.$n$,$n$And after this they will turn to Me in all uprightness and with all (their) heart and with all (their) soul, and I shall circumcise the foreskin of their heart and the foreskin of the heart of their seed, and I shall create in them a holy spirit, and I shall cleanse them so that they shall not turn away from Me from that day to eternity.$n$,true),
(4907,'T',1,25,1,25,$n$*And they will all be called children of the living Elohim (God), and every angel and every spirit will know, yea, they will know that these are My children, and that I am their Father in uprightness and righteousness, and that I love them.* (Jubilees 1:25). *Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit):* (Matthew 28:19). *Children of the living Elohim (God)* is the name Hosea gave to the house that had been called Not My People: *in the place where it was said unto them, Ye are not my people, there it shall be said unto them, Ye are the sons of the living Elohim (God)* (Hosea 1:10), and Paul quotes it for the same gathering (Romans 9:26). Jubilees has the Father using the name for the ones He gathers and cleanses, and making every angel and spirit know it. The commission goes to all nations because that is the place where it was said to them *not my people*; that is where they were sown. The disciples are sent to find the Father's children where they were scattered, so that what heaven already knows about them becomes their own.$n$,$n$And they will all be called children of the living Elohim (God), and every angel and every spirit will know, yea, they will know that these are My children, and that I am their Father in uprightness and righteousness, and that I love them.$n$,true),
(7853,'T',10,8,10,8,$n$*And forthwith Yahusha (Jesus) gave them leave.* That a remnant of these spirits operates on the earth at all is the Hebrew library's own framework: *And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said... let some of them remain before me... And He said: "Let the tenth part of them remain before him, and let nine parts descend into the place of condemnation."* (Jubilees 10:8–9) The legion is part of the tenth-part left to lead astray; the King encountering and casting them out is the partially-restrained-spirits framework walked into a single deliverance.$n$,$n$And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say to them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men; for these are for corruption and leading astray before my judgment, for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.”$n$,false),
(7916,'T',10,11,10,11,$n$*And we did according to all His words: all the malignant evil ones we bound in the place of condemnation, and a tenth part of them we left that they might be subject before Satan on the earth.* (Jubilees 10:11). *But when a stronger than he shall come upon him, and overcome him, he taketh from him all his armour wherein he trusted, and divideth his spoils.* (Luke 11:22). Nine parts were bound at Noah's prayer and a tenth left loose on the earth under Satan. Satan is the strong man armed, keeping his palace with that tenth. Yahusha (Jesus) had just cast a devil out of a dumb man (Luke 11:14), and he tells them what it means: a stronger one has come upon him, overcome him, taken the armour he trusted in, and is dividing the spoils. The binding that began in Noah's day is being finished by the one stronger than Satan; every devil cast out is spoil carried from a house whose master is already beaten. The last of the binding waits for the thousand years (Revelation 20:2).$n$,$n$And we did according to all His words: all the malignant evil ones we bound in the place of condemnation, and a tenth part of them we left that they might be subject before Satan on the earth.$n$,true),
(7942,'T',5,2,5,2,$n$*And lawlessness increased on the earth and all flesh corrupted its way, alike men and cattle and beasts and birds and everything that walks on the earth-all of them corrupted their ways and their orders, and they began to devour each other, and lawlessness increased on the earth and every imagination of the thoughts of all men (was) thus evil continually.* (Jubilees 5:2). *They did eat, they drank, they married wives, they were given in marriage, until the day that Noe entered into the ark, and the flood came, and destroyed them all.* (Luke 17:27). Luke gives the surface of Noah's days: eating, drinking, marrying, until the ark door closed. Jubilees gives what was under the surface: lawlessness increasing, all flesh corrupting its way, every imagination of the thoughts of all men evil continually. The ordinary life Yahusha (Jesus) describes was not innocent. It was carrying on as usual on top of that corruption, deaf to the hammer on the ark. That is the warning for the day of the Son of Adam: not that people will be eating and marrying, but that they will be doing it with the law thrown off and the judgment already appointed, and never look up.$n$,$n$And lawlessness increased on the earth and all flesh corrupted its way, alike men and cattle and beasts and birds and everything that walks on the earth-all of them corrupted their ways and their orders, and they began to devour each other, and lawlessness increased on the earth and every imagination of the thoughts of all men (was) thus evil continually.$n$,true),
(7991,'T',1,20,1,20,$n$*Let Your mercy, O Yahuah (God), be lifted up upon Your people, and create in them an upright spirit, and let not the spirit of Beliar rule over them ... create in them a clean heart and a holy spirit, and let them not be ensnared in their sins from henceforth until eternity.* (Jubilees 1:20–21) Mosheh (Moses) prays for the very thing Yahusha (Jesus) tells Nicodemus is required to *see the kingdom of Elohim (God)* — a clean heart and a holy spirit created in the people from above. The new birth of John 3:3 is not a new religion but the clean-heart-and-holy-spirit creation the Hebrew library names as the work of mercy lifted upon the covenant people, not a credential the flesh can manufacture.$n$,$n$Let Your mercy, O Yahuah (God), be lifted up upon Your people, and create in them an upright spirit, and let not the spirit of Beliar rule over them to accuse them before You, and to ensnare them from all the paths of righteousness, so that they may perish from before Your face.$n$,false),
(9880,'T',6,18,6,18,$n$*For this reason it is ordained and written on the heavenly tables, that they should celebrate the feast of weeks in this month once a year, to renew the covenant every year. And this whole festival was celebrated in heaven from the day of creation till the days of Noah-twenty-six jubilees and five weeks of years: and Noah and his sons observed it for seven jubilees and one week of years, till the day of Noah’s death, and from the day of Noah’s death his sons did away with (it) until the days of Abraham, and they ate blood.* (Jubilees 6:17–18). *When the day of Pentecost was fully come* (Acts 2:1) — the day was the feast of weeks, Shavuot. The Hebrew library names what the feast is for: to renew the covenant every year. The Spirit falls on the covenant-renewal feast, not on a day that abolishes the covenant.$n$,$n$And this whole festival was celebrated in heaven from the day of creation till the days of Noah-twenty-six jubilees and five weeks of years: and Noah and his sons observed it for seven jubilees and one week of years, till the day of Noah’s death, and from the day of Noah’s death his sons did away with (it) until the days of Abraham, and they ate blood.$n$,false),
(12765,'T',16,13,16,13,$n$*And in the middle of the sixth month Yahuah (God) visited Sarah and did to her as He had spoken, and she conceived. And she bare a son in the third month, and in the middle of the month, at the time of which Yahuah (God) had spoken to Abraham, on the festival of the first-fruits of the harvest, Isaac was born.* (Jubilees 16:12–13). *But he of the freewoman was by promise.* (Galatians 4:23). The Hebrew library marks the promise-child born to the appointed calendar of Yahuah (God): Isaac came *at the time of which Yahuah (God) had spoken... on the festival of the first-fruits of the harvest.* The son of promise is born on a feast of the Most High — the very appointed times the no-gods' calendar (Galatians 4:10) counterfeits. Promise keeps the Father's set time.$n$,$n$And she bare a son in the third month, and in the middle of the month, at the time of which Yahuah (God) had spoken to Abraham, on the festival of the first-fruits of the harvest, Isaac was born.$n$,false),
(16046,'T',7,21,7,21,$n$Jubilees 7:21–22 — *For owing to these three things came the flood upon the earth, namely, owing to the fornication wherein the Watchers against the law of their ordinances went a whoring after the daughters of men, and took themselves wives of all which they chose: and they made the beginning of uncleanness. And they begat sons the Nâphîdîm, and they were all unlike, and they devoured one another: and the Giants slew the Nâphîl, and the Nâphîl slew the Eljô, and the Eljô mankind, and one man another.* Jubilees names the giant brood born of the same union 1 Enoch 7:2 records, and their mutual slaughter.$n$,$n$For owing to these three things came the flood upon the earth, namely, owing to the fornication wherein the Watchers against the law of their ordinances went a whoring after the daughters of men, and took themselves wives of all which they chose: and they made the beginning of uncleanness.$n$,false),
(16266,'T',5,11,5,12,$n$*And He made for all His works a new and righteous nature, so that they should not sin in their whole nature for ever, but should be all righteous each in his kind alway.* (Jubilees 5:12). *Destroy all wrong from the face of the earth and let every evil work come to an end: and let the plant of righteousness and truth appear: and it shall prove a blessing; the works of righteousness and truth shall be planted in truth and joy for evermore.* (1 Enoch 10:16). After the flood Yahuah (God) did not only wash the earth; He made for all His works a new and righteous nature, so that each kind should be righteous in its kind. Enoch heard the same thing commanded before it was done: destroy all wrong, let every evil work end, and let the plant of righteousness and truth appear. The plant Enoch was shown is the nature Jubilees says was made. Judgment in both books is not the end of the story but the clearing of the ground for what is planted in truth and joy for evermore.$n$,$n$And He made for all His works a new and righteous nature, so that they should not sin in their whole nature for ever, but should be all righteous each in his kind alway.$n$,true),
(16402,'T',3,6,3,6,$n$*"This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she will be called my wife; because she was taken from her husband." Therefore shall man and wife be one, and therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and cleave to his wife, and they shall be one flesh* (Jubilees 3:6–7). The restored witness retells Genesis 2:22-24 — the woman *brought... unto the man* and named bone of his bone — sealing the one-flesh ordinance from the first marriage.$n$,$n$And He awaked Adam out of his sleep and on awaking he rose on the sixth day, and He brought her to him, and he knew her, and said to her: “This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she will be called my wife; because she was taken from her husband.”$n$,false),
(16776,'T',4,25,4,26,$n$*For Yahuah (God) has four places on the earth, the Garden of Eden, and the Mount of the East, and this mountain on which you are this day, Mount Sinai, and Mount Zion (which) will be sanctified in the new creation for a sanctification of the earth; through it will the earth be sanctified from all (its) guilt and its uncleanness throughout the generations of the world.* (Jubilees 4:26). *And he answered saying: ‘This high mountain which thou hast seen, whose summit is like the throne of Elohim (God), is His throne, where the Holy Great One, Yahuah (Lord) of Glory, the Eternal King, will sit, when He shall come down to visit the earth with goodness.* (1 Enoch 25:3). Jubilees counts four places of Yahuah (God) on the earth and says the last of them, Mount Zion, will be sanctified in the new creation and sanctify the earth through it. Enoch was shown a high mountain whose summit is like the throne of Elohim (God), where the Eternal King will sit when He comes down to visit the earth with goodness. The two books are looking at the same thing from two sides, a mountain on this earth that becomes the seat of His coming. Jubilees names it among the holy places; Enoch sees Him sitting on it. The sanctifying of the earth and the visiting of the earth with goodness are one descent.$n$,$n$For Yahuah (God) has four places on the earth, the Garden of Eden, and the Mount of the East, and this mountain on which you are this day, Mount Sinai, and Mount Zion (which) will be sanctified in the new creation for a sanctification of the earth; through it will the earth be sanctified from all (its) guilt and its uncleanness throughout the generations of the world.$n$,true),
(17218,'T',3,21,3,21,$n$Jubilees 3:21–22 — *And when she had first covered her shame with fig-leaves, she gave thereof to Adam and he ate, and his eyes were opened, and he saw that he was naked. And he took fig-leaves and sewed (them) together, and made an apron for himself, and covered his shame.* Jubilees carries the same eyes-opened, naked, fig-leaf account Enoch points back to at the tree of wisdom.$n$,$n$And when she had first covered her shame with fig-leaves, she gave thereof to Adam and he ate, and his eyes were opened, and he saw that he was naked.$n$,false),
(17358,'T',4,4,4,4,$n$*And Yahuah (God) reproved Cain because of Abel, because he had slain him, and he made him a fugitive on the earth because of the blood of his brother, and he cursed him upon the earth. And on this account it is written on the heavenly tables, "Cursed is he who smites his neighbour treacherously"* (Jubilees 4:4–5). The sentence *a fugitive and a vagabond shalt thou be in the earth* (Genesis 4:12) is grounded in the restored book on the heavenly tables — the curse of the treacherous murderer is not arbitrary anger but written law, *he made him a fugitive ... because of the blood of his brother*.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) reproved Cain because of Abel, because he had slain him, and he made him a fugitive on the earth because of the blood of his brother, and he cursed him upon the earth.$n$,false),
(17376,'T',4,17,4,17,$n$*And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book, that men might know the seasons of the years according to the order of their separate months... and set in order the months and recounted the Sabbaths of the years* (Jubilees 4:17–18). The walk of *Enoch... with Elohim (God)* (Genesis 5:22) included the keeping of the appointed times: he set in order the months and the Sabbaths so men might know the seasons of Yahuah's calendar.$n$,$n$And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book, that men might know the seasons of the years according to the order of their separate months.$n$,false),
(17397,'T',5,7,5,7,$n$*And against the angels whom He had sent upon the earth, He was exceedingly wroth, and He gave commandment to root them out of all their dominion, and He bade us to bind them in the depths of the earth, and behold they are bound in the midst of them, and are (kept) separate. And against their sons went forth a command from before His face that they should be smitten with the sword, and be removed from under heaven* (Jubilees 5:6–7). The fate of the *sons of Elohim (God)* of Genesis 6:4 is sealed: the rebel angels *bound in the depths of the earth* and their giant *sons... smitten with the sword* — the same binding Jude 6 and 2 Peter 2:4 confirm, the seed-corruption purged from the earth.$n$,$n$And against their sons went forth a command from before His face that they should be smitten with the sword, and be removed from under heaven.$n$,false),
(18199,'T',5,24,5,24,$n$*And Yahuah (God) opened seven flood-gates of heaven, And the mouths of the fountains of the great deep, seven mouths in number. And the flood-gates began to pour down water from the heaven forty days and forty nights, And the fountains of the deep also sent up waters, until the whole world was full of water* (Jubilees 5:24–25). The Torah's *all the fountains of the great deep broken up, and the windows of heaven were opened* (Genesis 7:11) is here counted out — *seven flood-gates of heaven... seven mouths* of the deep — the waters above and the waters below, divided in Genesis 1, let back in at once: the un-creation, the world un-made by the same waters it was framed from.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) opened seven flood-gates of heaven, And the mouths of the fountains of the great deep, seven mouths in number.$n$,false),
(18251,'T',7,11,7,11,$n$*And he blessed Shem, and said: "Blessed be Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) of Shem, and Canaan shall be his servant. Elohim (God) shall enlarge Japheth, and Elohim (God) shall dwell in the dwelling of Shem, and Canaan shall be his servant"* (Jubilees 7:11–12). The blessing of Genesis 9:26-27 runs through Shem — *Blessed be Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) of Shem... Elohim (God) shall enlarge Japheth, and he shall dwell in the tents of Shem*. The covenant-line is Shem's, the seed that carries to Abraham; Japheth's enlargement is the nations coming to dwell in Shem's tents — a foreshadow of the gathering, not a hierarchy of races.$n$,$n$And he blessed Shem, and said: "Blessed be Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) of Shem, and Canaan shall be his servant.$n$,false),
(18282,'T',12,2,12,2,$n$*"What help and profit have we from those idols which you do worship... For there is no spirit in them, For they are dumb forms, and a misleading of the heart. Worship them not: Worship the Elohim (God) of heaven, Who causes the rain and the dew to descend on the earth..."* (Jubilees 12:2–4). The Ur that Abram is called out of (Genesis 11:28,31) was a house of idols, and the restored witness puts Abram's confession in his mouth: the idols are dumb forms; worship instead the Elohim of heaven who made all things.$n$,$n$And he said, "What help and profit have we from those idols which you do worship, And before which you do bow yourself?$n$,false),
(20236,'T',12,22,12,22,$n$*"Get you up from your country, and from your kindred and from the house of your father to a land which I shall show you, and I shall make you a great and numerous nation. And I shall bless you And I shall make your name great, And you will be blessed in the earth, And in you will all families of the earth be blessed..."* (Jubilees 12:22–23). The restored witness delivers the very call of Genesis 12:1-3 — *Get thee out of thy country... and in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed* — word for word, set after Abram has already turned from his fathers' idols, so the call falls on a man already given wholly to Yahuah.$n$,$n$And he made an end of speaking and praying, and behold the word of Yahuah (God) was sent to him through me, saying: "Get you up from your country, and from your kindred and from the house of your father to a land which I shall show you, and I shall make you a great and numerous nation.$n$,false),
(20247,'T',13,8,13,8,$n$*And he blessed Yahuah (God) who had led him out of Ur of the Chaldees, and had brought him to this land... he built an altar on this mountain, and called on the name of Yahuah (God): "You, the eternal Elohim (God), are my Elohim (God)."* (Jubilees 13:7–8). The altar of Genesis 12:7-8 — *there builded he an altar unto Yahuah (LORD)... and called upon the name of Yahuah (LORD)* — is given voice in the restored witness: the worship is a confession, *You, the eternal Elohim, are my Elohim*, the covenant-man planting the Name in the land.$n$,$n$And it came to pass in the first year, in the seventh week, on the new moon of the first month, that he built an altar on this mountain, and called on the name of Yahuah (God): “You, the eternal Elohim (God), are my Elohim (God).”$n$,false),
(20284,'T',13,8,13,15,$n$*And Pharaoh gave back Sarai, the wife of Abram, and he sent him out of the land of Egypt, and he journeyed to the place where he had pitched his tent at the beginning, to the place of the altar, with Ai on the east, and Bethel on the west, and he blessed Yahuah (God) his Elohim (God) who had brought him back in peace. And it came to pass in the forty-first jubilee, in the third year of the first week, that he returned to this place and offered thereon a burnt sacrifice, and called on the name of Yahuah (God), and said: “You, the most high Elohim (God), are my Elohim (God) for ever and ever.”* (Jubilees 13:15–16). *Unto the place of the altar, which he had made there at the first: and there Abram called on the name of Yahuah (LORD).* (Genesis 13:4). Genesis gives the return in one line: back to the first altar, and there Abram called on the name of Yahuah (LORD). Jubilees fills in what the calling said. He blessed the one who had brought him back in peace, and then he called on the name and spoke to it: You, the most high Elohim (God), are my Elohim (God) for ever and ever. Calling on the name was never a bare invocation. It was a man coming back to the place where he had first built an altar and saying out loud, with the land and the altar as witnesses, whose he was and for how long.$n$,$n$And Pharaoh gave back Sarai, the wife of Abram, and he sent him out of the land of Egypt, and he journeyed to the place where he had pitched his tent at the beginning, to the place of the altar, with Ai on the east, and Bethel on the west, and he blessed Yahuah (God) his Elohim (God) who had brought him back in peace.$n$,true),
(20326,'T',14,6,14,6,$n$*And he believed in Yahuah (God), and it was counted to him for righteousness. And He said to him: “I am Yahuah (God) that brought you out of Ur of the Chaldees, to give you the land of the Canaanites to possess it for ever; and I shall be Elohim (God) to you and to your seed after you.”* (Jubilees 14:6–7). *And he believed in Yahuah (LORD); and he counted it to him for righteousness.* (Genesis 15:6). Jubilees carries Genesis 15:6 almost word for word, and then does not let the reader walk away from it. In the next breath the one who counted the belief for righteousness says who He is: the one who brought Abram out of Ur, who gives the land to his seed, who will be Elohim (God) to him and to his seed after him. The reckoned righteousness is not a doorway out of the covenant; it is spoken inside it, with the land and the seed and the Elohim (God) of the seed all named in the same speech. Faith was counted and the covenant was declared, and neither was set against the other.$n$,$n$And he believed in Yahuah (God), and it was counted to him for righteousness.$n$,true),
(20330,'T',14,17,14,17,$n$*And you will go to your fathers in peace, and be buried in a good old age. But in the fourth generation they will return hither; for the iniquity of the Amorites is not yet full." And he awoke from his sleep, and he arose, and the sun had set; and there was a flame, and behold! a furnace was smoking, and a flame of fire passed between the pieces* (Jubilees 14:15–17). The restored witness keeps the heart of the sign: *a furnace was smoking, and a flame of fire passed between the pieces* — the smoking furnace and burning lamp of *passed between those pieces* (Genesis 15:17), Yahuah alone moving through the divided sacrifice.$n$,$n$And he awoke from his sleep, and he arose, and the sun had set; and there was a flame, and behold! a furnace was smoking, and a flame of fire passed between the pieces.$n$,false),
(20331,'T',14,11,14,11,$n$*And he built there an altar, and sacrificed all these; and he poured their blood upon the altar, and divided them in the midst, and laid them over against each other; but the birds divided he not. And birds came down upon the pieces, and Abram drove them away* (Jubilees 14:11–12). The restored witness gathers the cutting of the covenant-beasts — *divided them in the midst... but the birds divided he not* — matching *divided them in the midst, and laid each piece one against another: but the birds divided he not* (Genesis 15:10), and Abram driving off the fowls that came down (Genesis 15:11).$n$,$n$And he built there an altar, and sacrificed all these; and he poured their blood upon the altar, and divided them in the midst, and laid them over against each other; but the birds divided he not.$n$,false),
(20338,'T',14,14,14,14,$n$*And the nation also to whom they will be in bondage shall I judge, and after that they will come forth thence with much substance.* (Jubilees 14:14). *And also that nation, whom they shall serve, will I judge: and afterward shall they come out with great substance.* (Genesis 15:14). Jubilees carries the second half of the oath to Abram entire, not only the four hundred years of affliction but the judgment on the nation that afflicts and the coming out afterward with much in hand. Yahuah (God) told Abram the end before the bondage began; the exodus was promised in the same breath as the servitude. When Yashar'el (Israel) walked out of Egypt with the silver and gold of their neighbours, they were collecting on a word spoken to their father four centuries earlier.$n$,$n$And the nation also to whom they will be in bondage shall I judge, and after that they will come forth thence with much substance.$n$,true),
(21302,'T',5,11,5,11,$n$Jubilees 5:11–12 — *And He destroyed all from their places, and there was not left one of them whom He judged not according to all their wickedness. And He made for all His works a new and righteous nature, so that they should not sin in their whole nature for ever, but should be all righteous each in his kind alway.* The earth cleansed and made righteous in Enoch 10:20 is Jubilees' new and righteous nature after the Flood.$n$,$n$And He destroyed all from their places, and there was not left one of them whom He judged not according to all their wickedness.$n$,false),
(21357,'T',7,21,7,21,$n$Jubilees 7:21–22 — *For owing to these three things came the flood upon the earth, namely, owing to the fornication wherein the Watchers against the law of their ordinances went a whoring after the daughters of men, and took themselves wives of all which they chose: and they made the beginning of uncleanness. And they begat sons the Nâphîdîm, and they were all unlike, and they devoured one another: and the Giants slew the Nâphîl, and the Nâphîl slew the Eljô, and the Eljô mankind, and one man another.* Jubilees keeps the same Watcher-account — rebellion against the law of their ordinances — that Enoch lays out by name.$n$,$n$For owing to these three things came the flood upon the earth, namely, owing to the fornication wherein the Watchers against the law of their ordinances went a whoring after the daughters of men, and took themselves wives of all which they chose: and they made the beginning of uncleanness.$n$,false),
(22530,'T',6,32,6,32,$n$Jubilees 6:32–33 — *And command you the children of Yashar'el (Israel) that they observe the years according to this reckoning-three hundred and sixty-four days, and (these) will constitute a complete year, and they will not disturb its time from its days and from its feasts; for everything will fall out in them according to their testimony, and they will not leave out any day nor disturb any feasts. But if they do neglect and do not observe them according to His commandment, then they will disturb all their seasons, and the years will be dislodged from this (order), and they will disturb the seasons and the years will be dislodged and they will neglect their ordinances.* Jubilees commands the 364-day solar reckoning whose neglect disturbs every feast — the human counterpart to Enoch's stars that abandoned their appointed times.$n$,$n$And command you the children of Yashar’el (Israel) that they observe the years according to this reckoning-three hundred and sixty-four days, and (these) will constitute a complete year, and they will not disturb its time from its days and from its feasts; for everything will fall out in them according to their testimony, and they will not leave out any day nor disturb any feasts.$n$,false),
(22582,'T',6,37,6,37,$n$Jubilees 6:37–38 — *For this reason the years will come upon them when they will disturb (the order), and make an abominable (day) the day of testimony, and an unclean day a feast day, and they will confound all the days, the holy with the unclean, and the unclean day with the holy; for they will go wrong as to the months and sabbaths and feasts and jubilees. For this reason I command and testify to you that you may testify to them; for after your death your children will disturb (them), so that they will not make the year three hundred and sixty-four days only, and for this reason they will go wrong as to the new moons and seasons and sabbaths and festivals, and they will eat all kinds of blood with all kinds of flesh.* Jubilees names what Enoch foresaw — the chiefs of the stars transgressing their order and not appearing at the seasons prescribed (80:6) — and adds the result among men: going wrong as to new moons, seasons, sabbaths and festivals, and eating blood with all kinds of flesh.$n$,$n$For this reason the years will come upon them when they will disturb (the order), and make an abominable (day) the day of testimony, and an unclean day a feast day, and they will confound all the days, the holy with the unclean, and the unclean day with the holy; for they will go wrong as to the months and sabbaths and feasts and jubilees.$n$,false),
(22602,'T',6,38,6,38,$n$Jubilees 6:37–38 — *...they will go wrong as to the months and sabbaths and feasts and jubilees. For this reason I command and testify to you that you may testify to them; for after your death your children will disturb (them), so that they will not make the year three hundred and sixty-four days only, and for this reason they will go wrong as to the new moons and seasons and sabbaths and festivals, and they will eat all kinds of blood with all kinds of flesh.* Jubilees and Enoch describe the same coming apostasy from the reckoning: men at fault, not reckoning the days in the whole reckoning of the year (82:5) — Jubilees adds the going wrong as to new moons, sabbaths and feasts, down to eating blood with flesh.$n$,$n$For this reason I command and testify to you that you may testify to them; for after your death your children will disturb (them), so that they will not make the year three hundred and sixty-four days only, and for this reason they will go wrong as to the new moons and seasons and sabbaths and festivals, and they will eat all kinds of blood with all kinds of flesh.$n$,false),
(23895,'T',5,23,5,24,$n$*And Yahuah (God) opened seven flood-gates of heaven, And the mouths of the fountains of the great deep, seven mouths in number.* (Jubilees 5:24). *And I saw again, and behold fountains were opened on the surface of that great enclosure, and that water began to swell and rise upon the surface, and I saw that enclosure till all its surface was covered with water.* (1 Enoch 89:3). Jubilees counts the openings: seven flood-gates of heaven and seven mouths of the fountains of the great deep. Enoch saw the fountains from above, opened on the surface of the great enclosure, the water swelling and rising until the whole surface was covered. One book gives the number and the other gives the sight, and they are describing the same morning. The deep did not leak; it was opened, by mouths, on purpose, and both witnesses say so.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) opened seven flood-gates of heaven, And the mouths of the fountains of the great deep, seven mouths in number.$n$,true),
(25564,'S',1,25,1,26,$n$*And do you write down for yourself all these words which I declare to you on this mountain, the first and the last, which shall come to pass in all the divisions of the days in the law and in the testimony and in the weeks and the jubilees to eternity, until I descend and dwell with them throughout eternity."* (Jubilees 1:26). *Thine eyes did see my substance, yet being unperfect; and in thy book all my members were written, which in continuance were fashioned, when as yet there was none of them.* (Psalm 139:16). Moses is told to write down the first and the last, everything that will come to pass in all the divisions of the days, before any of those days have arrived. David says the same thing about his own body: every member written in the book of Yahuah (God) while there was not yet one of them. In both the writing comes first and the days come after. The history of Yashar'el (Israel) laid out on Sinai in weeks and jubilees is not a forecast; it is a reading from a book already written, the same book David saw his unformed self in.$n$,$n$And do you write down for yourself all these words which I declare to you on this mountain, the first and the last, which shall come to pass in all the divisions of the days in the law and in the testimony and in the weeks and the jubilees to eternity, until I descend and dwell with them throughout eternity."$n$,true),
(25565,'S',1,25,1,26,$n$Malachi 3:16 — *Then they that feared Yahuah (LORD) spake often one to another: and Yahuah (LORD) hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared Yahuah (LORD), and that thought upon his name.* Jubilees 1:26's charge to write the first and the last echoes the book of remembrance written before Yahuah.$n$,$n$And do you write down for yourself all these words which I declare to you on this mountain, the first and the last, which shall come to pass in all the divisions of the days in the law and in the testimony and in the weeks and the jubilees to eternity, until I descend and dwell with them throughout eternity."$n$,false),
(25591,'S',2,16,2,17,$n$Genesis 2:3 — *And Elohim (God) blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it: because that in it he had rested from all his work which Elohim (God) created and made.* Jubilees 2:17 gives this same blessed, sanctified seventh day as the great sign over creation.$n$,$n$And He gave us a great sign, the Sabbath day, that we should work six days, but keep Sabbath on the seventh day from all work.$n$,false),
(25610,'S',3,6,3,7,$n$Matthew 19:5 — *And said, For this cause shall a man leave father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife: and they twain shall be one flesh?* Yahusha appeals to the same creation ordinance Jubilees 3:7 records as given from the second week — "man and wife be one."$n$,$n$Therefore shall man and wife be one, and therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and cleave to his wife, and they shall be one flesh.$n$,false),
(25616,'S',3,11,3,12,$n$Genesis 2:8 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) planted a garden eastward in Eden; and there he put the man whom he had formed.* The garden Jubilees 3:12 calls "holier than all the earth besides" is this planted Eden of Genesis.$n$,$n$And when she had completed these eighty days we brought her into the Garden of Eden, for it is holier than all the earth besides, and every tree that is planted in it is holy.$n$,false),
(25617,'S',3,16,3,17,$n$Genesis 3:1 — *Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath Elohim (God) said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?* Jubilees 3:17 quotes the serpent's opening question almost word for word.$n$,$n$And after the completion of the seven years, which he had completed there, seven years exactly, and in the second month, on the seventeenth day (of the month), the serpent came and approached the woman, and the serpent said to the woman, “Has Elohim (God) commanded you, saying, You shall not eat of every tree of the garden?”$n$,false),
(25626,'S',3,30,3,31,$n$Genesis 3:21 — *Unto Adam also and to his wife did Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) make coats of skins, and clothed them.* The covering Jubilees 3:31 prescribes "on the heavenly tables" — that they not uncover themselves as the nations do — flows from this first God-given covering.$n$,$n$On this account, it is prescribed on the heavenly tables as touching all those who know the judgment of the law, that they should cover their shame, and should not uncover themselves as the nations uncover themselves.$n$,false),
(25631,'S',4,17,4,18,$n$Jude 1:14 — *And Enoch also, the seventh from Adam, prophesied of these, saying, Behold, Yahuah (Lord) cometh with ten thousands of his saints.* The canon itself names Enoch the testifying prophet Jubilees 4:18 calls the first to write a testimony.$n$,$n$And he was the first to write a testimony, and he testified to the sons of men among the generations of the earth, and recounted the weeks of the jubilees, and made known to them the days of the years, and set in order the months and recounted the Sabbaths of the years as we made (them) known to him.$n$,false),
(25637,'S',4,4,4,5,$n$Genesis 4:15 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto him, Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold. And Yahuah (LORD) set a mark upon Cain, lest any finding him should kill him.* The Genesis judgment on Cain matches the heavenly-tablet curse of Jubilees 4:5.$n$,$n$And on this account it is written on the heavenly tables, “Cursed is he who smites his neighbour treacherously, and let all who have seen and heard say, So be it; and the man who has seen and not declared (it), let him be accursed as the other.” .$n$,false),
(25648,'S',5,2,5,2,$n$*And lawlessness increased on the earth and all flesh corrupted its way, alike men and cattle and beasts and birds and everything that walks on the earth-all of them corrupted their ways and their orders, and they began to devour each other, and lawlessness increased on the earth and every imagination of the thoughts of all men (was) thus evil continually.* (Jubilees 5:2). *Who consumed all the acquisitions of men.* (1 Enoch 7:3). Jubilees says all flesh corrupted its way and they began to devour each other, and leaves the devouring without a face. Enoch gives it one. The giants born to the angels' wives consumed all the acquisitions of men, everything men had laboured for, until there was nothing left to give them. The lawlessness that filled the earth before the flood was not only men sinning against men; it was the offspring of the fallen eating the world out from under the sons of Adam. Read together, the two books show that the flood came against a devouring that had a lineage, not merely a mood.$n$,$n$And lawlessness increased on the earth and all flesh corrupted its way, alike men and cattle and beasts and birds and everything that walks on the earth-all of them corrupted their ways and their orders, and they began to devour each other, and lawlessness increased on the earth and every imagination of the thoughts of all men (was) thus evil continually.$n$,true),
(25658,'S',5,13,5,13,$n$*And the judgment of all is ordained and written on the heavenly tables in righteousness–even (the judgment of) all who depart from the path which is ordained for them to walk in; and if they walk not therein judgment is written down for every creature and for every kind.* (Jubilees 5:13). *Thine eyes did see my substance, yet being unperfect; and in thy book all my members were written, which in continuance were fashioned, when as yet there was none of them.* (Psalm 139:16). David knew a book in which things were written before they were. Jubilees shows another page of that same book: judgment ordained and written on the heavenly tables for every creature that leaves the path laid out for it. The path and the penalty were both written down ahead of time, not improvised after the fall. What the Watchers met at the flood was not a new decision; it was a line already written.$n$,$n$And the judgment of all is ordained and written on the heavenly tables in righteousness–even (the judgment of) all who depart from the path which is ordained for them to walk in; and if they walk not therein judgment is written down for every creature and for every kind.$n$,true),
(25665,'S',5,16,5,17,$n$Leviticus 16:29 — *And this shall be a statute for ever unto you: that in the seventh month, on the tenth day of the month, ye shall afflict your souls, and do no work at all.* Jubilees 5:17 promises Israel forgiveness if they turn — the Torah fixes the 'statute for ever' on which that yearly pardon hangs.$n$,$n$And of the children of Yashar’el (Israel) it has been written and ordained: If they turn to Him in righteousness, He will forgive all their transgressions and pardon all their sins.$n$,false),
(25667,'S',5,20,5,21,$n$Genesis 6:14 — *Make thee an ark of gopher wood; rooms shalt thou make in the ark, and shalt pitch it within and without with pitch.* The ark Noah is commanded to make in Jubilees 5:21 is Genesis' ark of gopher wood.$n$,$n$And He commanded Noah to make him an ark, that he might save himself from the waters of the flood.$n$,false),
(25669,'S',5,23,5,25,$n$Genesis 7:12 — *And the rain was upon the earth forty days and forty nights.* The forty days and forty nights of Jubilees 5:25 are Genesis' forty-day rain.$n$,$n$And the flood-gates began to pour down water from the heaven forty days and forty nights, And the fountains of the deep also sent up waters, until the whole world was full of water.$n$,false),
(25670,'S',5,23,5,27,$n$Genesis 7:24 — *And the waters prevailed upon the earth an hundred and fifty days.* The 'five months-one hundred and fifty days' of Jubilees 5:27 is Genesis' hundred and fifty days exactly.$n$,$n$And the water prevailed on the face of the earth five months-one hundred and fifty days.$n$,false),
(25679,'S',6,6,6,7,$n$Genesis 9:4 — *But flesh with the life thereof, which is the blood thereof, shall ye not eat.* The blood prohibition Jubilees 6:7 gives Noah is taken straight from Genesis' covenant with Noah.$n$,$n$But flesh, with the life thereof, with the blood, you shall not eat; for the life of all flesh is in the blood, lest your blood of your lives be required. At the hand of every man, at the hand of every (beast), shall I require the blood of man.$n$,false),
(25681,'S',6,11,6,12,$n$Leviticus 17:10 — *And whatsoever man there be of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), or of the strangers that sojourn among you, that eateth any manner of blood; I will even set my face against that soul that eateth blood, and will cut him off from among his people.* The Torah carries the Noahic no-blood law forward to Israel, with the same cutting-off Jubilees 6:12,13 commands.$n$,$n$And this testimony is written concerning you that you should observe it continually, so that you should not eat on any day any blood of beasts or birds or cattle during all the days of the earth, and the man who eats the blood of beast or of cattle or of birds during all the days of the earth, he and his seed shall be rooted out of the land.$n$,false),
(25682,'S',6,6,6,7,$n$Leviticus 17:11 — *For the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement for your souls: for it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul.* Jubilees 6:7's reason — the life of all flesh is in the blood — is the Torah's own reason, and it is why the blood belongs on the altar (Jubilees 6:14).$n$,$n$But flesh, with the life thereof, with the blood, you shall not eat; for the life of all flesh is in the blood, lest your blood of your lives be required. At the hand of every man, at the hand of every (beast), shall I require the blood of man.$n$,false),
(25683,'S',6,6,6,7,$n$Leviticus 17:14 — *For it is the life of all flesh; the blood of it is for the life thereof: therefore I said unto the children of Yashar'el (Israel), Ye shall eat the blood of no manner of flesh: for the life of all flesh is the blood thereof: whosoever eateth it shall be cut off.* Word for word the rationale of Jubilees 6:7 — the life of all flesh is the blood — binding the same eternal commandment.$n$,$n$But flesh, with the life thereof, with the blood, you shall not eat; for the life of all flesh is in the blood, lest your blood of your lives be required. At the hand of every man, at the hand of every (beast), shall I require the blood of man.$n$,false),
(25699,'S',7,6,7,7,$n$Genesis 9:21 — *And he drank of the wine, and was drunken; and he was uncovered within his tent:* the Genesis source Jubilees 7:7 retells almost verbatim.$n$,$n$And it was evening, and he went into his tent, and being drunken he lay down and slept, and was uncovered in his tent as he slept.$n$,false),
(25708,'S',7,21,7,22,$n$Genesis 6:4 — *There were giants in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the sons of Elohim (God) came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown:* the giants Jubilees 7:22 calls the Nâphîdîm who devoured one another.$n$,$n$And they begat sons the Nâphîdîm, and they were all unlike, and they devoured one another: and the Giants slew the Nâphîl, and the Nâphîl slew the Eljô, and the Eljô mankind, and one man another.$n$,false),
(25709,'S',7,21,7,22,$n$1 Enoch 7:2 — *And they became pregnant, and they bare great giants, whose height was three thousand ells:* the live Enoch witness to the giants Jubilees 7:22 names from the Watchers.$n$,$n$And they begat sons the Nâphîdîm, and they were all unlike, and they devoured one another: and the Giants slew the Nâphîl, and the Nâphîl slew the Eljô, and the Eljô mankind, and one man another.$n$,false),
(25710,'S',7,21,7,22,$n$1 Enoch 7:3 — *Who consumed all the acquisitions of men. And when men could no longer sustain them, the giants turned against them and devoured mankind:* the same mutual devouring Jubilees 7:22 describes among the Nâphîdîm.$n$,$n$And they begat sons the Nâphîdîm, and they were all unlike, and they devoured one another: and the Giants slew the Nâphîl, and the Nâphîl slew the Eljô, and the Eljô mankind, and one man another.$n$,false),
(25721,'S',8,18,8,19,$n$1 Enoch 26:1 — *And I went from thence to the middle of the earth, and I saw a blessed place in which there were trees with branches abiding and blooming [of a dismembered tree].* Enoch's vision of the middle of the earth — the holy mountain and Zion — is the same centre Jubilees 8:19 sets at the navel of the world.$n$,$n$And he knew that the Garden of Eden is the holy of holies, and the dwelling of Yahuah (God), and Mount Sinai the centre of the desert, and Mount Zion–the centre of the navel of the earth: these three were created as holy places facing each other.$n$,false),
(25722,'S',8,18,8,19,$n$Ezekiel 38:12 — *To take a spoil, and to take a prey; to turn thine hand upon the desolate places that are now inhabited, and upon the people that are gathered out of the nations, which have gotten cattle and goods, that dwell in the midst of the land.* The regathered seed dwelling in the navel of the earth — the centre Shem inherits in Jubilees 8:19.$n$,$n$And he knew that the Garden of Eden is the holy of holies, and the dwelling of Yahuah (God), and Mount Sinai the centre of the desert, and Mount Zion–the centre of the navel of the earth: these three were created as holy places facing each other.$n$,false),
(25723,'S',8,18,8,19,$n$Ezekiel 5:5 — *Thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); This is Jerusalem: I have set it in the midst of the nations and countries that are round about her.* Yahuah Himself names Jerusalem the midst of the earth that Jubilees 8:19 calls Zion the navel.$n$,$n$And he knew that the Garden of Eden is the holy of holies, and the dwelling of Yahuah (God), and Mount Sinai the centre of the desert, and Mount Zion–the centre of the navel of the earth: these three were created as holy places facing each other.$n$,false),
(25919,'S',10,17,10,18,$n$Genesis 11:1 — *And the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech.* Jubilees 10:18 names the same moment in Peleg's days — one people, evil in purpose, before the tongues were confounded.$n$,$n$And in the three and thirtieth jubilee, in the first year in the second week, Peleg took to himself a wife, whose name was Lômnâ the daughter of Sînâ’ar, and she bare him a son in the fourth year of this week, and he called his name Reu; for he said: “Behold the children of men have become evil through the wicked purpose of building for themselves a city and a tower in the land of Shinar.”$n$,false),
(25932,'S',11,14,11,16,$n$Acts 7:3 — *And said unto him, Get thee out of thy country, and from thy kindred, and come into the land which I shall shew thee.* The call out of the idolatrous house Jubilees 11:16-17 shows Abraham already straining toward as a child.$n$,$n$And the child began to understand the errors of the earth that all went astray after graven images and after uncleanness, and his father taught him writing, and he was two weeks of years old, and he separated himself from his father that he might not worship idols with him.$n$,false),
(25934,'S',11,14,11,16,$n$Joshua 24:3 — *And I took your father Abraham from the other side of the flood, and led him throughout all the land of Canaan, and multiplied his seed, and gave him Isaac.* The election Jubilees 11:16 dramatizes as the boy separating himself from his father's idols.$n$,$n$And the child began to understand the errors of the earth that all went astray after graven images and after uncleanness, and his father taught him writing, and he was two weeks of years old, and he separated himself from his father that he might not worship idols with him.$n$,false),
(25941,'S',11,14,11,15,$n$Genesis 11:26 — *And Terah lived seventy years, and begat Abram, Nahor, and Haran:* the climax of the line, Abram born to Terah exactly as Jubilees 11:15 narrates.$n$,$n$And in the seventh year of this week she bare him a son, and he called his name Abram, by the name of the father of his mother; for he had died before his daughter had conceived a son.$n$,false),
(25943,'S',11,6,11,7,$n$Genesis 11:22 — *And Serug lived thirty years, and begat Nahor:* matching Jubilees 11:7-8 where Serug dwells in Ur and his wife bears him Nahor.$n$,$n$And he grew up, and dwelt in Ur of the Chaldees, near to the father of his wife’s mother, and he worshipped idols, and he took to himself a wife in the thirty-sixth jubilee, in the fifth week, in the first year thereof, and her name was Mêlkâ, the daughter of Kâbêr, the daughter of his father’s brother.$n$,false),
(25950,'S',12,22,12,23,$n$Genesis 12:3 — *And I will bless them that bless thee, and curse him that curseth thee: and in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed.* The blessing-and-cursing and all-families promise of Jubilees 12:23 is Genesis 12:3 set side by side.$n$,$n$And I shall bless you And I shall make your name great, And you will be blessed in the earth, And in you will all families of the earth be blessed, And I shall bless them that bless you, And curse them that curse you.$n$,false),
(25952,'S',12,17,12,20,$n$1 Enoch 10:16 — *Destroy all wrong from the face of the earth and let every evil work come to an end: and let the plant of righteousness and truth appear: and it shall prove a blessing; the works of righteousness and truth' shall be planted in truth and joy for evermore.* Abram's plea to stablish him and his seed for ever in Jubilees 12:20 is the planting of the righteous seed against the seed-war that 1 Enoch 10 promises after the Watchers are bound.$n$,$n$Deliver me from the hands of evil spirits who have sway over the thoughts of men’s hearts, And let them not lead me astray from You, my Elohim (God). And stablish You me and my seed for ever That we go not astray from henceforth and for evermore."$n$,false),
(25955,'S',12,14,12,15,$n$Genesis 11:31 — *And Terah took Abram his son, and Lot the son of Haran his son's son, and Sarai his daughter in law, his son Abram's wife; and they went forth with them from Ur of the Chaldees, to go into the land of Canaan; and they came unto Haran, and dwelt there.* The family's departure from Ur toward Canaan in Jubilees 12:15 is the same journey Genesis records.$n$,$n$And Terah went forth from Ur of the Chaldees, he and his sons, to go into the land of Lebanon and into the land of Canaan, and he dwelt in the land of Haran, and Abram, dwelt with Terah his father in Haran two weeks of years.$n$,false),
(25959,'S',12,2,12,5,$n$Deuteronomy 4:28 — *And there ye shall serve gods, the work of men's hands, wood and stone, which neither see, nor hear, nor eat, nor smell.* The Torah's verdict on idols is exactly Abram's in Jubilees 12:5: they are the work of men's hands with no spirit in them.$n$,$n$Why do you worship things that have no spirit in them? For they are the work of (men’s) hands, And on your shoulders do you bear them, And you have no help from them, But they are a great cause of shame to those who make them, And a misleading of the heart to those who worship them: Worship them not."$n$,false),
(25968,'S',13,20,13,22,$n$Genesis 14:1 — *And it came to pass in the days of Amraphel king of Shinar, Arioch king of Ellasar, Chedorlaomer king of Elam, and Tidal king of nations.* Jubilees 13:22 names the same coalition of kings.$n$,$n$And in this year came Chedorlaomer, king of Elam, and Amraphel, king of Shinar, and Arioch, king of Sêllâsar and Têrgâl, king of nations, and slew the king of Gomorrah, and the king of Sodom fled, and many fell through wounds in the vale of Siddim, by the Salt Sea.$n$,false),
(25978,'S',13,7,13,8,$n$Genesis 12:8 — *And he removed from thence unto a mountain on the east of Beth-el, and pitched his tent, having Beth-el on the west, and Hai on the east: and there he builded an altar unto Yahuah (LORD), and called upon the name of Yahuah (LORD).* Jubilees 13:8 has Abram between Bethel and Ai building the altar and calling on the Name.$n$,$n$And it came to pass in the first year, in the seventh week, on the new moon of the first month, that he built an altar on this mountain, and called on the name of Yahuah (God): “You, the eternal Elohim (God), are my Elohim (God).”$n$,false),
(25979,'S',13,2,13,3,$n$Genesis 12:7 — *And Yahuah (LORD) appeared unto Abram, and said, Unto thy seed will I give this land: and there builded he an altar unto Yahuah (LORD), who appeared unto him.* The land-and-seed promise and the altar of Jubilees 13:3,4 are Genesis verbatim.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) said to him: “To you and to your seed will I give this land.”$n$,false),
(25984,'T',6,18,6,18,$n$Jubilees 6:17–18 — *For this reason it is ordained and written on the heavenly tables, that they should celebrate the feast of weeks in this month once a year, to renew the covenant every year. And this whole festival was celebrated in heaven from the day of creation till the days of Noah-twenty-six jubilees and five weeks of years: and Noah and his sons observed it for seven jubilees and one week of years, till the day of Noah's death, and from the day of Noah's death his sons did away with (it) until the days of Abraham, and they ate blood.* The covenant Abram renews "as we covenanted with Noah" (Jubilees 14:20) is the Feast of Weeks, engraved on the heavenly tablets and kept since creation.$n$,$n$And this whole festival was celebrated in heaven from the day of creation till the days of Noah-twenty-six jubilees and five weeks of years: and Noah and his sons observed it for seven jubilees and one week of years, till the day of Noah’s death, and from the day of Noah’s death his sons did away with (it) until the days of Abraham, and they ate blood.$n$,false),
(25987,'S',14,16,14,17,$n$Genesis 15:17 — *And it came to pass, that, when the sun went down, and it was dark, behold a smoking furnace, and a burning lamp that passed between those pieces.* The smoking furnace and flame passing between the pieces (Jubilees 14:17) is the sign of Yahuah cutting the covenant alone.$n$,$n$And he awoke from his sleep, and he arose, and the sun had set; and there was a flame, and behold! a furnace was smoking, and a flame of fire passed between the pieces.$n$,false),
(25990,'S',14,6,14,7,$n$Genesis 17:7 — *And I will establish my covenant between me and thee and thy seed after thee in their generations for an everlasting covenant, to be a Elohim (God) unto thee, and to thy seed after thee.* The "to you and to your seed after you" of Jubilees 14:7 is the everlasting seed-covenant Genesis 17 seals with circumcision.$n$,$n$And He said to him: “I am Yahuah (God) that brought you out of Ur of the Chaldees, to give you the land of the Canaanites to possess it for ever; and I shall be Elohim (God) to you and to your seed after you.”$n$,false),
(25993,'S',14,6,14,7,$n$Genesis 15:7 — *And he said unto him, I am Yahuah (LORD) that brought thee out of Ur of the Chaldees, to give thee this land to inherit it.* The self-naming by the deliverance out of Ur (Jubilees 14:7) is Abraham's election out of the idolatrous nations.$n$,$n$And He said to him: “I am Yahuah (God) that brought you out of Ur of the Chaldees, to give you the land of the Canaanites to possess it for ever; and I shall be Elohim (God) to you and to your seed after you.”$n$,false),
(26009,'S',15,18,15,19,$n$Genesis 17:19 — *And Elohim (God) said, Sarah thy wife shall bear thee a son indeed; and thou shalt call his name Isaac: and I will establish my covenant with him for an everlasting covenant, and with his seed after him.* Jubilees 15:19 names Isaac and establishes the everlasting covenant in him exactly as Genesis does.$n$,$n$And Elohim (God) said: “Yea, and Sarah also will bear you a son, and you will call his name Isaac, and I shall establish My covenant with him, an everlasting covenant, and for his seed after him.$n$,false),
(26012,'S',15,5,15,7,$n$Romans 4:17 — *(As it is written, I have made thee a father of many nations,) before him whom he believed, even Elohim (God), who quickeneth the dead, and calleth those things which be not as though they were.* Paul takes the "father of many nations" of Jubilees 15:7 as the seal of Abraham's faith in the God who raises the dead.$n$,$n$Neither will your name any more be called Abram, But your name from henceforth, even for ever, shall be Abraham. For the father of many nations have I made you.$n$,false),
(26014,'S',15,5,15,9,$n$Genesis 17:7 — *And I will establish my covenant between me and thee and thy seed after thee in their generations for an everlasting covenant, to be a Elohim (God) unto thee, and to thy seed after thee.* Jubilees 15:9 names the same everlasting covenant to Abraham and his seed throughout their generations.$n$,$n$And I shall establish My covenant between Me and you, and your seed after you, throughout their generations, for an eternal covenant, so that I may be a Elohim (God) to you, and to your seed after you.$n$,false),
(26015,'S',15,5,15,7,$n$Genesis 17:5 — *Neither shall thy name any more be called Abram, but thy name shall be Abraham; for a father of many nations have I made thee.* Jubilees 15:7 carries the renaming word for word — Abram becomes Abraham, father of many nations forever.$n$,$n$Neither will your name any more be called Abram, But your name from henceforth, even for ever, shall be Abraham. For the father of many nations have I made you.$n$,false),
(26027,'S',16,17,16,18,$n$1 Peter 2:9 — *But ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people; that ye should shew forth the praises of him who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light:* The portion of the Most High, the kingdom and priests of Jubilees 16:18, is the same calling the apostle lays on the regathered remnant.$n$,$n$For he should become the portion of the El Elyon (Most High), and all his seed had fallen into the possession of Elohim (God), that it should be to Yahuah (God) a people for (His) possession above all nations and that it should become a kingdom and priests and a holy nation.$n$,false),
(26028,'S',16,17,16,18,$n$Exodus 19:6 — *And ye shall be unto me a kingdom of priests, and an holy nation. These are the words which thou shalt speak unto the children of Yashar'el (Israel).* The kingdom and priests and holy nation Jubilees 16:18 sees in Abraham's holy seed is Sinai's own charter for Israel.$n$,$n$For he should become the portion of the El Elyon (Most High), and all his seed had fallen into the possession of Elohim (God), that it should be to Yahuah (God) a people for (His) possession above all nations and that it should become a kingdom and priests and a holy nation.$n$,false),
(26032,'S',16,12,16,13,$n$Genesis 21:2 — *For Sarah conceived, and bare Abraham a son in his old age, at the set time of which Elohim (God) had spoken to him.* The set time of which Yahuah had spoken to Abraham is the same set time Jubilees 16:13 records for Isaac's birth.$n$,$n$And she bare a son in the third month, and in the middle of the month, at the time of which Yahuah (God) had spoken to Abraham, on the festival of the first-fruits of the harvest, Isaac was born.$n$,false),
(26035,'S',16,6,16,7,$n$Genesis 19:29 — *And it came to pass, when Elohim (God) destroyed the cities of the plain, that Elohim (God) remembered Abraham, and sent Lot out of the midst of the overthrow, when he overthrew the cities in the which Lot dwelt.* Lot is saved because Elohim remembered Abraham — Jubilees 16:7 keeps this Torah reason verbatim.$n$,$n$But Lot we saved; for Elohim (God) remembered Abraham, and sent him out from the midst of the overthrow.$n$,false),
(26670,'S',17,12,17,13,$n$Genesis 21:20 — *And Elohim (God) was with the lad; and he grew, and dwelt in the wilderness, and became an archer.* The same growth into an archer that Jubilees 17:13 records.$n$,$n$And the child grew and became an archer, and Elohim (God) was with him; and his mother took him a wife from among the daughters of Egypt.$n$,false),
(26681,'S',18,7,18,8,$n$Hebrews 11:17 — *By faith Abraham, when he was tried, offered up Isaac: and he that had received the promises offered up his only begotten son,* The offered son of Jubilees 18:8 is Hebrews' first witness of tried faith — the one who had the promise yet offered the heir.$n$,$n$And he built an altar, and he placed the wood on the altar, and bound Isaac his son, and placed him on the wood which was upon the altar, and stretched forth his hand to take the knife to slay Isaac his son.$n$,false),
(26683,'S',18,7,18,8,$n$James 2:21 — *Was not Abraham our father justified by works, when he had offered Isaac his son upon the altar?* The altar-offering of Jubilees 18:8 is James's proof that Abraham was justified by works, not faith alone.$n$,$n$And he built an altar, and he placed the wood on the altar, and bound Isaac his son, and placed him on the wood which was upon the altar, and stretched forth his hand to take the knife to slay Isaac his son.$n$,false),
(26685,'S',18,14,18,15,$n$Genesis 22:17 — *That in blessing I will bless thee, and in multiplying I will multiply thy seed as the stars of the heaven, and as the sand which is upon the sea shore; and thy seed shall possess the gate of his enemies;* The seed multiplied as the stars and the sand in Jubilees 18:15 is the Genesis oath verbatim — the covenant kept in the fathers.$n$,$n$And He said: "By Myself have I sworn, says Yahuah (God), Because you have done this thing, And have not withheld your son, your beloved son, from Me, That in blessing I shall bless you And in multiplying I shall multiply your seed As the stars of heaven, And as the sand which is on the seashore. And your seed will inherit the cities of its enemies,$n$,false),
(26686,'S',18,14,18,15,$n$Genesis 22:16 — *And said, By myself have I sworn, saith Yahuah (LORD), for because thou hast done this thing, and hast not withheld thy son, thine only son:* The self-sworn oath of Jubilees 18:15 is the Torah's own — Yahuah binds Himself because the son was not withheld.$n$,$n$And He said: "By Myself have I sworn, says Yahuah (God), Because you have done this thing, And have not withheld your son, your beloved son, from Me, That in blessing I shall bless you And in multiplying I shall multiply your seed As the stars of heaven, And as the sand which is on the seashore. And your seed will inherit the cities of its enemies,$n$,false),
(26687,'S',18,12,18,13,$n$Genesis 22:14 — *And Abraham called the name of that place Yahuah Yireh (Jehovah-jireh): as it is said to this day, In the mount of Yahuah (LORD) it shall be seen.* Jubilees 18:13 names the same place — “Yahuah has seen” — and locates it as Mount Sion, the mountain of provision.$n$,$n$And Abraham called that place “Yahuah (God) has seen,” so that it is said “(in the mount) Yahuah (God) has seen”: that is Mount Sion.$n$,false),
(26694,'S',18,1,18,2,$n$Genesis 22:2 — *And he said, Take now thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest, and get thee into the land of Moriah; and offer him there for a burnt offering upon one of the mountains which I will tell thee of.* The command Jubilees 18:2 retells — the beloved son offered on a mountain Elohim will point out — is the very word of Genesis.$n$,$n$And He said, “Take your beloved son whom you love, (even) Isaac, and go to the high country, and offer him on one of the mountains which I will point out to you.”$n$,false),
(26698,'S',19,19,19,22,$n$Genesis 13:16 — *And I will make thy seed as the dust of the earth: so that if a man can number the dust of the earth, then shall thy seed also be numbered.* Abraham's blessing on Jacob in Jubilees 19:22 — seed numberless as the sand — is the very promise spoken to Abraham himself.$n$,$n$If a man can number the sand of the earth, His seed also will be numbered.$n$,false),
(26702,'S',19,14,19,15,$n$Genesis 25:28 — *And Isaac loved Esau, because he did eat of his venison: but Rebekah loved Jacob.* Jubilees 19:15 carries the same divided love — Abraham loved Jacob, Isaac loved Esau.$n$,$n$And Abraham loved Jacob, but Isaac loved Esau.$n$,false),
(26707,'S',19,9,19,10,$n$Genesis 24:67 — *And Isaac brought her into his mother Sarah's tent, and took Rebekah, and she became his wife; and he loved her: and Isaac was comforted after his mother's death.* This is the marriage Jubilees 19:10 records — Abraham takes a wife for his son Isaac.$n$,$n$And in the fourth year thereof he took a wife for his son Isaac and her name was Rebecca the daughter of Bethuel, the son of Nahor, the brother of Abraham the sister of Laban and daughter of Bethuel; and Bethuel was the son of Mêlcâ, who was the wife of Nahor, the brother of Abraham.$n$,false),
(26708,'S',19,9,19,10,$n$Genesis 24:15 — *And it came to pass, before he had done speaking, that, behold, Rebekah came out, who was born to Bethuel, son of Milcah, the wife of Nahor, Abraham's brother, with her pitcher upon her shoulder.* Jubilees 19:10 names her by the same lineage — Rebecca daughter of Bethuel, son of Nahor, brother of Abraham.$n$,$n$And in the fourth year thereof he took a wife for his son Isaac and her name was Rebecca the daughter of Bethuel, the son of Nahor, the brother of Abraham the sister of Laban and daughter of Bethuel; and Bethuel was the son of Mêlcâ, who was the wife of Nahor, the brother of Abraham.$n$,false),
(26716,'S',19,6,19,7,$n$Genesis 23:1 — *And Sarah was an hundred and seven and twenty years old: these were the years of the life of Sarah.* Jubilees 19:7 keeps the same count to the year — one hundred and twenty-seven years of Sarah's life.$n$,$n$And all the days of the life of Sarah were one hundred and twenty-seven years, that is, two jubilees and four weeks and one year: these are the days of the years of the life of Sarah.$n$,false),
(26717,'S',20,10,20,11,$n$Genesis 25:6 — *But unto the sons of the concubines, which Abraham had, Abraham gave gifts, and sent them away from Isaac his son, while he yet lived, eastward, unto the east country.* Jubilees 20:11's sending of Ishmael and Keturah's sons away with gifts, the inheritance reserved to Isaac, is the same event Genesis records.$n$,$n$And he gave to Ishmael and to his sons, and to the sons of Keturah, gifts, and sent them away from Isaac his son, and he gave everything to Isaac his son.$n$,false),
(26736,'S',21,16,21,17,$n$Leviticus 17:13 — *And whatsoever man there be of the children of Yashar'el (Israel), or of the strangers that sojourn among you, which hunteth and catcheth any beast or fowl that may be eaten; he shall even pour out the blood thereof, and cover it with dust.* Abraham's "cover it with dust" is the Torah's own command for the blood of the hunt — kept centuries before Moses wrote it.$n$,$n$And let no blood appear upon you nor upon your clothes; be on your guard, my son, against blood, be on your guard exceedingly; cover it with dust.$n$,false),
(26741,'S',21,6,21,7,$n$Leviticus 1:9 — *But his inwards and his legs shall he wash in water: and the priest shall burn all on the altar, to be a burnt sacrifice, an offering made by fire, of a sweet savour unto Yahuah (LORD).* Abraham's "sweet savour before Yahuah" upon the altar is the Torah's own phrase for the accepted burnt offering.$n$,$n$And if you do slay a victim as an acceptable peace-offering, slay you it, and pour out its blood upon the altar, and all the fat of the offering offer on the altar with fine flour (and the meat-offering) mingled with oil, with its drink-offering–offer them all together on the altar of burnt-offering; it is a sweet savour before Yahuah (God).$n$,false),
(26763,'S',22,9,22,10,$n$Genesis 28:4 — *And give thee the blessing of Abraham, to thee, and to thy seed with thee; that thou mayest inherit the land wherein thou art a stranger, which Elohim (God) gave unto Abraham.* The blessing of Abraham passing to Jacob's seed in Jubilees 22:10 is the very blessing Isaac renews over Jacob at his sending.$n$,$n$And he called Jacob and said My son Jacob, may the Elohim (God) of all bless you and strengthen you to do righteousness, and His will before Him, and may He choose you and your seed that you may become a people for His inheritance according to His will alway. And do you, my son, Jacob, draw near and kiss me."$n$,false),
(26768,'S',23,30,23,32,$n$Malachi 3:16 — *Then they that feared Yahuah (LORD) spake often one to another: and Yahuah (LORD) hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared Yahuah (LORD), and that thought upon his name.* Jubilees' closing “record (them) on the heavenly tables for a testimony” is Malachi's book of remembrance written before Yahuah — the law and the history pre-written.$n$,$n$And do you, Moses, write down these words; for thus are they written, and they record (them) on the heavenly tables for a testimony for the generations for ever.$n$,false),
(26774,'S',23,25,23,26,$n$Deuteronomy 30:3 — *That then Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) will turn thy captivity, and have compassion upon thee, and will return and gather thee from all the nations, whither Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath scattered thee.* The lengthening days and healing of Jubilees 23 follow exactly on the Torah's “return” — the regathering after the return-to-the-way.$n$,$n$And in those days the children will begin to study the laws, And to seek the commandments, And to return to the path of righteousness.$n$,false),
(26775,'S',23,25,23,26,$n$Deuteronomy 30:2 — *And shalt return unto Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God), and shalt obey his voice according to all that I command thee this day, thou and thy children, with all thine heart, and with all thy soul;* Jubilees' children who “seek the commandments, and return to the path of righteousness” are the children the Torah promised would return and obey.$n$,$n$And in those days the children will begin to study the laws, And to seek the commandments, And to return to the path of righteousness.$n$,false),
(26777,'S',23,17,23,22,$n$Isaiah 65:12 — *Therefore will I number you to the sword, and ye shall all bow down to the slaughter: because when I called, ye did not answer; when I spake, ye did not hear; but did evil before mine eyes, and did choose that wherein I delighted not.* Jubilees' faithless generation handed “over to the sword” is Isaiah's people numbered to the sword for refusing to hear.$n$,$n$And a great punishment will befall the deeds of this generation from Yahuah (God), and He will give them over to the sword and to judgment and to captivity, and to be plundered and devoured.$n$,false),
(26780,'S',23,8,23,9,$n$Genesis 6:3 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said, My spirit shall not always strive with man, for that he also is flesh: yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years.* Jubilees' “after the Flood they began to grow less” is the Torah's bounding of human days at the Flood.$n$,$n$For the days of the forefathers, of their life, were nineteen jubilees; and after the Flood they began to grow less than nineteen jubilees, and to decrease in jubilees, and to grow old quickly, and to be full of their days by reason of manifold tribulation and the wickedness of their ways, with the exception of Abraham.$n$,false),
(26781,'S',23,6,23,7,$n$Genesis 25:10 — *The field which Abraham purchased of the sons of Heth: there was Abraham buried, and Sarah his wife.* Jubilees buries him “near Sarah his wife” — exactly Genesis' burial beside Sarah.$n$,$n$And his sons Isaac and Ishmael buried him in the double cave, near Sarah his wife, and they wept for him forty days, all the men of his house, and Isaac and Ishmael, and all their sons, and all the sons of Keturah in their places, and the days of weeping for Abraham were ended.$n$,false),
(26782,'S',23,6,23,7,$n$Genesis 25:9 — *And his sons Isaac and Ishmael buried him in the cave of Machpelah, in the field of Ephron the son of Zohar the Hittite, which is before Mamre;* the same two sons, the same cave — Jubilees' “double cave” is Machpelah.$n$,$n$And his sons Isaac and Ishmael buried him in the double cave, near Sarah his wife, and they wept for him forty days, all the men of his house, and Isaac and Ishmael, and all their sons, and all the sons of Keturah in their places, and the days of weeping for Abraham were ended.$n$,false),
(26785,'S',24,32,24,33,$n$Exodus 32:32 — *Yet now, if thou wilt forgive their sin—; and if not, blot me, I pray thee, out of thy book which thou hast written.* The heavenly tables of Jubilees 24:33, engraved for the day of judgment, are the very book Moses pleads over.$n$,$n$And thus is it written and engraved concerning him on the heavenly tables, to do to him on the day of judgment, so that he may be rooted out of the earth.$n$,false),
(26786,'S',24,32,24,33,$n$Malachi 3:16 — *Then they that feared Yahuah (LORD) spake often one to another: and Yahuah (LORD) hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared Yahuah (LORD), and that thought upon his name.* Jubilees 24:33's “written and engraved concerning him on the heavenly tables” is the same pre-written book of remembrance the canon names.$n$,$n$And thus is it written and engraved concerning him on the heavenly tables, to do to him on the day of judgment, so that he may be rooted out of the earth.$n$,false),
(26788,'S',24,21,24,22,$n$Genesis 26:24 — *And Yahuah (LORD) appeared unto him the same night, and said, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham thy father: fear not, for I am with thee, and will bless thee, and multiply thy seed for my servant Abraham’s sake.* Jubilees 24:22 quotes the Beer-sheba theophany verbatim, adding only its new-moon dating.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) appeared to him that night, on the new moon of the first month, and said to him: “I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham your father; fear not, for I am with you, and shall bless you and shall surely multiply your seed as the sand of the earth, for the sake of Abraham my servant.”$n$,false),
(26798,'S',24,6,24,7,$n$Romans 9:12 — *It was said unto her, The elder shall serve the younger.* Jubilees 24:7 makes the election plain — “Jacob became the elder” — the seed kept by Yahuah's choice, not by merit.$n$,$n$And Jacob became the elder, and Esau was brought down from his dignity.$n$,false),
(26799,'S',24,6,24,7,$n$Hebrews 12:17 — *For ye know how that afterward, when he would have inherited the blessing, he was rejected: for he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears.* Jubilees 24:7 says Esau was “brought down from his dignity” — the rejection Hebrews carries forward.$n$,$n$And Jacob became the elder, and Esau was brought down from his dignity.$n$,false),
(27279,'S',26,21,26,22,$n$Genesis 27:27 — *And he came near, and kissed him: and he smelled the smell of his raiment, and blessed him, and said, See, the smell of my son is as the smell of a field which Yahuah (LORD) hath blessed:* Jubilees 26:22 opens the blessing with the same smell-of-the-field that Yahuah has blessed.$n$,$n$And he smelled the smell of his raiment, and he blessed him and said: "Behold, the smell of my son is as the smell of a (full) field which Yahuah (God) has blessed.$n$,false),
(27280,'S',26,21,26,23,$n$Genesis 27:28 — *Therefore Elohim (God) give thee of the dew of heaven, and the fatness of the earth, and plenty of corn and wine:* The dew of heaven and plenty of corn of Jubilees 26:23 is the Genesis blessing word for word.$n$,$n$And may Yahuah (God) give you of the dew of heaven And of the dew of the earth, and plenty of corn and oil: Let nations serve you, And peoples bow down to you.$n$,false),
(27281,'S',26,21,26,23,$n$Genesis 27:29 — *Let people serve thee, and nations bow down to thee: be lord over thy brethren, and let thy mother’s sons bow down to thee: cursed be every one that curseth thee, and blessed be he that blesseth thee.* Jubilees 26:23,24 carries the same nations-serve-you, mother's-sons-bow, cursed/blessed formula.$n$,$n$And may Yahuah (God) give you of the dew of heaven And of the dew of the earth, and plenty of corn and oil: Let nations serve you, And peoples bow down to you.$n$,false),
(27288,'S',26,29,26,30,$n$Genesis 27:36 — *And he said, Is not he rightly named Jacob? for he hath supplanted me these two times: he took away my birthright; and, behold, now he hath taken away my blessing. And he said, Hast thou not reserved a blessing for me?* Jubilees 26:30 has Esau name the two supplantings — birthright then blessing — in the same words.$n$,$n$And he said to him: “Your brother came with guile, and has taken away your blessing.” And he said: “Now I know why his name is named Jacob: behold, he has supplanted me these two times: he took away my birth-right, and now he has taken away my blessing.”$n$,false),
(27293,'S',26,34,26,35,$n$Genesis 27:41 — *And Esau hated Jacob because of the blessing wherewith his father blessed him: and Esau said in his heart, The days of mourning for my father are at hand; then will I slay my brother Jacob.* Jubilees 26:35 ends with the identical murderous resolve in Esau's heart.$n$,$n$And Esau kept threatening Jacob because of the blessing wherewith his father blessed him, and he said in his heart: “May the days of mourning for my father now come, so that I may slay my brother Jacob.”$n$,false),
(27295,'S',27,26,27,27,$n$Leviticus 27:30 — *And all the tithe of the land, whether of the seed of the land, or of the fruit of the tree, is the LORD’S: it is holy unto Yahuah (LORD).* The Torah engraves as holy the very tithe Jacob vows to Yahuah at Bethel in Jubilees 27:27.$n$,$n$And Jacob vowed a vow to Yahuah (God), saying: “If Yahuah (God) will be with me, and will keep me in this way that I go, and give me bread to eat and raiment to put on, so that I come again to my father’s house in peace, then shall Yahuah (God) be my Elohim (God), and this stone which I have set up as a pillar for a sign in this place, shall be Yahuah (God)’s house, and of all that you give me, I shall give the tenth to you, my Elohim (God).”$n$,false),
(27296,'S',27,26,27,27,$n$Genesis 14:20 — *And blessed be the El Elyon (most high God), which hath delivered thine enemies into thy hand. And he gave him tithes of all.* Abraham tithed before Jacob ever did — the tenth Jacob vows in Jubilees 27:27 is the fathers' practice, not a new thing.$n$,$n$And Jacob vowed a vow to Yahuah (God), saying: “If Yahuah (God) will be with me, and will keep me in this way that I go, and give me bread to eat and raiment to put on, so that I come again to my father’s house in peace, then shall Yahuah (God) be my Elohim (God), and this stone which I have set up as a pillar for a sign in this place, shall be Yahuah (God)’s house, and of all that you give me, I shall give the tenth to you, my Elohim (God).”$n$,false),
(27307,'S',27,11,27,12,$n$Genesis 28:5 — *And Isaac sent away Jacob: and he went to Padan-aram unto Laban, son of Bethuel the Syrian, the brother of Rebekah, Jacob’s and Esau’s mother.* The sending to Mesopotamia/Padan-aram that Jubilees 27:12 records in its own words.$n$,$n$And Isaac sent Jacob away, and he went to Mesopotamia, to Laban the son of Bethuel the Syrian, the brother of Rebecca, Jacob’s mother.$n$,false),
(27316,'S',28,14,28,15,$n$Hebrews 7:14 — *For it is evident that our Lord sprang out of Juda; of which tribe Moses spake nothing concerning priesthood.* The kingly tribe born in Jubilees 28:15 is the tribe of the Messiah — Judah's sceptre fulfilled.$n$,$n$And again Jacob went in to her, and she conceived, and bare him a fourth son, and he called his name Yahudah (Judah), on the fifteenth of the third month, in the first year of the fourth week.$n$,false),
(27317,'S',28,14,28,15,$n$Numbers 24:17 — *...there shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Yashar'el (Israel), and shall smite the corners of Moab, and destroy all the children of Sheth.* The Sceptre of Judah's line, first dated at his birth in Jubilees 28:15, is the Star Balaam foresaw.$n$,$n$And again Jacob went in to her, and she conceived, and bare him a fourth son, and he called his name Yahudah (Judah), on the fifteenth of the third month, in the first year of the fourth week.$n$,false),
(27318,'S',28,14,28,15,$n$Genesis 49:10 — *The sceptre shall not depart from Yahudah (Judah), nor a lawgiver from between his feet, until Shiloh come; and unto him shall the gathering of the people be.* The Yahudah born in Jubilees 28:15 carries the kingship and the Messianic sceptre.$n$,$n$And again Jacob went in to her, and she conceived, and bare him a fourth son, and he called his name Yahudah (Judah), on the fifteenth of the third month, in the first year of the fourth week.$n$,false),
(27336,'S',29,11,29,13,$n$Genesis 36:8 — *Thus dwelt Esau in mount Seir: Esau is Edom.* The Torah seals Esau's settling in Seir that Jubilees 29:13 marks as his departure.$n$,$n$And he passed over the Jabbok in the ninth month, on the eleventh thereof. And on that day Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents.$n$,false),
(27337,'S',29,11,29,13,$n$Genesis 33:16 — *So Esau returned that day on his way unto Seir.* Genesis says exactly what Jubilees 29:13 says: Esau departed to the land of Seir.$n$,$n$And he passed over the Jabbok in the ninth month, on the eleventh thereof. And on that day Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents.$n$,false),
(27338,'S',29,11,29,13,$n$Genesis 33:3 — *And he passed over before them, and bowed himself to the ground seven times, until he came near to his brother.* Jacob's bowing approach is the reconciliation Jubilees 29:13 names in a phrase.$n$,$n$And he passed over the Jabbok in the ninth month, on the eleventh thereof. And on that day Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents.$n$,false),
(27339,'S',29,11,29,13,$n$Genesis 33:1 — *And Jacob lifted up his eyes, and looked, and, behold, Esau came, and with him four hundred men. And he divided the children unto Leah, and unto Rachel, and unto the two handmaids.* Genesis fills out the meeting Jubilees 29:13 records as Esau coming to Jacob.$n$,$n$And he passed over the Jabbok in the ninth month, on the eleventh thereof. And on that day Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents.$n$,false),
(27343,'S',29,6,29,7,$n$Genesis 31:52 — *This heap be witness, and this pillar be witness, that I will not pass over this heap to thee, and that thou shalt not pass over this heap and this pillar unto me, for harm.* This is the exact non-crossing oath Jubilees 29:7 compresses into a single line.$n$,$n$And on the fifteenth of those days Jacob made a feast for Laban, and for all who came with him, and Jacob sware to Laban that day, and Laban also to Jacob, that neither should cross the mountain of Gilead to the other with evil purpose.$n$,false),
(27346,'S',29,6,29,7,$n$Genesis 31:44 — *Now therefore come thou, let us make a covenant, I and thou; and let it be for a witness between me and thee.* Laban's proposal is the covenant-oath Jubilees 29:7 says the two men swore.$n$,$n$And on the fifteenth of those days Jacob made a feast for Laban, and for all who came with him, and Jacob sware to Laban that day, and Laban also to Jacob, that neither should cross the mountain of Gilead to the other with evil purpose.$n$,false),
(27347,'T',27,26,27,27,$n$*And Jacob vowed a vow to Yahuah (God), saying: “If Yahuah (God) will be with me, and will keep me in this way that I go, and give me bread to eat and raiment to put on, so that I come again to my father’s house in peace, then shall Yahuah (God) be my Elohim (God), and this stone which I have set up as a pillar for a sign in this place, shall be Yahuah (God)’s house, and of all that you give me, I shall give the tenth to you, my Elohim (God).”* (Jubilees 27:27). *For he told them how he had seen everything in a dream, even all that He had spoken to him that he should return to his father’s house; and they said: “To every place whither you go we will go with you.”* (Jubilees 29:3). Jacob's vow at Bethel asks for one thing in particular: to come again to his father's house in peace. Years later, when he tells his wives why they are leaving Laban, what he tells them is a dream in which Yahuah (God) spoke to him that he should return to his father's house. The vow and the dream use the same words because the dream is the answer to the vow. Jubilees lets the reader see both ends of it: the stone set up as a pillar with a promise attached, and the command that sends him back to keep it.$n$,$n$And Jacob vowed a vow to Yahuah (God), saying: “If Yahuah (God) will be with me, and will keep me in this way that I go, and give me bread to eat and raiment to put on, so that I come again to my father’s house in peace, then shall Yahuah (God) be my Elohim (God), and this stone which I have set up as a pillar for a sign in this place, shall be Yahuah (God)’s house, and of all that you give me, I shall give the tenth to you, my Elohim (God).”$n$,true),
(27351,'S',30,21,30,22,$n$Revelation 3:5 — *He that overcometh, the same shall be clothed in white raiment; and I will not blot out his name out of the book of life, but I will confess his name before my Father, and before his angels.* The Messiah keeps the same book of life Jubilees 30:22 says the unfaithful are blotted from.$n$,$n$But if they transgress and work uncleanness in every way, they will be recorded on the heavenly tables as adversaries, and they will be destroyed out of the book of life, and they will be recorded in the book of those who will be destroyed and with those who will be rooted out of the earth.$n$,false),
(27354,'S',30,21,30,22,$n$Exodus 32:33 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Moses, Whosoever hath sinned against me, him will I blot out of my book.* The blotting from the book is exactly the destruction out of the book of life threatened in Jubilees 30:22.$n$,$n$But if they transgress and work uncleanness in every way, they will be recorded on the heavenly tables as adversaries, and they will be destroyed out of the book of life, and they will be recorded in the book of those who will be destroyed and with those who will be rooted out of the earth.$n$,false),
(27356,'S',30,16,30,17,$n$Deuteronomy 33:9 — *Who said unto his father and to his mother, I have not seen him; neither did he acknowledge his brethren, nor knew his own children: for they have observed thy word, and kept thy covenant.* Levi's setting of covenant above kin is the same zeal reckoned for righteousness in Jubilees 30:17,18.$n$,$n$For this reason I have commanded you, saying: "Testify this testimony to Yashar’el (Israel): see how the Shechemites fared and their sons: how they were delivered into the hands of two sons of Jacob, and they slew them under tortures, and it was (reckoned) to them for righteousness, and it is written down to them for righteousness.$n$,false),
(27374,'S',31,32,31,32,$n$*And he said: “Now I know that I have an eternal hope, and my sons also, before the Elohim (God) of all;” and thus is it ordained concerning the two; and they record it as an eternal testimony to them on the heavenly tables how Isaac blessed them.* (Jubilees 31:32). *Thine eyes did see my substance, yet being unperfect; and in thy book all my members were written, which in continuance were fashioned, when as yet there was none of them.* (Psalm 139:16). Isaac's blessing on Levi and Judah is not left to the wind; it is recorded on the heavenly tables as an eternal testimony. David looked into that same book and found his own members written there before any of them were. The book holds both: a man's frame before he is formed, and a father's blessing after it is spoken. Isaac can say "now I know that I have an eternal hope" because what is written in heaven does not fade with the voice that spoke it.$n$,$n$And he said: “Now I know that I have an eternal hope, and my sons also, before the Elohim (God) of all;” and thus is it ordained concerning the two; and they record it as an eternal testimony to them on the heavenly tables how Isaac blessed them.$n$,true),
(27378,'S',31,18,31,19,$n$Hebrews 7:14 — *For it is evident that our Lord sprang out of Juda; of which tribe Moses spake nothing concerning priesthood.* The deliverer found in Judah (Jubilees 31:19) is the Lord the New Testament says sprang out of Judah.$n$,$n$In you shall be the help of Jacob, And in you be found the salvation of Yashar’el (Israel).$n$,false),
(27379,'S',31,18,31,19,$n$Numbers 24:17 — *I shall see him, but not now… there shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Yashar'el (Israel), and shall smite the corners of Moab, and destroy all the children of Sheth.* The salvation of Israel in Judah (Jubilees 31:19) is the Sceptre that rises out of Israel.$n$,$n$In you shall be the help of Jacob, And in you be found the salvation of Yashar’el (Israel).$n$,false),
(27383,'S',31,13,31,14,$n$Numbers 18:2 — *And thy brethren also of the tribe of Levi, the tribe of thy father, bring thou with thee, that they may be joined unto thee, and minister unto thee: but thou and thy sons with thee shall minister before the tabernacle of witness.* The Levites' joining to minister is the drawing-near to serve Isaac foretells in Jubilees 31:14.$n$,$n$And may Yahuah (God) give to you and to your seed greatness and great glory, and cause you and your seed, from among all flesh, to approach Him to serve in His sanctuary as the angels of the presence and as the holy ones. (Even) as they, will the seed of your sons be for glory and greatness and holiness, and may He make them great to all the ages.$n$,false),
(27384,'S',31,13,31,14,$n$Deuteronomy 10:8 — *At that time Yahuah (LORD) separated the tribe of Levi, to bear the ark of the covenant of Yahuah (LORD), to stand before Yahuah (LORD) to minister unto him, and to bless in his name, unto this day.* Levi's seed approaching to serve in the sanctuary (Jubilees 31:14) is the separated tribe that stands before Yahuah.$n$,$n$And may Yahuah (God) give to you and to your seed greatness and great glory, and cause you and your seed, from among all flesh, to approach Him to serve in His sanctuary as the angels of the presence and as the holy ones. (Even) as they, will the seed of your sons be for glory and greatness and holiness, and may He make them great to all the ages.$n$,false),
(27386,'S',31,13,31,14,$n$Deuteronomy 33:8 — *And of Levi he said, Let thy Thummim and thy Urim be with thy holy one, whom thou didst prove at Massah, and with whom thou didst strive at the waters of Meribah;* Moses' blessing of Levi as Yahuah's holy one matches Isaac drawing Levi's seed near to serve in the sanctuary (Jubilees 31:14).$n$,$n$And may Yahuah (God) give to you and to your seed greatness and great glory, and cause you and your seed, from among all flesh, to approach Him to serve in His sanctuary as the angels of the presence and as the holy ones. (Even) as they, will the seed of your sons be for glory and greatness and holiness, and may He make them great to all the ages.$n$,false),
(27387,'T',27,26,27,26,$n$Jubilees 27:26–27 — *And Jacob arose early in the morning, and took the stone which he had put under his head and set it up as a pillar for a sign… And Jacob vowed a vow to Yahuah (God)… of all that you give me, I shall give the tenth to you, my Elohim (God).* The pillar and the sacrifice of Jubilees 31:3 pay the vow Jubilees first recorded at the ladder.$n$,$n$And Jacob arose early in the morning, and took the stone which he had put under his head and set it up as a pillar for a sign, and he poured oil upon the top of it. And he called the name of that place Bethel; but the name of the place was Luz at the first.$n$,false),
(27398,'S',32,20,32,21,$n$*And he saw in a vision of the night, and behold an angel descended from heaven with seven tablets in his hands, and he gave them to Jacob, and he read them and knew all that was written therein which would befall him and his sons through- out all the ages.* (Jubilees 32:21). *Thine eyes did see my substance, yet being unperfect; and in thy book all my members were written, which in continuance were fashioned, when as yet there was none of them.* (Psalm 139:16). Jacob is handed seven tablets and reads on them everything that will befall him and his sons through all the ages, and he reads it as settled, not as possibility. David had seen the same kind of writing: his members written in the book of Yahuah (God) when as yet there was none of them. The tablets Jacob read and the book David speaks of are the same heavenly writing, where the days are set down before they are lived. What came to Jacob's sons over the ages had already been read by their father at Bethel.$n$,$n$And he saw in a vision of the night, and behold an angel descended from heaven with seven tablets in his hands, and he gave them to Jacob, and he read them and knew all that was written therein which would befall him and his sons through- out all the ages.$n$,true),
(27403,'S',32,10,32,10,$n$*And for this reason it is ordained on the heavenly tables as a law for the tithing again the tithe to eat before Yahuah (God) from year to year, in the place where it is chosen that His name should dwell, and to this law there is no limit of days for ever.* (Jubilees 32:10). *And thou shalt eat before Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God), in the place which he shall choose to place his name there, the tithe of thy corn, of thy wine, and of thine oil, and the firstlings of thy herds and of thy flocks; that thou mayest learn to fear Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) always.* (Deuteronomy 14:23). Moses' command to eat the tithe before Yahuah (God) in the place where he sets his name is not new at Sinai. Jubilees roots it in Jacob at Bethel tithing again the tithe, and says it is ordained on the heavenly tables with no limit of days. The place, the eating, and the name dwelling there are the same in both books. A law written in heaven before it was given through Moses is not one a later century can retire; Jubilees says plainly that it has no end of days.$n$,$n$And for this reason it is ordained on the heavenly tables as a law for the tithing again the tithe to eat before Yahuah (God) from year to year, in the place where it is chosen that His name should dwell, and to this law there is no limit of days for ever.$n$,true),
(28021,'S',33,16,33,16,$n$*For until that time there had not been revealed the ordinance and judgment and law in its completeness for all, but in your days (it has been revealed) as a law of seasons and of days, and an everlasting law for the everlasting generations.* (Jubilees 33:16). *Thine eyes did see my substance, yet being unperfect; and in thy book all my members were written, which in continuance were fashioned, when as yet there was none of them.* (Psalm 139:16). The law against a son lying with his father's wife was always written; Jubilees says only that it had not yet been revealed in its completeness until the days of Moses, and then as an everlasting law for every generation. That is the order David describes: the thing stands in the book first, and the day it appears on earth comes afterward. Reuben was spared the full penalty not because the law did not exist but because it had not yet been shown. Revelation has a date; the writing does not.$n$,$n$For until that time there had not been revealed the ordinance and judgment and law in its completeness for all, but in your days (it has been revealed) as a law of seasons and of days, and an everlasting law for the everlasting generations.$n$,true),
(28023,'S',33,11,33,12,$n$Deuteronomy 27:20 — *Cursed be he that lieth with his father's wife; because he uncovereth his father's skirt. And all the people shall say, Amen.* The very 'second time' curse Jubilees 33:12 quotes, with the people's answering 'So be it; so be it.'$n$,$n$And again, it is written a second time: “Cursed he be who lies with the wife of his father, for he has uncovered his father’s shame”; and all the holy ones of Yahuah (God) said “So be it; so be it.”$n$,false),
(28030,'S',34,19,34,20,$n$Genesis 28:1 — *And Isaac called Jacob, and blessed him, and charged him, and said unto him, Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Canaan.* Isaac's charge to Jacob is the seed-kept concern that the wife-roster of Jubilees 34:20 quietly measures.$n$,$n$And after Joseph perished, the sons of Jacob took to themselves wives. The name of Reuben’s wife is ’Adâ and the name of Simeon’s wife is ’Adîbâ’a, a Canaanite; and the name of Levi’s wife is Mêlkâ, of the daughters of Aram, of the seed of the sons of Terah; and the name of Yahudah’s (Judah’s) wife, Bêtasû’êl, a Canaanite; and the name of Issachar’s wife, Hêzaqâ; and the name of Zebulon’s wife, Nî’îmân; and the name of Dan’s wife, ’Êglâ; and the name of Naphtali’s wife, Rasû’û, of Mesopotamia; and the name of Gad’s wife, Mâka; and the name of Asher’s wife, ’Îjônâ; and the name of Joseph’s wife, Asenath, the Egyptian; and the name of Benjamin’s wife, ’Îjasaka.$n$,false),
(28031,'S',34,19,34,20,$n$Genesis 24:3 — *And I will make thee swear by Yahuah (LORD), the Elohim (God) of heaven, and the Elohim (God) of the earth, that thou shalt not take a wife unto my son of the daughters of the Canaanites, among whom I dwell.* Abraham's oath frames Jubilees 34:20's careful naming of which wives were Canaanite and which were of the line of Terah.$n$,$n$And after Joseph perished, the sons of Jacob took to themselves wives. The name of Reuben’s wife is ’Adâ and the name of Simeon’s wife is ’Adîbâ’a, a Canaanite; and the name of Levi’s wife is Mêlkâ, of the daughters of Aram, of the seed of the sons of Terah; and the name of Yahudah’s (Judah’s) wife, Bêtasû’êl, a Canaanite; and the name of Issachar’s wife, Hêzaqâ; and the name of Zebulon’s wife, Nî’îmân; and the name of Dan’s wife, ’Êglâ; and the name of Naphtali’s wife, Rasû’û, of Mesopotamia; and the name of Gad’s wife, Mâka; and the name of Asher’s wife, ’Îjônâ; and the name of Joseph’s wife, Asenath, the Egyptian; and the name of Benjamin’s wife, ’Îjasaka.$n$,false),
(28036,'S',34,16,34,17,$n$Genesis 37:35 — *And all his sons and all his daughters rose up to comfort him; but he refused to be comforted; and he said, For I will go down into the grave unto my son mourning. Thus his father wept for him.* Jacob's vow in Jubilees 34:17, 'Let me go down to the grave mourning for my son,' echoes Genesis word for word.$n$,$n$And he mourned for Joseph one year, and did not cease, for he said “Let me go down to the grave mourning for my son.”$n$,false),
(28039,'S',34,11,34,12,$n$Genesis 37:31 — *And they took Joseph's coat, and killed a kid of the goats, and dipped the coat in the blood.* The slaughtered kid and the dipped coat of Jubilees 34:12 match Genesis stitch for stitch.$n$,$n$And the sons of Jacob slaughtered a kid, and dipped the coat of Joseph in the blood, and sent (it) to Jacob their father on the tenth of the seventh month.$n$,false),
(28044,'S',34,6,34,7,$n$Genesis 48:21 — *And Yashar'el (Israel) said unto Joseph, Behold, I die: but Elohim (God) shall be with you, and bring you again unto the land of your fathers.* The same Jacob who slew the Amorites in the pastures of Shechem (Jubilees 34:7) blesses Joseph with the portion he won there.$n$,$n$And he slew them in the pastures of Shechem, and pursued those who fled, and he slew them with the edge of the sword, and he slew ’Arêsa and Tâphû and Sarêgân and Sêlô and ’Amânîsakîr and Gâgâ’as, and he recovered his herds.$n$,false),
(28046,'S',35,26,35,27,$n$Genesis 35:27 — *And Jacob came unto Isaac his father unto Mamre, unto the city of Arbah, which is Hebron, where Abraham and Isaac sojourned.* The double cave near Sarah where Esau and Jacob bury Rebekah sits at Hebron-Mamre, the patriarchs' dwelling in the Genesis account Jubilees retells.$n$,$n$And they ate and drank, she and her sons that night, and she died, three jubilees and one week and one year old, on that night, and her two sons, Esau and Jacob, buried her in the double cave near Sarah, their father’s mother.$n$,false),
(28075,'S',36,6,36,7,$n$Deuteronomy 6:5 — *And thou shalt love Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy might.* Isaac binds his sons to fear and worship Yahuah in Jubilees 36:7 — the first of the two great commandments.$n$,$n$And now I shall make you swear a great oath–for there is no oath which is greater than it by the name glorious and honoured and great and splendid and wonderful and mighty, which created the heavens and the earth and all things together–that you will fear Him and worship Him.$n$,false),
(28094,'S',38,14,38,15,$n$Genesis 36:31 — *And these are the kings that reigned in the land of Edom, before there reigned any king over the children of Yashar’el (Israel)* — Jubilees 38:15 carries Genesis’ own heading word for word, the kings of Edom before Israel had a king.$n$,$n$And these are the kings that reigned in Edom before there reigned any king over the children of Yashar’el (Israel) until this day in the land of Edom.$n$,false),
(28098,'S',38,11,38,12,$n$Malachi 1:3 — *And I hated Esau, and laid his mountains and his heritage waste for the dragons of the wilderness* — the love-of-Jacob, hatred-of-Esau election that grounds Jubilees 38:12 is the same Yahuah declares to Israel through Malachi.$n$,$n$And Jacob sent word to his sons that they should make peace, and they made peace with them, and placed the yoke of servitude upon them, so that they paid tribute to Jacob and to his sons always.$n$,false),
(28099,'S',38,11,38,12,$n$Numbers 24:18 — *And Edom shall be a possession, Seir also shall be a possession for his enemies; and Yashar’el (Israel) shall do valiantly* — Balaam’s oracle of Edom-as-possession is the standing yoke of tribute laid on Esau’s sons in Jubilees 38:12.$n$,$n$And Jacob sent word to his sons that they should make peace, and they made peace with them, and placed the yoke of servitude upon them, so that they paid tribute to Jacob and to his sons always.$n$,false),
(28110,'S',39,11,39,12,$n$Genesis 39:21 — *But Yahuah (LORD) was with Joseph, and shewed him mercy, and gave him favour in the sight of the keeper of the prison.* The very favour-in-prison Jubilees 39:12 records — Yahuah with Joseph before the chief of the prison guards.$n$,$n$And he was there in the prison; and Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour in the sight of the chief of the prison guards and compassion before him, for he saw that Yahuah (God) was with him, and that Yahuah (God) made all that he did to prosper.$n$,false),
(28126,'S',40,9,40,7,$n$*And he clothed him with byssus garments, and he put a gold chain upon his neck, and (a herald) proclaimed before him “’Êl ’Êl wa’ Abîrĕr,” and he placed a ring on his hand and made him ruler over all his house, and magnified him, and said to him: “Only on the throne shall I be greater than you.”* (Jubilees 40:7). *He made him lord of his house, and ruler of all his substance:* (Psalm 105:21). Pharaoh does the clothing, the chain, the ring, and the speaking, and Jubilees records every piece of it; the herald even cries before Joseph as he passes. The psalm names none of that. It says *He made him lord of his house*, and the He of Psalm 105 is Yahuah (God), whom the psalm has been praising from its first line. Jubilees shows the hand of the king setting Joseph over all his house; the psalm shows whose hand moved the king. The ruler of all Egypt was made ruler by the one who had been with him in the pit and the prison.$n$,$n$And he clothed him with byssus garments, and he put a gold chain upon his neck, and (a herald) proclaimed before him “’Êl ’Êl wa’ Abîrĕr,” and he placed a ring on his hand and made him ruler over all his house, and magnified him, and said to him: “Only on the throne shall I be greater than you.”$n$,true),
(28128,'S',40,10,40,12,$n$Daniel 2:28 — *But there is a Elohim (God) in heaven that revealeth secrets, and maketh known to the king Nebuchadnezzar what shall be in the latter days. Thy dream, and the visions of thy head upon thy bed, are these.* Joseph the dream-revealer of Jubilees 40 is the same type Daniel stands in — Elohim, not Egypt's or Babylon's wise men, reveals the secret.$n$,$n$And in that year Isaac died. And it came to pass as Joseph had said in the interpretation of his two dreams, according as he had said it, there were seven years of plenty over all the land of Egypt, and the land of Egypt produced abundantly, one measure (producing) eighteen hundred measures.$n$,false),
(28131,'T',39,12,39,12,$n$Jubilees 39:11–12 — *And the Egyptian saw the garment of Joseph and the broken door, and heard the words of his wife, and cast Joseph into prison… and Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour in the sight of the chief of the prison guards and compassion before him, for he saw that Yahuah (God) was with him, and that Yahuah (God) made all that he did to prosper.* The upright Joseph of Jubilees 40:8 is the same man who kept his purity in Jubilees 39:12 — “Yahuah was with him” ties the pit to the throne.$n$,$n$And he was there in the prison; and Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour in the sight of the chief of the prison guards and compassion before him, for he saw that Yahuah (God) was with him, and that Yahuah (God) made all that he did to prosper.$n$,false),
(28133,'S',40,6,40,7,$n$Genesis 41:42 — *And Pharaoh took off his ring from his hand, and put it upon Joseph’s hand, and arrayed him in vestures of fine linen, and put a gold chain about his neck.* The ring, the byssus garments, and the gold chain of Jubilees 40:7 are the regalia of Genesis 41:42.$n$,$n$And he clothed him with byssus garments, and he put a gold chain upon his neck, and (a herald) proclaimed before him “’Êl ’Êl wa’ Abîrĕr,” and he placed a ring on his hand and made him ruler over all his house, and magnified him, and said to him: “Only on the throne shall I be greater than you.”$n$,false),
(28134,'S',40,6,40,7,$n$Genesis 41:40 — *Thou shalt be over my house, and according unto thy word shall all my people be ruled: only in the throne will I be greater than thou.* Pharaoh's words in Jubilees 40:7 (“made him ruler over all his house… Only on the throne shall I be greater than you”) are Genesis 41:40 exactly.$n$,$n$And he clothed him with byssus garments, and he put a gold chain upon his neck, and (a herald) proclaimed before him “’Êl ’Êl wa’ Abîrĕr,” and he placed a ring on his hand and made him ruler over all his house, and magnified him, and said to him: “Only on the throne shall I be greater than you.”$n$,false),
(28136,'S',40,3,40,3,$n$*And he said before Pharaoh that his two dreams were one, and he said to him: “Seven years will come (in which there will be) plenty over all the land of Egypt, and after that seven years of famine, such a famine as has not been in all the land.* (Jubilees 40:3). *And there shall arise after them seven years of famine; and all the plenty shall be forgotten in the land of Egypt; and the famine shall consume the land;* (Genesis 41:30). Joseph's reading of the dreams stands word for word between the two books: seven years of plenty over all Egypt, then seven years of famine. Jubilees adds that the two dreams were one, and that the famine would be one such as had not been seen in the land; Genesis says the plenty will be forgotten and the famine will consume the land. It is the same interpreter speaking, and the interpretation came from Elohim (God), not from Joseph, which is why it came to pass exactly.$n$,$n$And he said before Pharaoh that his two dreams were one, and he said to him: “Seven years will come (in which there will be) plenty over all the land of Egypt, and after that seven years of famine, such a famine as has not been in all the land.$n$,true),
(28873,'S',41,26,41,27,$n$Ruth 4:17 — *And the women her neighbours gave it a name, saying, There is a son born to Naomi; and they called his name Obed: he is the father of Jesse, the father of David.* The seed “not rooted out” in Jubilees 41:27 runs through Perez down to David.$n$,$n$And to Yahudah (Judah) we said that his two sons had not lain with her, and for this reason his seed was established for a second generation, and would not be rooted out.$n$,false),
(28890,'S',42,21,42,22,$n$Genesis 50:20 — *But as for you, ye thought evil against me; but Elohim (God) meant it unto good, to bring to pass, as it is this day, to save much people alive.* The feast Joseph spreads in Jubilees 42:22 foreshadows his final word: the evil done him was meant by Elohim for good, to save much people alive.$n$,$n$And he sent him into his house and he brought forth Simeon to them and he made a feast for them, and they presented to him the gift which they had brought in their hands.$n$,false),
(28898,'S',42,11,42,12,$n$Genesis 42:35 — *And it came to pass as they emptied their sacks, that, behold, every man’s bundle of money was in his sack: and when both they and their father saw the bundles of money, they were afraid.* The returned money that makes Jacob fear to send Benjamin in Jubilees 42:12 is the silver discovered in Genesis.$n$,$n$For he saw that their money had been returned to every man in his sack, and for this reason he feared to send him.$n$,false),
(28901,'S',42,6,42,7,$n$Genesis 42:25 — *Then Joseph commanded to fill their sacks with corn, and to restore every man’s money into his sack, and to give them provision for the way: and thus did he unto them.* The gold put back in the sacks unknown in Jubilees 42:7 is Genesis' restored money.$n$,$n$And he filled their sacks with corn, and he put their gold in their sacks, and they did not know.$n$,false),
(28927,'S',43,6,43,7,$n$Genesis 44:12 — *And he searched, and began at the eldest, and left at the youngest: and the cup was found in Benjamin’s sack* — the same search order and discovery that Jubilees 43:7 records.$n$,$n$And as he was searching in their vessels, beginning with the eldest and ending with the youngest, it was found in Benjamin’s sack.$n$,false),
(28944,'S',44,6,44,6,$n$*I shall go down with you, and I shall bring you up (again), and in this land will you be buried, and Joseph will put his hands upon your eyes. Fear not; go down into Egypt.”* (Jubilees 44:6). *And also that nation, whom they shall serve, will I judge: and afterward shall they come out with great substance.* (Genesis 15:14). When Jacob fears to go down to Egypt, the word he receives is the oath to his grandfather coming due. Abram was told his seed would serve a nation, that the nation would be judged, and that they would come out afterward with great wealth. "I shall go down with you, and I shall bring you up" is that promise in the first person, spoken to the man about to enter the bondage. Jacob's burial in Canaan and Joseph's hands on his eyes are the near end; the coming out with much is the far end, fixed before he ever went down.$n$,$n$I shall go down with you, and I shall bring you up (again), and in this land will you be buried, and Joseph will put his hands upon your eyes. Fear not; go down into Egypt.”$n$,true),
(28950,'T',31,13,31,13,$n$Jubilees 31:13–14 — *And he turned to Levi first, and began to bless him first, and said to him:, ’May the Elohim (God) of all, the very Yahuah (God) of all the ages, bless you and your children throughout all the ages. And may Yahuah (God) give to you and to your seed greatness and great glory, and cause you and your seed, from among all flesh, to approach Him to serve in His sanctuary as the angels of the presence and as the holy ones.* Jubilees 45:15 entrusts the books to Levi — the very priestly line Isaac blessed to serve in the sanctuary.$n$,$n$And he turned to Levi first, and began to bless him first, and said to him:, ’May the Elohim (God) of all, the very Yahuah (God) of all the ages, bless you and your children throughout all the ages.$n$,false),
(28953,'S',45,13,45,14,$n$Genesis 49:1 — *And Jacob called unto his sons, and said, Gather yourselves together, that I may tell you that which shall befall you in the last days.* Jubilees 45:14 says Israel made known to his sons what would come upon them in the last days — this is that deathbed prophecy.$n$,$n$And Yashar’el (Israel) blessed his sons before he died and told them everything that would befall them in the land of Egypt; and he made known to them what would come upon them in the last days, and blessed them and gave to Joseph two portions in the land.$n$,false),
(28955,'S',45,11,45,12,$n$Genesis 47:26 — *And Joseph made it a law over the land of Egypt unto this day, that Pharaoh should have the fifth part; except the land of the priests only, which became not Pharaoh’s.* Jubilees 45:12 calls it an ordinance for Egypt "until this day" — the very phrasing of Genesis.$n$,$n$And Joseph took of the corn of the harvest the fifth part for the king and left four parts for them for food and for seed, and Joseph made it an ordinance for the land of Egypt until this day.$n$,false),
(28956,'S',45,11,45,12,$n$Genesis 47:24 — *And it shall come to pass in the increase, that ye shall give the fifth part unto Pharaoh, and four parts shall be your own, for seed of the field, and for your food, and for them of your households, and for food for your little ones.* The fifth-for-the-king, four-parts-for-the-people split of Jubilees 45:12 is taken straight from Joseph's decree here.$n$,$n$And Joseph took of the corn of the harvest the fifth part for the king and left four parts for them for food and for seed, and Joseph made it an ordinance for the land of Egypt until this day.$n$,false),
(28974,'S',46,6,46,7,$n$Exodus 1:8 — *Now there arose up a new king over Egypt, which knew not Joseph.* The stronger new king of Jubilees 46:7 is Exodus' king who knew not Joseph.$n$,$n$But he was not able to enter, for another, a new king, had become king of Egypt, and he was stronger than he, and he returned to the land of Canaan, and the gates of Egypt were closed, and none went out and none came into Egypt.$n$,false),
(28981,'S',47,11,47,12,$n$Hebrews 11:27 — *By faith he forsook Egypt, not fearing the wrath of the king: for he endured, as seeing him who is invisible.* The flight from Pharaoh's wrath in Jubilees 47:12 is the faith Hebrews credits to Moses' forsaking of Egypt.$n$,$n$And he was angry and indignant, and said “Who made you a prince and a judge over us? Thinkest you to kill me as you killedst the Egyptian yesterday?” And you did fear and flee on account of these words.$n$,false),
(28982,'S',47,11,47,12,$n$Acts 7:25 — *For he supposed his brethren would have understood how that Elohim (God) by his hand would deliver them: but they understood not.* The brother who turns on Moses in Jubilees 47:12 is Stephen's portrait of the deliverer rejected by his own.$n$,$n$And he was angry and indignant, and said “Who made you a prince and a judge over us? Thinkest you to kill me as you killedst the Egyptian yesterday?” And you did fear and flee on account of these words.$n$,false),
(28983,'S',47,11,47,12,$n$Exodus 2:14 — *And he said, Who made thee a prince and a judge over us? intendest thou to kill me, as thou killedst the Egyptian? And Moses feared, and said, Surely this thing is known.* The rebuke and the fearful flight in Jubilees 47:12 quote Exodus 2:14 nearly verbatim.$n$,$n$And he was angry and indignant, and said “Who made you a prince and a judge over us? Thinkest you to kill me as you killedst the Egyptian yesterday?” And you did fear and flee on account of these words.$n$,false),
(29006,'S',48,12,48,13,$n$Exodus 14:21 — *And Moses stretched out his hand over the sea; and Yahuah (LORD) caused the sea to go back by a strong east wind all that night, and made the sea dry land, and the waters were divided.* This is the sea crossed 'as if it were dry land' in Jubilees 48:13.$n$,$n$And I stood between the Egyptians and Yashar’el (Israel), and we delivered Yashar’el (Israel) out of his hand, and out of the hand of his people, and Yahuah (God) brought them through the midst of the sea as if it were dry land.$n$,false),
(29007,'S',48,12,48,13,$n$Exodus 14:19 — *And the angel of Elohim (God), which went before the camp of Yashar'el (Israel), removed and went behind them; and the pillar of the cloud went from before their face, and stood behind them.* This is the 'I stood between the Egyptians and Yashar'el' of Jubilees 48:13.$n$,$n$And I stood between the Egyptians and Yashar’el (Israel), and we delivered Yashar’el (Israel) out of his hand, and out of the hand of his people, and Yahuah (God) brought them through the midst of the sea as if it were dry land.$n$,false),
(29020,'S',49,21,49,22,$n$Exodus 12:15 — *Seven days shall ye eat unleavened bread; even the first day ye shall put away leaven out of your houses: for whosoever eateth leavened bread from the first day until the seventh day, that soul shall be cut off from Yashar'el (Israel).* Jubilees' "unleavened bread seven days" is the Torah's seven-day feast joined to Passover.$n$,$n$And do you, Moses, command the children of Yashar’el (Israel) to observe the ordinances of the passover, as it was commanded to you; declare you to them every year and the day of its days, and the festival of unleavened bread, that they should eat unleavened bread seven days, (and) that they should observe its festival, and that they bring an oblation every day during those seven days of joy before Yahuah (God) on the altar of your Elohim (God).$n$,false),
(29110,'T',4,17,4,17,$n$Jubilees 4:17–18 — *And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom... And he was the first to write a testimony, and he testified to the sons of men among the generations of the earth* the wisdom Jasher 3:8 says the spirit of Elohim gave Enoch to teach all his men.$n$,$n$And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book, that men might know the seasons of the years according to the order of their separate months.$n$,false),
(29154,'T',5,8,5,8,$n$*And He said “Your spirit will not always abide on man; for they also are flesh and their days shall be one hundred and twenty years.”* (Jubilees 5:8). *For thus says Yahuah (the Lord), Behold I give you a period of one hundred and twenty years; if you will turn to me and forsake your evil ways, then will I also turn away from the evil which I told you, and it shall not exist, says Yahuah (the Lord).* (Jasher 5:8). Both books put the same number on the same mercy. In Jubilees it is a limit set on flesh: the Spirit will not always abide on man, and a hundred and twenty years is the measure. Jasher hears it as a door left open: a period of a hundred and twenty years, and if you will turn to me the evil will not come. Genesis 6:3 carries the number; these two tell you it was a space for repentance before the flood, not merely a lifespan. The decree and the offer were the same hundred and twenty years.$n$,$n$And He said “Your spirit will not always abide on man; for they also are flesh and their days shall be one hundred and twenty years.”$n$,true),
(29167,'T',5,22,5,22,$n$*And Noah made the ark in all respects as He commanded him, in the twenty-seventh jubilee of years, in the fifth week in the fifth year (on the new moon of the first month).* (Jubilees 5:22). *And Noah rose up, and he made the ark, in the place where Elohim had commanded him, and Noah did as Elohim had ordered him.* (Jasher 5:33). Jasher says Noah rose up and made the ark where Elohim (God) commanded; Jubilees says he made it in all respects as He commanded, and gives the date. Obedience is the point in both. Jubilees adds the calendar, the twenty-seventh jubilee, fifth week, fifth year, on the new moon of the first month, because Jubilees counts everything by the reckoning of time Yahuah (God) gave. The ark was built on command, in the right place, and on a dated day.$n$,$n$And Noah made the ark in all respects as He commanded him, in the twenty-seventh jubilee of years, in the fifth week in the fifth year (on the new moon of the first month).$n$,true),
(29198,'T',8,10,8,10,$n$*And it came to pass in the beginning of the thirty-third jubilee that they divided the earth into three parts, for Shem and Ham and Japheth, according to the inheritance of each, in the first year in the first week, when one of us, who had been sent, was with them.* (Jubilees 8:10). *And these are the names of the sons of Noah: Japheth, Ham and Shem; and children were born to them after the flood, for they had taken wives before the flood.* (Jasher 7:1). Jasher names the three sons and says children were born to them after the flood; Jubilees picks up once those families have grown and shows the earth divided into three parts, one for each son, by inheritance. The division was not a scramble for land; one who had been sent, an angel, was present with them as they did it. The brothers of Jasher 7 become the heads of the three portions of the whole earth. What Jasher begins as a family list, Jubilees finishes as a map.$n$,$n$And it came to pass in the beginning of the thirty-third jubilee that they divided the earth into three parts, for Shem and Ham and Japheth, according to the inheritance of each, in the first year in the first week, when one of us, who had been sent, was with them.$n$,true),
(29214,'T',8,8,8,8,$n$*And in the sixth year thereof, she bare him a son, and he called his name Peleg; for in the days when he was born the children of Noah began to divide the earth amongst themselves: for this reason he called his name Peleg.* (Jubilees 8:8). *These are the generations of Shem; Shem begat Arpachshad and Arpachshad begat Shelach, and Shelach begat Eber and to Eber were born two children, the name of one was Peleg, for in his days the sons of men were divided, and in the latter days, the earth was divided.* (Jasher 7:19). Both books explain the name the same way: Peleg means division, and he was named for what was happening in his days. Jasher says the sons of men were divided and in the latter days the earth was divided; Jubilees says the children of Noah began to divide the earth amongst themselves when he was born. The name is a date stamp. Eber named his son after a world event, and both books kept the reason.$n$,$n$And in the sixth year thereof, she bare him a son, and he called his name Peleg; for in the days when he was born the children of Noah began to divide the earth amongst themselves: for this reason he called his name Peleg.$n$,true),
(29219,'T',10,19,10,19,$n$*For they departed from the land of Ararat eastward to Shinar; for in his days they built the city and the tower, saying, “Go to, let us ascend thereby into heaven.”* (Jubilees 10:19). *And whilst he was reigning according to his heart’s desire, after having conquered all his enemies around, he advised with his counselors to build a city for his palace, and they did so.* (Jasher 7:42). Jasher gives the man: Nimrod, reigning after his heart's desire, advised by his counselors to build a city for his palace. Jubilees gives the place and the boast: they left Ararat eastward to Shinar and built the city and the tower to climb into heaven by it. Put together, the tower was a king's project with a king's ambition, not a village's. "In his days" in Jubilees is Nimrod's days, and Jasher tells you who he was.$n$,$n$For they departed from the land of Ararat eastward to Shinar; for in his days they built the city and the tower, saying, “Go to, let us ascend thereby into heaven.”$n$,true),
(29225,'T',12,4,12,4,$n$Jubilees 12:4–5 — *Worship the Elohim (God) of heaven, Who causes the rain and the dew to descend on the earth, And does everything upon the earth... Why do you worship things that have no spirit in them?* Jubilees gives the same father — an idolater Abram must rebuke — that Jasher shows bargaining with Nimrod.$n$,$n$Worship the Elohim (God) of heaven, Who causes the rain and the dew to descend on the earth, And does everything upon the earth, And has created everything by His word, And all life is from before His face.$n$,false),
(29441,'T',9,3,9,3,$n$*And for Asshur came forth the second portion, all the land of Asshur and Nineveh and Shinar and to the border of India, and it ascends and skirts the river.* (Jubilees 9:3). *And Ashur son of Shem and his children and household went forth at that time, a very large body of them, and they went to a distant land that they found, and they met with a very extensive valley in the land that they went to, and they built themselves four cities, and they called them after their own names and occurrences.* (Jasher 10:32). Jasher tells of Asshur son of Shem going out with a large company to a distant land, finding a wide valley, and building four cities named after themselves. Jubilees had already allotted him that ground: the second portion of Shem's share, all the land of Asshur and Nineveh and Shinar to the border of India. Jasher's "distant land that they found" was not stumbled upon; it was his by lot. The walking and the allotment are the same inheritance seen from two sides.$n$,$n$And for Asshur came forth the second portion, all the land of Asshur and Nineveh and Shinar and to the border of India, and it ascends and skirts the river.$n$,true),
(29447,'T',9,1,9,1,$n$*And Ham divided amongst his sons, and the first portion came forth for Cush towards the east, and to the west of him for Mizraim, and to the west . of him for Put, and to the west of him and to the west thereof on the sea for Canaan.* (Jubilees 9:1). *And the children of Ham were Cush, Mitzraim, Phut and Canaan according to their generation and cities.* (Jasher 10:19). Jasher names Ham's four sons; Jubilees shows Ham dividing his portion among those same four, laid out from east to west: Cush to the east, then Mizraim, then Put, then Canaan on the sea. One book is the roll call and the other is the map. Canaan's lot on the sea matters later, because Canaan did not keep to it but settled in Shem's portion, and the land that bears his name was never his by right.$n$,$n$And Ham divided amongst his sons, and the first portion came forth for Cush towards the east, and to the west of him for Mizraim, and to the west . of him for Put, and to the west of him and to the west thereof on the sea for Canaan.$n$,true),
(29451,'T',9,8,9,8,$n$*And the first portion came forth for Gomer to the east from the north side to the river Tînâ; and in the north there came forth for Magog all the inner portions of the north until it reaches to the sea of Mê’at.* (Jubilees 9:8). *And these are the sons of Japheth according to their families, Gomer, Magog, Medai, Javan, Tubal, Meshech and Tiras; these are the children of Japheth according to their generations.* (Jasher 10:7). Jasher lists Japheth's seven sons; Jubilees takes the first two of that list and places them on the ground, Gomer's portion east from the north side to the river Tina, Magog's the inner north as far as the sea of Meot. The same names, now with borders. The far north was Magog's lot from the division of the earth, which is worth remembering whenever the prophets speak of Magog and the north.$n$,$n$And the first portion came forth for Gomer to the east from the north side to the river Tînâ; and in the north there came forth for Magog all the inner portions of the north until it reaches to the sea of Mê’at.$n$,true),
(29454,'T',10,25,10,25,$n$*For this reason the whole land of Shinar is called Babel, because Yahuah (God) did there confound all the language of the children of men, and from thence they were dispersed into their cities, each according to his language and his nation.* (Jubilees 10:25). *And when Yahuah (the Lord) had scattered the sons of men on account of their sin at the tower, behold they spread forth into many divisions, and all the sons of men were dispersed into the four corners of the earth.* (Jasher 10:2). Jasher opens with the result: the sons of men scattered for their sin at the tower into the four corners of the earth. Jubilees gives the means and the name: the language confounded at Shinar, so the place is called Babel, and from there they went out each to his own cities according to his language and his nation. Jasher's "many divisions" are Jubilees' languages. The scattering was judgment on the tower, and both books say so.$n$,$n$For this reason the whole land of Shinar is called Babel, because Yahuah (God) did there confound all the language of the children of men, and from thence they were dispersed into their cities, each according to his language and his nation.$n$,true),
(29459,'T',8,8,8,8,$n$*And in the sixth year thereof, she bare him a son, and he called his name Peleg; for in the days when he was born the children of Noah began to divide the earth amongst themselves: for this reason he called his name Peleg.* (Jubilees 8:8). *And Peleg the son of Eber died in those days, in the forty-eighth year of the life of Abram son of Terah, and all the days of Peleg were two hundred and thirty-nine years.* (Jasher 10:1). Jasher 10 opens with Peleg's death in Abram's forty-eighth year, at two hundred and thirty-nine years old; Jubilees gives his birth and the reason for his name. The man whose name means division was born as the earth was being divided and died in the days of the man through whom it would be gathered into one seed. Two books tell the two ends of Peleg's life. The name given at his birth was the headline of his generation.$n$,$n$And in the sixth year thereof, she bare him a son, and he called his name Peleg; for in the days when he was born the children of Noah began to divide the earth amongst themselves: for this reason he called his name Peleg.$n$,true),
(29483,'T',12,2,12,2,$n$Jubilees 12:2–4 — *What help and profit have we from those idols which you do worship, And before which you do bow yourself? For there is no spirit in them, For they are dumb forms, and a misleading of the heart. Worship them not: Worship the Elohim (God) of heaven, Who causes the rain and the dew to descend on the earth...* Abram's idol-polemic to his father, the same plea Jasher gives him to cast away the vain things and serve Yahuah (Jasher 12:68).$n$,$n$And he said, "What help and profit have we from those idols which you do worship, And before which you do bow yourself?$n$,false),
(29507,'T',12,28,12,28,$n$*And it came to pass in the seventh year of the sixth week that he spoke to his father, and informed him that he would leave Haran to go into the land of Canaan to see it and return to him.* (Jubilees 12:28). *And Terah took his son Abram and his grandson Lot, the son of Haran, and Sarai his daughter-in-law, the wife of his son Abram, and all the souls of his household and went with them from Ur Casdim to go to the land of Canaan. And when they came as far as the land of Haran they remained there, for it was exceedingly good land for pasture, and of sufficient extent for those who accompanied them.* (Jasher 13:1). Jasher tells how Terah brought the family out of Ur as far as Haran and stayed because the pasture was good. Jubilees picks up at the end of that stay: Abram speaks to his father and tells him he is going on to Canaan to see it. Haran was meant as a stop and became a settlement, so the call to Canaan had to be answered from inside a comfortable place. Both books keep the same road and the same halt on it.$n$,$n$And it came to pass in the seventh year of the sixth week that he spoke to his father, and informed him that he would leave Haran to go into the land of Canaan to see it and return to him.$n$,true),
(29515,'T',13,3,13,3,$n$*And Yahuah (God) said to him: “To you and to your seed will I give this land.”* (Jubilees 13:3). *And Yahuah (the Lord) appeared to Abram when he came to the land of Canaan, and said to him, This is the land which I gave to you and to your seed after you forever, and I will make your seed like the stars of heaven, and I will give to your seed for an inheritance all the lands which you see.* (Jasher 13:7). At Abram's entry into Canaan both books have Yahuah (God) speak the same grant: this land to you and to your seed. Jasher expands it, forever, seed like the stars of heaven, all the lands you see; Jubilees gives it in one sentence. The land was given to a bloodline before that bloodline existed. Every later argument about who holds it begins at this word to Abram, not at any treaty.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) said to him: “To you and to your seed will I give this land.”$n$,true),
(29922,'T',15,28,15,28,$n$Jubilees 15:28–29 — *And do you command the children of Yashar'el (Israel) and let them observe the sign of this covenant for their generations as an eternal ordinance, and they will not be rooted out of the land. For the command is ordained for a covenant, that they should observe it for ever among all the children of Yashar'el (Israel).* Jubilees binds the eternal sign of Jasher 17:18 on all Israel forever.$n$,$n$And do you command the children of Yashar’el (Israel) and let them observe the sign of this covenant for their generations as an eternal ordinance, and they will not be rooted out of the land.$n$,false),
(29951,'T',16,6,16,6,$n$Jubilees 16:6–7 — *And, in like manner, Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites, like to the judgment of Sodom. But Lot we saved; for Elohim (God) remembered Abraham, and sent him out from the midst of the overthrow.* Jubilees grounds Lot's deliverance, which Jasher records, in election — Elohim remembered Abraham (Jasher 19:57).$n$,$n$And, in like manner, Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites, like to the judgment of Sodom.$n$,false),
(29977,'T',16,10,16,10,$n$*And in this month Abraham moved from Hebron, and departed and dwelt between Kadesh and Shur in the mountains of Gerar.* (Jubilees 16:10). *And at that time Abraham journeyed from the plain of Mamre, and he went to the land of the Philistines, and he dwelt in Gerar; it was in the twenty-fifth year of Abraham’s being in the land of Canaan, and the hundredth year of the life of Abraham, that he came to Gerar in the land of the Philistines.* (Jasher 20:1). Both books move Abraham from the plain of Mamre to Gerar at the same point in the story. Jubilees dates it by the month and places him between Kadesh and Shur in the mountains of Gerar; Jasher dates it to his hundredth year, the twenty-fifth in Canaan, and names the land as the Philistines'. The year of Isaac's birth is the year of the move. Sarah will be taken by Abimelech there, and both books are setting the stage.$n$,$n$And in this month Abraham moved from Hebron, and departed and dwelt between Kadesh and Shur in the mountains of Gerar.$n$,true),
(29999,'T',17,17,17,17,$n$*And Yahuah (God) knew that Abraham was faithful in all his afflictions; for He had tried him through his country and with famine, and had tried him with the wealth of kings, and had tried him again through his wife, when she was torn (from him), and with circumcision, and had tried him through Ishmael and Hagar, his maid-servant, when he sent them away.* (Jubilees 17:17). *And Yahuah (the Lord) heard the word that Isaac spoke to Ishmael, and it seemed good in the sight of Yahuah (the Lord), and he thought to try Abraham in this matter.* (Jasher 22:45). Jasher explains how the test of Isaac came about: Yahuah (the Lord) heard Isaac's word to Ishmael and thought to try Abraham in this matter. Jubilees lists the trials already behind him, his country, famine, the wealth of kings, his wife torn from him, circumcision, Ishmael and Hagar sent away, and says Yahuah (God) knew he was faithful. The test on the mountain was not to discover something unknown. It was the next in a series Abraham had already been passing, and the one who tried him knew him before he tried him.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) knew that Abraham was faithful in all his afflictions; for He had tried him through his country and with famine, and had tried him with the wealth of kings, and had tried him again through his wife, when she was torn (from him), and with circumcision, and had tried him through Ishmael and Hagar, his maid-servant, when he sent them away.$n$,true),
(30010,'T',16,11,16,11,$n$*And in the middle of the fifth month he moved from thence, and dwelt at the Well of the Oath.* (Jubilees 16:11). *And Abimelech took the seven ewe lambs which Abraham had given to him, for he had also given him cattle and herds in abundance, and Abimelech swore to Abraham concerning the well, therefore he called that well Beersheba, for there they both swore concerning it.* (Jasher 22:9). Jubilees calls the place by what happened there: the Well of the Oath. Jasher tells what the oath was, Abimelech swearing to Abraham over the well, the seven ewe lambs as witness, and the name Beersheba given because both swore. Jubilees dates Abraham's move there to the middle of the fifth month; Jasher explains why the well carried a name at all. Beersheba means a sworn well, and both books are leaning on the same story.$n$,$n$And in the middle of the fifth month he moved from thence, and dwelt at the Well of the Oath.$n$,true),
(30029,'T',18,2,18,2,$n$*And He said, “Take your beloved son whom you love, (even) Isaac, and go to the high country, and offer him on one of the mountains which I will point out to you.”* (Jubilees 18:2). *At that time the word of Yahuah (the Lord) came to Abraham, and he said to him, Abraham, and he said, Here I am.* (Jasher 23:1). Jasher gives the call: the word of Yahuah (the Lord) came, "Abraham," and he answered, "Here I am." Jubilees gives what the word said: take your beloved son, Isaac, go to the high country, and offer him on a mountain I will show you. One book has the summons and the other the command. "Here I am" was spoken before Abraham knew what was going to be asked, which is the whole shape of his faith.$n$,$n$And He said, “Take your beloved son whom you love, (even) Isaac, and go to the high country, and offer him on one of the mountains which I will point out to you.”$n$,true),
(30037,'T',19,2,19,2,$n$*And in the first year of the third week of this jubilee the days of the life of Sarah were accomplished, and she died in Hebron.* (Jubilees 19:2). *And at her bier was Shem, his sons Eber and Abimelech, together with Anar, Ashcol and Mamre, and all the grandees of the land followed her bier.* (Jasher 24:14). Jubilees records Sarah's death with its date, the first year of the third week of the jubilee, at Hebron. Jasher shows who came to her bier: Shem, with Eber and Abimelech, Anar, Ashcol and Mamre, and all the grandees of the land. Shem was still alive to bury Sarah; the generation of the ark and the generation of the promise overlapped. The woman through whom the seed came was followed to her grave by the man who came through the flood.$n$,$n$And in the first year of the third week of this jubilee the days of the life of Sarah were accomplished, and she died in Hebron.$n$,true),
(30043,'T',19,8,19,8,$n$*This is the tenth trial wherewith Abraham was tried, and he was found faithful, patient in spirit.* (Jubilees 19:8). *And the life of Sarah was one hundred and twenty-seven years, and Sarah died; and Abraham rose up from before his dead to seek a burial place to bury his wife Sarah; and he went and spoke to the children of Heth, the inhabitants of the land, saying,* (Jasher 24:1). Jasher tells it plainly: Sarah died at a hundred and twenty-seven, and Abraham rose up from before his dead to seek a burial place from the children of Heth. Jubilees counts that same errand as the tenth trial, and says he was found faithful and patient in spirit. Buying a grave in a land he had been promised and did not yet own, from the men who held it, without complaint, was a test, and Jubilees says he passed it. Faith is not only on the mountain with the knife; it is also in the field with the silver.$n$,$n$This is the tenth trial wherewith Abraham was tried, and he was found faithful, patient in spirit.$n$,true),
(30526,'T',20,10,20,10,$n$Jubilees 20:10–11 — *And you will be for a blessing on the earth, And all nations of the earth will desire you, And bless your sons in my name, That they may be blessed as I am.” And he gave to Ishmael and to his sons, and to the sons of Keturah, gifts, and sent them away from Isaac his son, and he gave everything to Isaac his son.* The parallel record gives the same verdict as Jasher 25:6 — gifts to the branches, everything to Isaac.$n$,$n$And you will be for a blessing on the earth, And all nations of the earth will desire you, And bless your sons in my name, That they may be blessed as I am.”$n$,false),
(30529,'T',19,11,19,11,$n$Jubilees 19:11–12 — *And Abraham took to himself a third wife, and her name was Keturah, from among the daughters of his household servants, for Hagar had died before Sarah. And she bare him six sons, Zimram, and Jokshan, and Medan, and Midian, and Ishbak, and Shuah, in the two weeks of years.* The same Keturah-and-six-sons scene in the parallel patriarchal record beside Jasher 25:2.$n$,$n$And Abraham took to himself a third wife, and her name was Keturah, from among the daughters of his household servants, for Hagar had died before Sarah.$n$,false),
(30533,'T',19,17,19,17,$n$Jubilees 19:17–18 — *My daughter, watch over my son Jacob, For he shall be in my stead on the earth, And for a blessing in the midst of the children of men, And for the glory of the whole seed of Shem. For I know that Yahuah (God) will choose him to be a people for possession to Himself, above all peoples that are upon the face of the earth.* Where Jasher 26:28 has Abraham bless Isaac and teach Jacob the way of Yahuah, Jubilees fixes the election squarely on Jacob as the chosen seed.$n$,$n$And he said to her: "My daughter, watch over my son Jacob, For he shall be in my stead on the earth, And for a blessing in the midst of the children of men, And for the glory of the whole seed of Shem.$n$,false),
(30534,'T',22,1,22,1,$n$*And it came to pass in the first week in the forty-fourth jubilee, in the second year, that is, the year in which Abraham died, that Isaac and Ishmael came from the Well of the Oath to celebrate the feast of weeks–that is, the feast of the first-fruits of the harvest–to Abraham, their father, and Abraham rejoiced because his two sons had come.* (Jubilees 22:1). *And all the inhabitants of the land of Haran, and all the families of the house of Abraham, and all the princes and grandees, and the sons of Abraham by the concubines, all came when they heard of Abraham’s death, and they requited Abraham’s kindness, and comforted Isaac his son, and they buried Abraham in the cave which he bought from Ephron the Hittite and his children, for the possession of a burial place.* (Jasher 26:31). Jubilees shows the last feast of weeks of Abraham's life, with Isaac and Ishmael both come to him from the Well of the Oath, and the old man glad to have his two sons together. Jasher shows the funeral: all Haran, all the house, the princes, and the sons of the concubines, burying him in the cave bought from Ephron. The feast and the burial sit in the same year. Both sons were at the table before they were at the grave, and Abraham died having kept the feast.$n$,$n$And it came to pass in the first week in the forty-fourth jubilee, in the second year, that is, the year in which Abraham died, that Isaac and Ishmael came from the Well of the Oath to celebrate the feast of weeks–that is, the feast of the first-fruits of the harvest–to Abraham, their father, and Abraham rejoiced because his two sons had come.$n$,true),
(30540,'T',19,14,19,14,$n$Jubilees 19:14–15 — *And the youths grew, and Jacob learned to write; but Esau did not learn, for he was a man of the field and a hunter, and he learnt war, and all his deeds were fierce. And Abraham loved Jacob, but Isaac loved Esau.* The same growing-up of the twins that Jasher 26:17 frames as Jacob learning the way of Yahuah while Esau hunted.$n$,$n$And the youths grew, and Jacob learned to write; but Esau did not learn, for he was a man of the field and a hunter, and he learnt war, and all his deeds were fierce.$n$,false),
(30541,'T',19,13,19,13,$n$*And in the sixth week, in the second year thereof, Rebecca bare to Isaac two sons, Jacob and Esau, and Jacob was a smooth and upright man, and Esau was fierce, a man of the field, and hairy, and Jacob dwelt in tents.* (Jubilees 19:13). *And after that came his brother, and his hand took hold of Esau’s heel, therefore they called his name Jacob.* (Jasher 26:15). Jasher gives the birth: the second came out with his hand on Esau's heel, so they called him Jacob. Jubilees gives the birth and then the men the two became, Jacob smooth and upright, dwelling in tents, Esau fierce and hairy, a man of the field. The division between the brothers began at the womb and showed in how they lived. Two sons of one mother, and only one of them called upright, and the book says so before the birthright is ever sold.$n$,$n$And in the sixth week, in the second year thereof, Rebecca bare to Isaac two sons, Jacob and Esau, and Jacob was a smooth and upright man, and Esau was fierce, a man of the field, and hairy, and Jacob dwelt in tents.$n$,true),
(30640,'T',28,29,28,29,$n$Jubilees 28:29–30 — *And Jacob's possessions multiplied exceedingly, and he possessed oxen and sheep and asses and camels, and menservants and maidservants. And Laban and his sons envied Jacob.* Jubilees joins the same multiplying-and-envy Jasher 31:31-35 records.$n$,$n$And Jacob’s possessions multiplied exceedingly, and he possessed oxen and sheep and asses and camels, and menservants and maidservants.$n$,false),
(30651,'T',29,13,29,13,$n$*And he passed over the Jabbok in the ninth month, on the eleventh thereof. And on that day Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents.* (Jubilees 29:13). *And Esau hearkened to the voice of Jacob, and Esau returned with the four hundred men that were with him on their road to Seir, and Jacob and all belonging to him went that day as far as the extremity of the land of Canaan in its borders, and he remained there some time.* (Jasher 32:73). Jubilees dates the crossing of the Jabbok and puts the meeting with Esau on the same day: reconciled, and then parted, Esau to Seir and Jacob to his tents. Jasher tells the parting from the road: Esau heard Jacob's voice and turned back with his four hundred men toward Seir while Jacob went on to the edge of Canaan. Peace between the brothers did not make them one house. Esau went his way with his army and Jacob stayed a dweller in tents, and the two lines stayed two lines.$n$,$n$And he passed over the Jabbok in the ninth month, on the eleventh thereof. And on that day Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents.$n$,true),
(31356,'T',32,1,32,1,$n$*And he abode that night at Bethel, and Levi dreamed that they had ordained and made him the priest of the El Elyon (Most High) Elohim (God), him and his sons for ever; and he awoke from his sleep and blessed Yahuah (God).* (Jubilees 32:1). *At that time Yahuah (the Lord) appeared to Jacob saying, Arise, go to Bethel and remain there, and make there an altar to Yahuah (the Lord) who appears to you, who delivered you and your sons from affliction.* (Jasher 36:1). Jasher records the command to go up to Bethel and build an altar to the One who delivered him; Jubilees records what happened the night Jacob was there: Levi dreamed he had been ordained priest of the Most High, he and his sons for ever, and woke and blessed Yahuah (God). The altar Jacob was sent to build needed a priesthood, and the priesthood was given at Bethel in a dream long before Sinai named Levi. Jasher gives the summons to Bethel and Jubilees gives what Bethel produced.$n$,$n$And he abode that night at Bethel, and Levi dreamed that they had ordained and made him the priest of the El Elyon (Most High) Elohim (God), him and his sons for ever; and he awoke from his sleep and blessed Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(31358,'T',32,30,32,30,$n$*And in the night, on the twenty-third of this month, Deborah Rebecca’s nurse died, and they buried her beneath the city under the oak of the river, and he called the name of this place, “The river of Deborah,” and the oak, “The oak of the mourning of Deborah.”* (Jubilees 32:30). *And the life of Rebecca was one hundred and thirty-three years, and she died and when Jacob heard that his mother Rebecca was dead he wept bitterly for his mother, and made a great mourning for her, and for Deborah her nurse beneath the oak, and he called the name of that place Allon-bachuth.* (Jasher 36:6). Both books bury Deborah, Rebecca's nurse, under an oak and name the place for the mourning. Jubilees dates it to the twenty-third of the month and gives the names, the river of Deborah and the oak of the mourning of Deborah; Jasher calls the oak Allon-bachuth and sets it beside Jacob's weeping for his mother Rebecca, dead at a hundred and thirty-three. A nurse received a named oak and a dated grave. The books remembered the servants, not only the patriarchs.$n$,$n$And in the night, on the twenty-third of this month, Deborah Rebecca’s nurse died, and they buried her beneath the city under the oak of the river, and he called the name of this place, “The river of Deborah,” and the oak, “The oak of the mourning of Deborah.”$n$,true),
(31363,'T',32,17,32,17,$n$*And Yahuah (God) appeared to him by night and blessed him and said to him: “Your name shall not be called Jacob, but Yashar’el (Israel) shall they name your name.”* (Jubilees 32:17). *And Jacob was a hundred years old when Yahuah (the Lord) appeared to him, and blessed him and called his name Israel, and Rachel the wife of Jacob conceived in those days.* (Jasher 36:8). Jasher says Yahuah (the Lord) appeared to Jacob when he was a hundred years old, blessed him, and called his name Israel; Jubilees gives the words of the blessing: your name shall not be called Jacob, but Yashar'el (Israel) shall they name your name. The name was not won in the wrestling alone; it was spoken by Yahuah (God) at Bethel by night. A people carries that name because it was placed on one man by the mouth of the One who appeared to him.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) appeared to him by night and blessed him and said to him: “Your name shall not be called Jacob, but Yashar’el (Israel) shall they name your name.”$n$,true),
(31369,'T',33,2,33,2,$n$*And Reuben saw Bilhah, Rachel’s maid, the concubine of his father, bathing in water in a secret place, and he loved her.* (Jubilees 33:2). *And Reuben was jealous for his mother Leah on account of this, and he was filled with anger, and he rose up in his anger and went and entered the tent of Bilhah and he from there removed his father’s bed.* (Jasher 36:14). Jubilees shows the beginning: Reuben saw Bilhah bathing in a hidden place and wanted her. Jasher shows the motive and the deed: jealousy for his mother Leah, anger, and the removal of his father's bed from Bilhah's tent. One book gives the eye and the other the anger, and both end in the same sin against his father. Genesis 35:22 says it in a line; these two tell you how a firstborn lost his birthright.$n$,$n$And Reuben saw Bilhah, Rachel’s maid, the concubine of his father, bathing in water in a secret place, and he loved her.$n$,true),
(31372,'T',33,22,33,22,$n$*And these were the names of the sons of Jacob: the first-born Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Yahudah (Judah), Issachar, Zebulon, the sons of Leah; and the sons of Rachel, Joseph and Benjamin; and the sons of Bilhah, Dan and Naphtali, and the sons of Zilpah, Gad and Asher; and Dinah, the daughter of Leah, the only daughter of Jacob.* (Jubilees 33:22). *And these are the generations of Jacob who were born to him in Padan-aram, and the sons of Jacob were twelve.* (Jasher 36:16). Jasher says the sons of Jacob were twelve; Jubilees names them by their mothers, Leah's six, Rachel's two, Bilhah's two, Zilpah's two, and adds Dinah, the only daughter. The roster matters because the tribes of Yashar'el (Israel) are these twelve men and no others, and the gathering of the scattered seed is a gathering of these names. Jasher gives the count and Jubilees gives the list.$n$,$n$And these were the names of the sons of Jacob: the first-born Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Yahudah (Judah), Issachar, Zebulon, the sons of Leah; and the sons of Rachel, Joseph and Benjamin; and the sons of Bilhah, Dan and Naphtali, and the sons of Zilpah, Gad and Asher; and Dinah, the daughter of Leah, the only daughter of Jacob.$n$,true),
(31379,'T',34,6,34,6,$n$Jubilees 34:6–7 — *And he arose from his housel he and his three sons and all the servants of his father, and his own servants, and he went against them with six thousand men, who carried swords. And he slew them in the pastures of Shechem, and pursued those who fled, and he slew them with the edge of the sword, and he slew ’Arêsa and Tâphû and Sarêgân and Sêlô and ’Amânîsakîr and Gâgâ’as, and he recovered his herds.* Jubilees compresses the whole battle Jasher 37:50 narrates king by king into one rout of the Amorite kings in the pastures of Shechem.$n$,$n$And he arose from his housel he and his three sons and all the servants of his father, and his own servants, and he went against them with six thousand men, who carried swords.$n$,false),
(31409,'T',34,6,34,6,$n$Jubilees 34:6–7 — *And he arose from his housel he and his three sons and all the servants of his father, and his own servants, and he went against them with six thousand men, who carried swords. And he slew them in the pastures of Shechem, and pursued those who fled, and he slew them with the edge of the sword.* Jubilees tells the identical war of Jacob slaying the Amorite kings that Jasher 38:6 details king by king.$n$,$n$And he arose from his housel he and his three sons and all the servants of his father, and his own servants, and he went against them with six thousand men, who carried swords.$n$,false),
(32225,'T',36,21,36,21,$n$Jubilees 36:21–22 — *And Leah his wife died in the fourth year of the second week of the forty-fifth jubilee, and he buried her in the double cave near Rebecca his mother, to the left of the grave of Sarah, his father's mother. And all her sons and his sons came to mourn over Leah his wife with him, and to comfort him regarding her, for he was lamenting her.* The Jubilees retelling carries the same death and the same double cave that Jasher 41:3 places in Machpelah.$n$,$n$And Leah his wife died in the fourth year of the second week of the forty-fifth jubilee, and he buried her in the double cave near Rebecca his mother, to the left of the grave of Sarah, his father’s mother.$n$,false),
(32229,'T',34,11,34,11,$n$Jubilees 34:11–12 — *And they dealt treacherously with him, and formed a plot against him to slay him, but changing their minds, they sold him to Ishmaelite merchants, and they brought him down into Egypt, and they sold him to Potiphar, the eunuch of Pharaoh, the chief of the cooks, priest of the city of 'Êlêw. And the sons of Jacob slaughtered a kid, and dipped the coat of Joseph in the blood, and sent (it) to Jacob their father on the tenth of the seventh month.* The same patriarchal sale Jasher 42:19 records — plot, change of mind, sold to the Ishmaelites down into Egypt.$n$,$n$And they dealt treacherously with him, and formed a plot against him to slay him, but changing their minds, they sold him to Ishmaelite merchants, and they brought him down into Egypt, and they sold him to Potiphar, the eunuch of Pharaoh, the chief of the cooks, priest of the city of ’Êlêw.$n$,false),
(32259,'T',34,11,34,11,$n$*And they dealt treacherously with him, and formed a plot against him to slay him, but changing their minds, they sold him to Ishmaelite merchants, and they brought him down into Egypt, and they sold him to Potiphar, the eunuch of Pharaoh, the chief of the cooks, priest of the city of ’Êlêw. And the sons of Jacob slaughtered a kid, and dipped the coat of Joseph in the blood, and sent (it) to Jacob their father on the tenth of the seventh month.* (Jubilees 34:11–12). *And they hastened and took Joseph’s coat and tore it, and they killed a kid of the goats and dipped the coat in the blood of the kid, and then trampled it in the dust, and they sent the coat to their father Jacob by the hand of Naphtali, and they commanded him to say these words:* (Jasher 43:13). Jubilees folds the whole betrayal into two breaths: the plot to kill him, the change of heart, the sale to the Ishmaelites, the house of Potiphar, and then the kid slaughtered and the coat dipped in its blood and sent to Jacob. Jasher opens that second moment wider, the coat torn, the blood of the kid, the coat trampled in the dust and carried to their father by the hand of Naphtali with words the brothers put in his mouth. Both witnesses agree on the order: the sale came first, and the blood on the coat was a lie told afterward to cover it. Jubilees even dates the lie, the tenth of the seventh month, and goes on to say that this is why Yashar'el (Israel) afflicts itself on that day each year; the day a kid's blood was sent to deceive a father became the day of atonement.$n$,$n$And they dealt treacherously with him, and formed a plot against him to slay him, but changing their minds, they sold him to Ishmaelite merchants, and they brought him down into Egypt, and they sold him to Potiphar, the eunuch of Pharaoh, the chief of the cooks, priest of the city of ’Êlêw.$n$,true),
(32268,'T',39,11,39,11,$n$*And the Egyptian saw the garment of Joseph and the broken door, and heard the words of his wife, and cast Joseph into prison into the place where the prisoners were kept whom the king imprisoned. And he was there in the prison; and Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour in the sight of the chief of the prison guards and compassion before him, for he saw that Yahuah (God) was with him, and that Yahuah (God) made all that he did to prosper.* (Jubilees 39:11–12). *And Potiphar heard their words, and he placed him in the prison house, the place where the king’s prisoners are confined, and Joseph was in the house of confinement twelve years.* (Jasher 44:76). Both witnesses put Joseph in the same place, not a common jail but the prison where the king's own prisoners were kept, and Jasher adds that he was there twelve years. Jubilees adds what Jasher's one line does not, that Yahuah (God) was with him inside the prison, giving him favour with the chief of the guards and prospering everything he did. A master's anger chose the place, yet it was the one prison where Pharaoh's butler and baker would be sent, the only prison from which a road ran to the throne. Twelve years is a long time to be favoured in a cell, but the favour inside the walls was the same favour that would open them.$n$,$n$And the Egyptian saw the garment of Joseph and the broken door, and heard the words of his wife, and cast Joseph into prison into the place where the prisoners were kept whom the king imprisoned.$n$,true),
(32275,'T',39,6,39,6,$n$*But he did not surrender his soul, and he remembered Yahuah (God) and the words which Jacob, his father, used to read from amongst the words of Abraham, that no man should commit fornication with a woman who has a husband; that for him the punishment of death has been ordained in the heavens before the El Elyon (Most High) Elohim (God), and the sin will be recorded against him in the eternal books continually before Yahuah (God). And Joseph remembered these words and refused to lie with her.* (Jubilees 39:6–7). *And Joseph answered her, saying, It is better for me to remain in this house than to hearken to your words, to sin against Elohim; and she said to him, If you will not perform my wish, I will pluck out your eyes, add fetters to your feet, and will deliver you into the hands of them whom you did not know before.* (Jasher 44:78). Joseph's refusal did not come out of nowhere. Jubilees says he remembered the words of Abraham that his father Jacob used to read aloud, that a man who takes another man's wife has a death sentence written against him in heaven and a sin recorded in the eternal books, and because he remembered he refused. Jasher gives the same refusal in Joseph's own mouth, that he would rather stay in the house than sin against Elohim (God), and it cost him the threat of his eyes and fetters. Adultery was sin in Egypt long before there was a mountain called Sinai, because the commandment was older than the stone it was later written on. Joseph kept Torah because his fathers had kept it and read it to him.$n$,$n$But he did not surrender his soul, and he remembered Yahuah (God) and the words which Jacob, his father, used to read from amongst the words of Abraham, that no man should commit fornication with a woman who has a husband; that for him the punishment of death has been ordained in the heavens before the El Elyon (Most High) Elohim (God), and the sin will be recorded against him in the eternal books continually before Yahuah (God).$n$,true),
(32280,'T',39,3,39,3,$n$*And he set Joseph over all his house, and the blessing of Yahuah (God) came upon the house of the Egyptian on account of Joseph, and Yahuah (God) prospered him in all that he did.* (Jubilees 39:3). *And Potiphar took Joseph and brought him to his house that he might serve him, and Joseph found favor in the sight of Potiphar, and he placed confidence in him, and made him overseer over his house, and all that belonged to him he delivered over into his hand.* (Jasher 44:11). Jasher tells how Joseph came into Potiphar's house and found favour there until he was made overseer and everything was put in his hand. Jubilees tells what that meant for the house: the blessing of Yahuah (God) came upon the Egyptian's household on Joseph's account, and everything Potiphar did prospered. A slave sold out of Canaan carried the blessing of Abraham's house into a stranger's house, which is the promise to Abraham working in miniature, that in his seed the families of the earth would be blessed. Potiphar was blessed for the sake of the man he owned.$n$,$n$And he set Joseph over all his house, and the blessing of Yahuah (God) came upon the house of the Egyptian on account of Joseph, and Yahuah (God) prospered him in all that he did.$n$,true),
(32288,'T',41,19,41,19,$n$*And Yahudah (Judah) acknowledged, and said: “Tamar is more righteous than I am. And therefore let them burn her not.”* (Jubilees 41:19). *And Tamar rose up and put off the garments of her widowhood, and she put a vail upon her, and she entirely covered herself, and she went and sat in the public thoroughfare, which is upon the road to Timnah.* (Jasher 45:31). These two verses are the beginning and the end of one story. Jasher shows Tamar putting off her widow's garments, veiling herself and sitting on the road to Timnah; Jubilees shows Yahudah (Judah) at the end of it, confessing that she is more righteous than he and refusing to let her be burned. She was not on that road for harlotry. The seed of her dead husband's house was owed to her, Yahudah (Judah) had withheld Shelah, and she went and claimed what Torah gave her by the only door left open. When he saw the pledge he owned it, and the line that runs to Messiah (Christ) passes through the son she bore him.$n$,$n$And Yahudah (Judah) acknowledged, and said: “Tamar is more righteous than I am. And therefore let them burn her not.”$n$,true),
(32292,'T',41,5,41,5,$n$*And Onan knew that the seed would not be his, (but) his brother’s only, and he went into the house of his brother’s wife, and spilt the seed on the ground, and he was wicked in the eyes of Yahuah (God), and He slew him.* (Jubilees 41:5). *And Onan took Tamar for a wife and he came to her, and Onan also did like to the work of his brother, and his work was evil in the sight of Yahuah (the Lord), and he slew him also.* (Jasher 45:26). Both witnesses say the same thing in almost the same words: Onan's work was evil in the eyes of Yahuah (God), and He slew him. Jubilees tells why it was evil. Onan knew the seed would not be counted his but his dead brother's, and he spilled it on the ground rather than raise a name for his brother. Jasher adds only that he did as his brother had done, which is to say the house of Yahudah (Judah) lost two sons to the same refusal. The judgment did not fall on lust; it fell on a man who took his brother's wife and would not give his brother a seed.$n$,$n$And Onan knew that the seed would not be his, (but) his brother’s only, and he went into the house of his brother’s wife, and spilt the seed on the ground, and he was wicked in the eyes of Yahuah (God), and He slew him.$n$,true),
(32332,'T',36,12,36,12,$n$Jubilees 36:11–12 — *I say and testify to you, my sons, according to the judgment which will come upon the man who wishes to injure his brother." And he divided all his possessions between the two on that day, and he gave the larger portion to him that was the first-born, and the tower and all that was about it, and all that Abraham possessed at the Well of the Oath.* Jubilees has Isaac himself divide the estate between the brothers, the same partition Jacob and Esau settle in Jasher 47:15-19.$n$,$n$And he divided all his possessions between the two on that day, and he gave the larger portion to him that was the first-born, and the tower and all that was about it, and all that Abraham possessed at the Well of the Oath.$n$,false),
(32350,'T',40,3,40,3,$n$*And he said before Pharaoh that his two dreams were one, and he said to him: “Seven years will come (in which there will be) plenty over all the land of Egypt, and after that seven years of famine, such a famine as has not been in all the land.* (Jubilees 40:3). *Behold the seven years that are coming there will be a great plenty throughout the land, and after that the seven years of famine will follow them, a very grievous famine; and all the plenty will be forgotten from the land, and the famine will consume the inhabitants of the land.* (Jasher 48:55). Joseph gave Pharaoh one reading for two dreams, and both witnesses carry the same reading word for word: seven years of plenty over the land, and after them seven years of famine such as had never been. Jasher's telling adds what the famine would do, that all the plenty would be forgotten and the famine would eat the inhabitants of the land. Jubilees says the two dreams were one, which is why the king needed a Hebrew and not a magician; the doubling was not two messages but one message fixed. The plenty was given so that the famine could be survived, and only one man in Egypt could see that.$n$,$n$And he said before Pharaoh that his two dreams were one, and he said to him: “Seven years will come (in which there will be) plenty over all the land of Egypt, and after that seven years of famine, such a famine as has not been in all the land.$n$,true),
(32355,'T',40,5,40,5,$n$*And Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour and mercy in the eyes of Pharaoh, and Pharaoh said to his servants: “We shall not find such a wise and discreet man as this man, for the spirit of Yahuah (God) is with him.”* (Jubilees 40:5). *And Joseph was then clothed with the spirit of Elohim before the king, and he knew all the things that would befall the king from that day forward, and he knew the proper interpretation of the king’s dream, and he spoke before the king.* (Jasher 48:52). Jasher shows the inside of the thing, Joseph clothed with the spirit of Elohim (God) before the king and knowing what would befall him from that day forward. Jubilees shows the outside, Pharaoh turning to his servants and saying out loud that the spirit of Yahuah (God) was with this man. A heathen king confessed what the court magicians could not produce. The wisdom was not Joseph's own, and Pharaoh knew it, which is why he did not look for a wiser Egyptian; there was none to find.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour and mercy in the eyes of Pharaoh, and Pharaoh said to his servants: “We shall not find such a wise and discreet man as this man, for the spirit of Yahuah (God) is with him.”$n$,true),
(32358,'T',40,2,40,2,$n$*And then the chief butler remembered Joseph and spake of him to the king, and he brought him forth from the prison, and he told his two dreams before him.* (Jubilees 40:2). *And there was with us a Hebrew servant belonging to the captain of the guard, his name was Joseph, for his master had been angry with him and placed him in the house of confinement, and he attended us there.* (Jasher 48:33). Jubilees gives the hinge in one verse, the chief butler remembering Joseph and speaking of him to the king, and Joseph brought up out of the prison. Jasher gives the butler's actual words, a Hebrew servant of the captain of the guard, put in the house of confinement by an angry master, who attended us there. The man who brought Joseph before Pharaoh was a man Joseph had served in a cell. The service he gave in confinement was the road that carried him out of it, and the one who had forgotten him was the one Yahuah (God) used to remember him.$n$,$n$And then the chief butler remembered Joseph and spake of him to the king, and he brought him forth from the prison, and he told his two dreams before him.$n$,true),
(32365,'T',40,1,40,1,$n$*And in those days Pharaoh dreamed two dreams in one night concerning a famine which was to be in all the land, and he awoke from his sleep and called all the interpreters of dreams that were in Egypt, and magicians, and told them his two dreams, and they were not able to declare (them).* (Jubilees 40:1). *At that time Pharaoh king of Egypt was sitting upon his throne in the land of Egypt, and lay in his bed and dreamed dreams, and Pharaoh saw in his dream that he was standing by the side of the river of Egypt.* (Jasher 48:2). Jasher sets the scene, Pharaoh on his bed in the land of Egypt, dreaming that he stood by the river. Jubilees tells what the dreams were about and what came of them: two dreams in one night concerning a famine, and all the interpreters and magicians of Egypt called in and unable to say what they meant. Yahuah (God) spoke to a heathen king in his sleep and shut the meaning from every wise man in his court, so that the only door left open was a Hebrew slave in the king's prison. The famine was coming either way; the dream was mercy, and the silence of the magicians was the thing that got Joseph out.$n$,$n$And in those days Pharaoh dreamed two dreams in one night concerning a famine which was to be in all the land, and he awoke from his sleep and called all the interpreters of dreams that were in Egypt, and magicians, and told them his two dreams, and they were not able to declare (them).$n$,true),
(33300,'T',40,10,40,10,$n$*And the king called Joseph’s name Sĕphânṭîphâns, and gave Joseph to wife the daughter of Potiphar, the daughter of the priest of Heliopolis, the chief cook.* (Jubilees 40:10). *And your name no more shall be called Joseph, but Zaphnath Paaneah shall be your name; you shall be second to me, and according to your word shall be all the affairs of my government, and at your word shall my people go out and come in.* (Jasher 49:21). The king gives Joseph a new name, and both books record it. Jubilees writes it as Sĕphânṭîphâns and Jasher writes it as Zaphnath Paaneah; it is one Egyptian name heard through two scribes' ears, the same name Genesis carries. Jasher adds what the name meant in Pharaoh's mouth: second to me, every affair of the government at your word, the people going out and coming in at your word. Jubilees adds the wife, the priest's daughter from Heliopolis. The two books do not compete; each keeps a piece of the same afternoon in Pharaoh's court.$n$,$n$And the king called Joseph’s name Sĕphânṭîphâns, and gave Joseph to wife the daughter of Potiphar, the daughter of the priest of Heliopolis, the chief cook.$n$,true),
(33322,'T',40,8,40,8,$n$*And Joseph ruled over all the land of Egypt, and all the princes of Pharaoh, and all his servants, and all who did the king’s business loved him, for he walked in uprightness, for he was without pride and arrogance, and he had no respect of persons, and did not accept gifts, but he judged in uprightness all the people of the land.* (Jubilees 40:8). *And at the revolution of the year, in the second year of Joseph’s reigning over Egypt, Yahuah (the Lord) gave great plenty throughout the land for seven years as Joseph had spoken, for Yahuah (the Lord) blessed all the produce of the earth in those days for seven years, and they ate and were greatly satisfied.* (Jasher 50:7). Jasher tells of the second year of Joseph's reign, when Yahuah (the Lord) gave the great plenty for seven years exactly as Joseph had spoken. Jubilees tells what kind of man was reigning while the plenty came: one who walked in uprightness, without pride, with no respect of persons, who took no gifts and judged all the people of the land fairly. That is the judge Torah later requires of Yashar'el (Israel), and here he is, a Hebrew ruling Egypt. The land was fed under a ruler who could not be bought, and the princes and servants loved him for it.$n$,$n$And Joseph ruled over all the land of Egypt, and all the princes of Pharaoh, and all his servants, and all who did the king’s business loved him, for he walked in uprightness, for he was without pride and arrogance, and he had no respect of persons, and did not accept gifts, but he judged in uprightness all the people of the land.$n$,true),
(33331,'T',42,6,42,6,$n$Jubilees 42:6–7 — *And after that he set them free again, and detained Simeon alone and sent off his nine brothers. And he filled their sacks with corn, and he put their gold in their sacks, and they did not know.* The live Jubilees layer keeps Simeon detained and the gold secretly restored, as Jasher 51:37-45.$n$,$n$And after that he set them free again, and detained Simeon alone and sent off his nine brothers.$n$,false),
(33342,'T',42,4,42,4,$n$*(Now the famine was very sore in the land of Canaan), and Jacob heard that there was food in Egypt, and he sent his ten sons that they should procure food for him in Egypt; but Benjamin he did not send, and (the ten sons of Jacob) arrived (in Egypt) among those that went (there.)* (Jubilees 42:4). *And Jacob afterward heard that there was corn in Egypt, and he called to his sons to go to Egypt to buy corn, for upon them also did the famine prevail, and he called to his sons, saying,* (Jasher 51:1). Both witnesses put the same scene at the door of the reunion: Jacob hearing that there is food in Egypt and sending his sons down to buy it, because the famine had reached Canaan too. Jubilees adds the detail the whole testing turns on, that he sent ten and kept Benjamin home. The famine that drove the ten down was the same famine Joseph had been set over, so the sons of Jacob were walking toward the brother they had sold without knowing it. Benjamin being withheld is what gave Joseph the lever he would use to find out whether they had changed.$n$,$n$(Now the famine was very sore in the land of Canaan), and Jacob heard that there was food in Egypt, and he sent his ten sons that they should procure food for him in Egypt; but Benjamin he did not send, and (the ten sons of Jacob) arrived (in Egypt) among those that went (there.)$n$,true),
(33362,'T',42,11,42,11,$n$Jubilees 42:11–12 — *And he said: “My son will not go down with you lest perchance he fall sick; for their mother gave birth to two sons, and one has perished, and this one also you will take from me. If perchance he took a fever on the road, you would bring down my old age with sorrow to death.” For he saw that their money had been returned to every man in his sack, and for this reason he feared to send him.* Jasher 52:4 carries Jacob's same fear of losing the last son of Rachel.$n$,$n$And he said: “My son will not go down with you lest perchance he fall sick; for their mother gave birth to two sons, and one has perished, and this one also you will take from me. If perchance he took a fever on the road, you would bring down my old age with sorrow to death.”$n$,false),
(33396,'T',43,14,43,14,$n$*And Joseph saw that they were all accordant in goodness one with another, and he could not refrain himself, and he told them that he was Joseph.* (Jubilees 43:14). *And Joseph observed all their acts, and said to them, Why do you look here and there? I am Joseph whom you sold to Egypt, now therefore let it not grieve you that you sold me, for as a support during the famine did Elohim send me before you.* (Jasher 54:69). Jubilees tells exactly when Joseph broke: when he saw that his brothers were all of one accord in goodness toward one another, he could not hold himself and told them who he was. Jasher gives the words, I am Joseph whom you sold to Egypt, and the comfort that came with them, that Elohim (God) had sent him ahead of them to keep them alive through the famine. He did not reveal himself to the men who had sold him; he waited until he saw them stand together for Benjamin, which was the opposite of what they had done to him. The test was whether they had changed, and when they had, the hidden brother showed his face.$n$,$n$And Joseph saw that they were all accordant in goodness one with another, and he could not refrain himself, and he told them that he was Joseph.$n$,true),
(33400,'T',43,13,43,13,$n$*Now rather let me, your servant, abide instead of the boy as a bondsman to my lord, and let the lad go with his brethren, for I became surety for him at the hand of your servant our father, and if I do not bring him back, your servant will bear the blame to our father for ever."* (Jubilees 43:13). *And Judah answered Joseph, saying, Surely you must know that I was security for the lad to his father, saying, If I brought him not to him I should bear his blame forever.* (Jasher 54:57). Jasher and Jubilees carry Yahudah's (Judah's) plea almost word for word: he had become surety for the lad to his father, and if he did not bring him back he would bear the blame for ever. Jubilees adds the offer that goes with it, let me stay as a bondsman in the boy's place and let the lad go home with his brothers. The brother who once proposed selling Joseph now offers himself as a slave so that Rachel's other son can go free. That is the change Joseph was waiting to see, and it is why the next thing in the story is Joseph weeping and telling them who he is.$n$,$n$Now rather let me, your servant, abide instead of the boy as a bondsman to my lord, and let the lad go with his brethren, for I became surety for him at the hand of your servant our father, and if I do not bring him back, your servant will bear the blame to our father for ever."$n$,true),
(33408,'T',45,12,45,12,$n$Jubilees 45:11–12 — *And this was the first year of the fourth week of the forty-fifth jubilee. And Joseph took of the corn of the harvest the fifth part for the king and left four parts for them for food and for seed, and Joseph made it an ordinance for the land of Egypt until this day.* Jubilees keeps the same fifth-part ordinance behind Joseph's gathering in Jasher 55:30.$n$,$n$And Joseph took of the corn of the harvest the fifth part for the king and left four parts for them for food and for seed, and Joseph made it an ordinance for the land of Egypt until this day.$n$,false),
(33410,'T',45,6,45,6,$n$Jubilees 45:6–7 — *And Joseph had given to his father and to his brothers as a gift the right of dwelling in the land of Goshen and in Rameses and all the region round about, which he ruled over before Pharaoh. And Yashar'el (Israel) and his sons dwelt in the land of Goshen, the best part of the land of Egypt; and Yashar'el (Israel) was one hundred and thirty years old when he came into Egypt, And Joseph nourished his father and his brethren and also their possessions with bread as much as sufficed them for the seven years of the famine.* Jubilees carries the same Goshen grant and the same hundred-and-thirtieth year Jasher 55:26 records.$n$,$n$And Joseph had given to his father and to his brothers as a gift the right of dwelling in the land of Goshen and in Rameses and all the region round about, which he ruled over before Pharaoh. And Yashar’el (Israel) and his sons dwelt in the land of Goshen, the best part of the land of Egypt; and Yashar’el (Israel) was one hundred and thirty years old when he came into Egypt,$n$,false),
(34519,'T',38,14,38,14,$n$Jubilees 38:14–15 — *And the sons of Edom have not got quit of the yoke of servitude which the twelve sons of Jacob had imposed on them until this day. And these are the kings that reigned in Edom before there reigned any king over the children of Yashar’el (Israel) until this day in the land of Edom.* Jasher 57:40's vow to take a king 'from a strange land to this day' frames the same Edomite king-list Jubilees opens here.$n$,$n$And the sons of Edom have not got quit of the yoke of servitude which the twelve sons of Jacob had imposed on them until this day.$n$,false),
(34520,'T',38,16,38,16,$n$*And Bâlâq, the son of Beor, reigned in Edom, and the name of his city was Danâbâ.* (Jubilees 38:16). *And all the children of Esau took him and anointed him and they crowned him for a king, and they bowed down to him, and they said to him, May the king live, may the king live.* (Jasher 57:42). Jubilees gives the name and the city, Balaq the son of Beor reigning in Edom from Danaba. Jasher shows the crowning itself, the children of Esau anointing a king and bowing to him and shouting that the king should live. Edom had a throne and a shout of loyalty while the sons of Jacob were still a family of shepherds, and long before Yashar'el (Israel) asked for a king of its own. The elder brother built a kingdom first; it did not make him the heir.$n$,$n$And Bâlâq, the son of Beor, reigned in Edom, and the name of his city was Danâbâ.$n$,true),
(34523,'T',38,10,38,10,$n$*And the sons of Jacob pressed hard upon the sons of Esau in the mountains of Seir, and bowed their necks so that they became servants of the sons of Jacob.* (Jubilees 38:10). *And it came to pass after the battle, the children of Esau turned back and came home to Seir, and the children of Esau slew those who had remained in the land of the children of Seir; they slew also their wives and little ones, they left not a soul alive except fifty young lads and damsels whom they suffered to live, and the children of Esau did not put them to death, and the lads became their slaves, and the damsels they took for wives.* (Jasher 57:35). These are two layers of the same mountain. Jubilees shows the sons of Jacob pressing the sons of Esau hard in Seir and bowing their necks until they served Jacob's sons. Jasher shows what the sons of Esau did when they came home from battle to Seir: they slaughtered the Horites who were left, wives and little ones, and kept only fifty lads and damsels, the lads as slaves and the damsels as wives. The ones made servants of Jacob turned and made servants of others, and the land of Seir changed hands by the sword twice over. Esau's house was always strong against the weak and bowed before the brother it hated.$n$,$n$And the sons of Jacob pressed hard upon the sons of Esau in the mountains of Seir, and bowed their necks so that they became servants of the sons of Jacob.$n$,true),
(34532,'T',38,8,38,8,$n$*And Simeon and Benjamin and Enoch, Reuben’s son, went forth on the west side of the tower, and fifty (men) with them, and they slew of Edom and of the Horites four hundred men, stout warriors; and six hundred fled, and four of the sons of Esau fled with them, and left their father lying slain, as he had fallen on the hill which is in ’Adûrâm.* (Jubilees 38:8). *And the battle was heavy between them, and the sons of Esau were smitten before the sons of Jacob, and the sons of Jacob slew of the sons of Esau eighty men, and not one died of the people of the sons of Jacob; and the hand of Joseph prevailed over all the people of the sons of Esau, and he took Zepho, the son of Eliphaz, the son of Esau, and fifty of his men captive, and he bound them with chains of iron, and gave them into the hand of his servants to bring them to Egypt.* (Jasher 57:2). Both witnesses tell of the sons of Jacob prevailing over the sons of Esau in the country of Seir, but they are not the same battle. Jubilees tells the one in which Esau himself fell on the hill at Aduram, Simeon and Benjamin and Reuben's son Enoch going out from the west side of the tower and slaying four hundred of Edom and the Horites. Jasher tells the later war, when Joseph's hand prevailed, eighty of Esau's sons fell and not one of Jacob's, and Zepho was taken in iron chains to Egypt. The feud did not end with Esau's death; his grandchildren came back for more and were beaten again. The enmity between the two brothers' houses outlived both brothers.$n$,$n$And Simeon and Benjamin and Enoch, Reuben’s son, went forth on the west side of the tower, and fifty (men) with them, and they slew of Edom and of the Horites four hundred men, stout warriors; and six hundred fled, and four of the sons of Esau fled with them, and left their father lying slain, as he had fallen on the hill which is in ’Adûrâm.$n$,true),
(34588,'T',46,1,46,1,$n$*And it came to pass that after Jacob died the children of Yashar’el (Israel) multiplied in the land of Egypt, and they became a great nation, and they were of one accord in heart, so that brother loved brother and every man helped his brother, and they increased abundantly and multiplied exceedingly, ten weeks of years, all the days of the life of Joseph.* (Jubilees 46:1). *In that year, being the seventy-ninth year of the Israelites going down to Egypt, died Reuben the son of Jacob, in the land of Egypt; Reuben was a hundred and twenty-five years old when he died, and they put him into a coffin, and he was given into the hands of his children.* (Jasher 62:1). Jasher counts this season by funerals, and here it is Reuben, dead at a hundred and twenty-five in the seventy-ninth year of the going down, put into a coffin and left in his children's keeping. Jubilees counts the same season by the living: after Jacob died the children of Yashar'el (Israel) multiplied in Egypt and became a great nation, brother loving brother, for ten weeks of years, all the days of Joseph. The fathers were being laid in coffins one by one while the seed grew into a people in a land that was not theirs. Both things were happening at once, and both had been told to Abram beforehand.$n$,$n$And it came to pass that after Jacob died the children of Yashar’el (Israel) multiplied in the land of Egypt, and they became a great nation, and they were of one accord in heart, so that brother loved brother and every man helped his brother, and they increased abundantly and multiplied exceedingly, ten weeks of years, all the days of the life of Joseph.$n$,true),
(34595,'T',46,8,46,8,$n$*And Joseph died in the forty-sixth jubilee, in the sixth week, in the second year, and they buried him in the land of Egypt, and his brethren died after him.* (Jubilees 46:8). *And it came to pass at that time that Judah the son of Jacob died in Egypt, in the eighty-sixth year of Jacob’s going down to Egypt, and Judah was a hundred and twenty-nine years old at his death, and they embalmed him and put him into a coffin, and he was given into the hands of his children.* (Jasher 62:23). Jubilees says it in one breath: Joseph died and was buried in Egypt, and his brethren died after him. Jasher spreads that one breath across a roll of dated deaths, and this entry is Yahudah (Judah), a hundred and twenty-nine years old in the eighty-sixth year of the going down, embalmed and put into a coffin and given to his children. The embalming and the coffin were not Egyptian vanity. The fathers were being kept, not buried, because the family knew they were going home and the bones were going with them.$n$,$n$And Joseph died in the forty-sixth jubilee, in the sixth week, in the second year, and they buried him in the land of Egypt, and his brethren died after him.$n$,true),
(34627,'T',46,8,46,8,$n$*And Joseph died in the forty-sixth jubilee, in the sixth week, in the second year, and they buried him in the land of Egypt, and his brethren died after him.* (Jubilees 46:8). *For Zepho heard that the mighty men of Egypt were dead and that Joseph and his brethren the sons at Jacob were dead, and that all their children the children of Israel remained in Egypt.* (Jasher 64:8). The death of Joseph and his brethren was not only a grief inside Yashar'el (Israel); it was a signal outside it. Jasher shows Zepho, Esau's grandson, hearing that the mighty men of Egypt were dead and that Joseph and his brethren were dead, and that only their children remained, and that is when he moved. Jubilees gives the deaths plainly, Joseph in the forty-sixth jubilee and his brethren after him. Enemies wait for the generation of the fathers to pass before they come against the children, and the king who knew not Joseph was of the same mind as Zepho.$n$,$n$And Joseph died in the forty-sixth jubilee, in the sixth week, in the second year, and they buried him in the land of Egypt, and his brethren died after him.$n$,true),
(35824,'T',46,14,46,14,$n$*And he set over them taskmasters to afflict them with slavery; and they built strong cities for Pharaoh, Pithom and Raamses, and they built all the walls and all the fortifications which had fallen in the cities of Egypt.* (Jubilees 46:14). *Behold in the land are Pithom and Rameses, cities unfortified against battle, it behooves you and us to build them, and to fortify them.* (Jasher 65:8). Jasher records the counsel that proposed the work: Pithom and Rameses stood unfortified against battle, and the Egyptians said it behooved them to build and fortify. Jubilees records the work itself: taskmasters set over the children of Yashar'el (Israel), and the strong cities Pithom and Raamses built for Pharaoh, with all the walls and fortifications that had fallen. The one book gives the plan and the other gives the labour, and the same two cities stand at the centre of both. The fortifying Jasher's counsellors called for is the fortification Jubilees says Yashar'el (Israel) raised with their own hands under the taskmasters.$n$,$n$And he set over them taskmasters to afflict them with slavery; and they built strong cities for Pharaoh, Pithom and Raamses, and they built all the walls and all the fortifications which had fallen in the cities of Egypt.$n$,true),
(35854,'T',46,15,46,15,$n$*And they made them serve with rigour, and the more they dealt evilly with them, the more they increased and multiplied.* (Jubilees 46:15). *And when they ploughed they were unable to hurt the infants of the children of Israel, so the people increased and waxed exceedingly.* (Jasher 67:59). Jubilees gives the rule: the more Egypt dealt evilly with them, the more they increased and multiplied. Jasher gives the strangest proof of it, the Egyptians ploughing the ground where the Hebrew women had given birth and finding they could not harm the infants, so the people grew all the more. Rigour, ploughing, and later the river, and every one of them a failure. What was meant to cut off the seed became the thing under which it multiplied, because the one who had promised Abraham a seed like the sand of the sea was the one keeping count.$n$,$n$And they made them serve with rigour, and the more they dealt evilly with them, the more they increased and multiplied.$n$,true),
(35863,'T',47,2,47,2,$n$*And Pharaoh, king of Egypt, issued a command regarding them that they should cast all their male children which were born into the river.* (Jubilees 47:2). *If it please the king let him order all their children which shall be born from this day forward, to be thrown into the water, for by this can you wipe away their name, for none of them, nor of their fathers, were tried in this manner.* (Jasher 67:49). Jubilees gives the decree, Pharaoh commanding that every male child born to them be thrown into the river. Jasher gives where the decree came from: Balaam counselling the king to throw the children into the water, and saying the aim out loud, by this you can wipe away their name. The war was never only about numbers; it was against the name of the seed. The same Balaam would later be hired to curse that seed in the wilderness, and here he is already at work before Moses was born. The order came from a prophet for hire before it came from a king.$n$,$n$And Pharaoh, king of Egypt, issued a command regarding them that they should cast all their male children which were born into the river.$n$,true),
(35877,'T',47,5,47,5,$n$*And in those days Tharmuth, the daughter of Pharaoh, came to bathe in the river, and she heard your voice crying, and she told her maidens to bring you forth, and they brought you to her.* (Jubilees 47:5). *And Bathia lifted up her eyes to the river, and she saw the ark upon the water, and sent her maid to fetch it.* (Jasher 68:18). Jubilees calls her Tharmuth and Jasher calls her Bathia, but both set the same scene: Pharaoh's daughter at the river, seeing or hearing the child, and sending her maid to bring it up out of the water. The river was the decree; the rescue came out of the very river, and by the hand of Pharaoh's own house. Jubilees tells it to Moses in the second person, she heard your voice crying, because this is the Angel reciting Moses' own life back to him. The king's daughter undid the king's command without knowing it.$n$,$n$And in those days Tharmuth, the daughter of Pharaoh, came to bathe in the river, and she heard your voice crying, and she told her maidens to bring you forth, and they brought you to her.$n$,true),
(35889,'T',47,2,47,2,$n$*And Pharaoh, king of Egypt, issued a command regarding them that they should cast all their male children which were born into the river.* (Jubilees 47:2). *And Pharaoh sent his officers to take the children and slay them; thus did the Egyptians to the Hebrew women all the days.* (Jasher 68:11). Jubilees gives the decree; Jasher shows it in force, Pharaoh's officers sent out to take the children and slay them, and the note that this was done to the Hebrew women all the days. The command was not a single bad night. It was a standing order carried out every day, and that is the Egypt Moses was born into. His mother's ark on the water was an answer to a decree that had been running for years, and it had to be hidden because the officers were still coming.$n$,$n$And Pharaoh, king of Egypt, issued a command regarding them that they should cast all their male children which were born into the river.$n$,true),
(35915,'T',47,9,47,9,$n$*And afterwards, when you were grown up, they brought you to the daughter of Pharaoh, and you did become her son, and Amram your father taught you writing, and after you had completed three weeks they brought you into the royal court.* (Jubilees 47:9). *And when Moses grew up in the king’s house, Bathia the daughter of Pharaoh considered him as a son, and all the household of Pharaoh honored him, and all the men of Egypt were afraid of him.* (Jasher 70:33). Jasher tells it from the outside: Moses grown in the king's house, Bathia counting him as her son, the household honouring him and all Egypt afraid of him. Jubilees tells it to Moses himself, the Angel reciting his own life back to him in the second person, you became her son, and they brought you into the royal court. But Jubilees keeps one thing Jasher does not: before the court had him, Amram his father taught him writing. The Hebrew father's teaching came first, and then Pharaoh's house. He was raised in the court, but he was taught at home.$n$,$n$And afterwards, when you were grown up, they brought you to the daughter of Pharaoh, and you did become her son, and Amram your father taught you writing, and after you had completed three weeks they brought you into the royal court.$n$,true),
(35940,'T',47,11,47,11,$n$Jubilees 47:11–12 — *And on the second day you did find two of the children of Yashar'el (Israel) striving together, and you did say to him who was doing the wrong: "Why do you smite your brother? And he was angry and indignant, and said “Who made you a prince and a judge over us? Thinkest you to kill me as you killedst the Egyptian yesterday?” And you did fear and flee on account of these words.* Jubilees gives the same exchange and the flight that follows Jasher 71:8.$n$,$n$And on the second day you did find two of the children of Yashar’el (Israel) striving together, and you did say to him who was doing the wrong: "Why do you smite your brother?$n$,false),
(35953,'T',47,10,47,10,$n$*And you were three weeks of years at court until the time when you did go forth from the royal court and did see an Egyptian smiting your friend who was of the children of Yashar’el (Israel), and you did slay him and hide him in the sand.* (Jubilees 47:10). *At that time that the war and the siege were against Cush, Moses fled from Egypt from Pharaoh who sought to kill him for having slain the Egyptian.* (Jasher 72:21). Jubilees gives the deed, told to Moses as his own memory: he went out from the court, saw an Egyptian smiting his friend of the children of Yashar'el (Israel), slew him and hid him in the sand. Jasher gives what followed, Moses fleeing because Pharaoh sought to kill him for it, and sets the flight in the days of the war against Cush. The man raised in the king's house chose the beaten Hebrew over the court that raised him, and it cost him the court. Three weeks of years in Pharaoh's palace ended in a day over one afflicted brother.$n$,$n$And you were three weeks of years at court until the time when you did go forth from the royal court and did see an Egyptian smiting your friend who was of the children of Yashar’el (Israel), and you did slay him and hide him in the sand.$n$,true),
(37310,'T',14,13,14,13,$n$*And it came to pass, when the sun had set, that an ecstasy fell upon Abram, and lo! an horror of great darkness fell upon him, and it was said to Abram: “Know of a surety that your seed shall be a stranger in a land (that is) not theirs, and they will bring them into bondage, and afflict them four hundred years.* (Jubilees 14:13). *For they said the period was completed which Yahuah (the Lord) had appointed to the children of Israel in the times of old, which he had spoken to Abraham.* (Jasher 75:2). Jubilees gives the word to Abram in the darkness: his seed would be strangers in a land not theirs, brought into bondage and afflicted four hundred years. Jasher shows what happened when that word was reckoned wrongly. The children of Ephraim said the period Yahuah (the Lord) had appointed was complete, and went out of Egypt before the time, and the chapter that follows tells of their slaughter. A true word misreckoned kills as surely as a false one. The years were fixed, but they were Yahuah's (God's) to count, and the ones who counted for Him died at the hands of the Philistines.$n$,$n$And it came to pass, when the sun had set, that an ecstasy fell upon Abram, and lo! an horror of great darkness fell upon him, and it was said to Abram: “Know of a surety that your seed shall be a stranger in a land (that is) not theirs, and they will bring them into bondage, and afflict them four hundred years.$n$,true),
(37317,'T',48,7,48,7,$n$Jubilees 48:7–8 — *And everything took place according to your words; ten great and terrible judgments came on the land of Egypt that you might execute vengeance on it for Yashar'el (Israel). And Yahuah (God) did everything for Yashar'el's (Israel's) sake, and according to His covenant, which He had ordained with Abraham that He would take vengeance on them as they had brought them by force into bondage.* The bodily plague on Pharaoh in Jasher 76:34 foreshadows the ten covenant-judgments Jubilees records on Egypt.$n$,$n$And everything took place according to your words; ten great and terrible judgments came on the land of Egypt that you might execute vengeance on it for Yashar’el (Israel).$n$,false),
(37325,'T',48,1,48,1,$n$*And in the sixth year of the third week of the forty-ninth jubilee you did depart and dwell in the land of Midian five weeks and one year. And you did return into Egypt in the second week in the second year in the fiftieth jubilee.* (Jubilees 48:1). *So Moses went to Midian, for he was afraid to return to Egypt on account of Pharaoh, and he went and sat at a well of water in Midian.* (Jasher 76:13). Jasher gives the first scene of the Midian years, Moses afraid to go back to Egypt, sitting down at a well. Jubilees gives their length, five weeks of years and one year, and the date he went in and the date he came out, down to the week and the jubilee. The one who would stand before Pharaoh spent the better part of his life first as a fugitive at a well. The years are counted so carefully because none of them were wasted; the man who returned in the fiftieth jubilee was not the man who fled in the forty-ninth.$n$,$n$And in the sixth year of the third week of the forty-ninth jubilee you did depart and dwell in the land of Midian five weeks and one year. And you did return into Egypt in the second week in the second year in the fiftieth jubilee.$n$,true),
(37347,'T',48,7,48,7,$n$*And everything took place according to your words; ten great and terrible judgments came on the land of Egypt that you might execute vengeance on it for Yashar’el (Israel). And Yahuah (God) did everything for Yashar’el’s (Israel’s) sake, and according to His covenant, which He had ordained with Abraham that He would take vengeance on them as they had brought them by force into bondage.* (Jubilees 48:7–8). *And Yahuah (the Lord) heard the cries of the children of Israel, and saw the oppression with which the Egyptians oppressed them.* (Jasher 78:15). Jasher gives the hearing and the seeing: Yahuah (the Lord) heard the cries of the children of Yashar'el (Israel) and saw the oppression. Jubilees gives what came of it, ten great and terrible judgments on Egypt, and says plainly what they were for, vengeance for Yashar'el (Israel) according to the covenant Yahuah (God) had ordained with Abraham. The plagues were not random wrath against a nation; they were a Father keeping an oath sworn four hundred years earlier, that the nation which brought Abraham's seed into bondage by force would be judged for it. He heard, He saw, and then He struck, and every one of the ten came according to the word of Moses.$n$,$n$And everything took place according to your words; ten great and terrible judgments came on the land of Egypt that you might execute vengeance on it for Yashar’el (Israel).$n$,true),
(37356,'T',48,1,48,1,$n$*And in the sixth year of the third week of the forty-ninth jubilee you did depart and dwell in the land of Midian five weeks and one year. And you did return into Egypt in the second week in the second year in the fiftieth jubilee.* (Jubilees 48:1). *In those days Moses, the son of Amram, in Midian, took Zipporah, the daughter of Reuel the Midianite, for a wife.* (Jasher 78:7). Jubilees gives the Midian years in a date, from the forty-ninth jubilee to the fiftieth, five weeks of years and one year. Jasher puts a marriage inside those years, Moses taking Zipporah the daughter of Reuel the Midianite for a wife. The deliverer of Yashar'el (Israel) married outside the camp and kept sheep for a stranger before he ever lifted a rod, and the years are not written as a gap in his story. They are dated to the week because Yahuah (God) was counting them as the preparation of the man.$n$,$n$And in the sixth year of the third week of the forty-ninth jubilee you did depart and dwell in the land of Midian five weeks and one year. And you did return into Egypt in the second week in the second year in the fiftieth jubilee.$n$,true),
(37407,'T',48,7,48,7,$n$Jubilees 48:7–8 — *And everything took place according to your words; ten great and terrible judgments came on the land of Egypt that you might execute vengeance on it for Yashar'el (Israel). And Yahuah (God) did everything for Yashar'el's (Israel's) sake, and according to His covenant, which He had ordained with Abraham that He would take vengeance on them as they had brought them by force into bondage.* Jubilees counts the same ten judgments Moses tallies in Jasher 80:51.$n$,$n$And everything took place according to your words; ten great and terrible judgments came on the land of Egypt that you might execute vengeance on it for Yashar’el (Israel).$n$,false),
(38878,'T',48,12,48,12,$n$Jubilees 48:12–13 — *the prince of the Mastêmâ ... cried to the Egyptians to pursue after you with all the powers of the Egyptians, with their chariots, and with their horses ... And I stood between the Egyptians and Yashar'el (Israel), and we delivered Yashar'el (Israel) out of his hand ... and Yahuah (God) brought them through the midst of the sea as if it were dry land.* Jubilees tells the same pursuit that overtakes Israel at the sea in Jasher 81:24.$n$,$n$And notwithstanding all (these) signs and wonders the prince of the Mastêmâ was not put to shame because he took courage and cried to the Egyptians to pursue after you with all the powers of the Egyptians, with their chariots, and with their horses, and with all the hosts of the peoples of Egypt.$n$,false),
(38882,'T',48,19,48,19,$n$*And we did not lead forth the children of Yashar’el (Israel) from Egypt empty handed.* (Jubilees 48:19). *And at the end of two hundred and ten years, Yahuah (the Lord) brought forth the children of Israel from Egypt with a strong hand.* (Jasher 81:4). The we of Jubilees is the Angel of the Presence speaking, telling Moses that they did not bring the children of Yashar'el (Israel) out of Egypt empty handed. Jasher gives the same going out in its sum, two hundred and ten years ended and Yahuah (the Lord) bringing them out with a strong hand. Angels led and the strong hand of Yahuah (the Lord) drove, and the people walked out carrying the wages of their bondage. The bondage was real and the years were counted, and when the counting was done they left as a nation paid, not as slaves escaped.$n$,$n$And we did not lead forth the children of Yashar’el (Israel) from Egypt empty handed.$n$,true),
(39065,'T',46,9,46,9,$n$*And the king of Egypt went forth to war with the king of Canaan in the forty-seventh jubilee, in the second week in the second year, and the children of Yashar’el (Israel) brought forth all the bones of the children of Jacob save the bones of Joseph, and they buried them in the field in the double cave in the mountain.* (Jubilees 46:9). *And it was in those days, when the children of Israel were dwelling securely in their cities, that they buried the coffins of the tribes of their ancestors, which they had brought up from Egypt, each man in the inheritance of his children, the twelve sons of Jacob did the children of Israel bury, each man in the possession of his children.* (Jasher 90:38). Both witnesses agree the sons of Jacob did not stay in Egypt's ground; they differ on when their bones went home. Jubilees has them carried up early, while the king of Egypt was off at war with Canaan, and laid in the double cave in the mountain, all but Joseph. Jasher has the coffins carried up out of Egypt at the exodus and buried after the land had rest, each man in the inheritance of his own children. Either way the fathers were waiting for the land and were brought into it. Joseph alone, in Jubilees' telling, had to wait for the whole nation to come out with him.$n$,$n$And the king of Egypt went forth to war with the king of Canaan in the forty-seventh jubilee, in the second week in the second year, and the children of Yashar’el (Israel) brought forth all the bones of the children of Jacob save the bones of Joseph, and they buried them in the field in the double cave in the mountain.$n$,true),
(39088,'T',13,20,13,20,$n$*For all the land which you see I shall give to you and to your seed for ever, and I shall make your seed as the sand of the sea: though a man may number the dust of the earth, yet your seed shall not be numbered.* (Jubilees 13:20). *And Yahuah (the Lord) gave to the children of Israel the whole land of Canaan, as he had sworn to their ancestors, and Yahuah (the Lord) gave them rest from those around them, and the children of Israel dwelt securely in their cities.* (Jasher 91:15). Jubilees gives the promise to Abram as he stood looking at the land: all of it to him and to his seed for ever, and a seed past counting. Jasher gives the receipt generations later, Yahuah (the Lord) giving the children of Yashar'el (Israel) the whole land of Canaan as he had sworn to their ancestors, and rest from those around them. The oath was to a man and his bloodline, and it was kept to the bloodline. The word in Jubilees is for ever, which means the land grant did not expire with Joshua or with any later exile; the same seed is still the heir.$n$,$n$For all the land which you see I shall give to you and to your seed for ever, and I shall make your seed as the sand of the sea: though a man may number the dust of the earth, yet your seed shall not be numbered.$n$,true),
(39384,'T',16,6,16,6,$n$Jubilees 16:6–7 — *And, in like manner, Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites, like to the judgment of Sodom. But Lot we saved; for Elohim (God) remembered Abraham, and sent him out from the midst of the overthrow.* The now-live Jubilees self-links Wisdom's deliverance of *the righteous man* from the fire (10:6).$n$,$n$And, in like manner, Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites, like to the judgment of Sodom.$n$,false),
(45819,'T',3,21,3,21,$n$Jubilees 3:21–22 — *And when she had first covered her shame with fig-leaves, she gave thereof to Adam and he ate, and his eyes were opened, and he saw that he was naked. And he took fig-leaves and sewed (them) together, and made an apron for himself, and covered his shame.* The same primeval history records the fig-leaf covering that 1 Adam & Eve 37:2 turns away from to seek the Tree of Life.$n$,$n$And when she had first covered her shame with fig-leaves, she gave thereof to Adam and he ate, and his eyes were opened, and he saw that he was naked.$n$,false),
(45902,'T',10,8,10,8,$n$Jubilees 10:8–9 — *And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say to them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men; for these are for corruption and leading astray before my judgment, for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.” And He said: “Let the tenth part of them remain before him, and let nine parts descend into the place of condemnation.”* The same divine patience that leaves the enemy his hosts "until the end of the world" in 1-adam-eve 44:5 lets a remnant of Mastema's spirits remain in Noah's day.$n$,$n$And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say to them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men; for these are for corruption and leading astray before my judgment, for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.”$n$,false),
(45959,'T',3,22,3,22,$n$*And he took fig-leaves and sewed (them) together, and made an apron for himself, and covered his shame. And Elohim (God) cursed the serpent, and was wroth with it for ever….* (Jubilees 3:22–23). *After this Satan called to his hosts, all of which came to him, and said to him:--* (1 Adam and Eve 48:1). Jubilees moves in two lines from Adam sewing fig-leaves to the serpent cursed for ever. The First Book of Adam and Eve picks the story up outside the garden, where the same serpent, now named Satan, calls his hosts together to plan against the man he has already brought down. The curse did not end his hostility; it fixed it. The one Elohim (God) was wroth with for ever is the one still gathering his hosts against the sons of Adam, and the later book shows the war continuing where Genesis and Jubilees fall silent.$n$,$n$And he took fig-leaves and sewed (them) together, and made an apron for himself, and covered his shame.$n$,true),
(46049,'T',3,26,3,26,$n$*And He made for them coats of skin, and clothed them, and sent them forth from the Garden of Eden.* (Jubilees 3:26). *And the cherub who guarded the garden was standing at the western gate, and guarding it against Adam and Eve, lest they should suddenly come into the garden. And the cherub turned around, as if to put them to death; according to the commandment Elohim (God) had given him.* (1 Adam and Eve 54:3). Jubilees gives the sending out in one verse: coats of skin, clothed, sent forth from the garden. The First Book of Adam and Eve shows what the sending out meant afterward, a cherub standing at the western gate, guarding it against the two of them and turning as if to kill them, because that was the commandment Elohim (God) had given him. They were clothed and shut out in the same hour. The coats were mercy and the sword at the gate was judgment, and both came by commandment from the same hand. There was no sneaking back into the garden; the way back would have to be opened from the inside.$n$,$n$And He made for them coats of skin, and clothed them, and sent them forth from the Garden of Eden.$n$,true),
(46157,'T',10,8,10,8,$n$Jubilees 10:8–9 — *And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me... for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.” And He said: “Let the tenth part of them remain before him, and let nine parts descend into the place of condemnation.”* As mercy curbs Mastema to a permitted tenth here, so mercy in 1 Adam & Eve 62:3 lets the buried figs live rather than perish.$n$,$n$And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say to them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men; for these are for corruption and leading astray before my judgment, for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.”$n$,false),
(46293,'T',3,6,3,6,$n$Jubilees 3:6–7 — *And He awaked Adam out of his sleep and on awaking he rose on the sixth day, and He brought her to him, and he knew her, and said to her: “This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she will be called my wife; because she was taken from her husband.” Therefore shall man and wife be one, and therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and cleave to his wife, and they shall be one flesh.* The one-flesh bond Adam fears the devils will counterfeit by their apparitions (72:24) is the genuine union Yahuah brought Adam from his own side.$n$,$n$And He awaked Adam out of his sleep and on awaking he rose on the sixth day, and He brought her to him, and he knew her, and said to her: “This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she will be called my wife; because she was taken from her husband.”$n$,false),
(46424,'T',4,13,4,13,$n$Jubilees 4:12–13 — *He began to call on the name of Yahuah (God) on the earth. And in the seventh jubilee in the third week Enos took Nôâm his sister to be his wife, and she bare him a son in the third year of the fifth week, and he called his name Kenan.* Jubilees keeps the same memory — the calling on the Name begun in Seth's line — the worship the Conflict shows in Adam's accepted offering for Seth.$n$,$n$And in the seventh jubilee in the third week Enos took Nôâm his sister to be his wife, and she bare him a son in the third year of the fifth week, and he called his name Kenan.$n$,false),
(46495,'T',4,26,4,26,$n$Jubilees 4:25–26 — *And he burnt the incense of the sanctuary, (even) sweet spices, acceptable before Yahuah (God) on the Mount. For Yahuah (God) has four places on the earth, the Garden of Eden, and the Mount of the East, and this mountain on which you are this day, Mount Sinai, and Mount Zion (which) will be sanctified in the new creation for a sanctification of the earth; through it will the earth be sanctified from all (its) guilt and its uncleanness throughout the generations of the world.* Enoch's burnt incense on the holy mountain matches the treasured incense kept by the fathers at the Cave of Treasures in 2 Adam and Eve 7:9.$n$,$n$For Yahuah (God) has four places on the earth, the Garden of Eden, and the Mount of the East, and this mountain on which you are this day, Mount Sinai, and Mount Zion (which) will be sanctified in the new creation for a sanctification of the earth; through it will the earth be sanctified from all (its) guilt and its uncleanness throughout the generations of the world.$n$,false),
(46499,'T',4,13,4,13,$n$Jubilees 4:12–13 — *He began to call on the name of Yahuah (God) on the earth. And in the seventh jubilee in the third week Enos took Nôâm his sister to be his wife, and she bare him a son in the third year of the fifth week, and he called his name Kenan.* Jubilees marks this Enos as the one who began to call on the Name and begat Cainan, the same holy generation of 2 Adam and Eve 7:7.$n$,$n$And in the seventh jubilee in the third week Enos took Nôâm his sister to be his wife, and she bare him a son in the third year of the fifth week, and he called his name Kenan.$n$,false),
(46671,'T',4,11,4,11,$n$Jubilees 4:10–11 — *And Adam knew Eve his wife and she bare yet nine sons. And in the fifth week of the fifth jubilee Seth took 'Azûrâ his sister to be his wife, and in the fourth (year of the sixth week) she bare him Enos.* Jubilees keeps the same holy line of Seth and Enos set against Cain's city, the covenant seed Jared is told to keep separate in 2 Adam and Eve 19:4.$n$,$n$And in the fifth week of the fifth jubilee Seth took ’Azûrâ his sister to be his wife, and in the fourth (year of the sixth week) she bare him Enos.$n$,false),
(46708,'T',4,26,4,26,$n$Jubilees 4:25–26 — *And he burnt the incense of the sanctuary, (even) sweet spices, acceptable before Yahuah (God) on the Mount. For Yahuah (God) has four places on the earth, the Garden of Eden, and the Mount of the East, and this mountain on which you are this day, Mount Sinai, and Mount Zion (which) will be sanctified in the new creation for a sanctification of the earth; through it will the earth be sanctified from all (its) guilt and its uncleanness throughout the generations of the world.* The incense Jared lays up (21:7) is the same sanctuary-incense Enoch offered on the holy Mount.$n$,$n$For Yahuah (God) has four places on the earth, the Garden of Eden, and the Mount of the East, and this mountain on which you are this day, Mount Sinai, and Mount Zion (which) will be sanctified in the new creation for a sanctification of the earth; through it will the earth be sanctified from all (its) guilt and its uncleanness throughout the generations of the world.$n$,false),
(46723,'T',4,17,4,17,$n$Jubilees 4:17–18 — *And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book... And he was the first to write a testimony.* Enoch who "wrote a celebrated book" (22:2) is Jubilees' first man to write a testimony.$n$,$n$And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book, that men might know the seasons of the years according to the order of their separate months.$n$,false),
(48520,'T',15,7,15,7,$n$*Neither will your name any more be called Abram, But your name from henceforth, even for ever, shall be Abraham. For the father of many nations have I made you... And I shall make you into nations, And kings will come forth from you* (Jubilees 15:7–8). The restored witness carries the name-change and the promise entire — *your name shall be Abraham; for a father of many nations have I made thee... and kings shall come out of thee* (Genesis 17:5-6) — the same renaming and the same word of nations and kings.$n$,$n$Neither will your name any more be called Abram, But your name from henceforth, even for ever, shall be Abraham. For the father of many nations have I made you.$n$,false),
(48567,'T',16,7,16,7,$n$*And, in like manner, Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites, like to the judgment of Sodom. But Lot we saved; for Elohim (God) remembered Abraham, and sent him out from the midst of the overthrow* (Jubilees 16:6–7). The intercession of *Wilt thou also destroy the righteous with the wicked?* (Genesis 18:23) bore fruit: *Lot we saved; for Elohim remembered Abraham*. The Judge who does right does not slay the righteous with the wicked — He remembers the intercessor and draws the one righteous man out of the fire.$n$,$n$But Lot we saved; for Elohim (God) remembered Abraham, and sent him out from the midst of the overthrow.$n$,false),
(48602,'T',12,2,12,2,$n$Jubilees 12:2–4 — *And he said, "What help and profit have we from those idols which you do worship, And before which you do bow yourself? For there is no spirit in them, For they are dumb forms, and a misleading of the heart. Worship them not: Worship the Elohim (God) of heaven, Who causes the rain and the dew to descend on the earth..."* The same Abraham, in the same house of Terah's idols, declares the dumb forms have no spirit and calls his father to the living Elohim.$n$,$n$And he said, "What help and profit have we from those idols which you do worship, And before which you do bow yourself?$n$,false),
(48623,'T',12,2,12,2,$n$Jubilees 12:2–3 — *And he said, "What help and profit have we from those idols which you do worship, And before which you do bow yourself? For there is no spirit in them, For they are dumb forms, and a misleading of the heart..."* Jubilees gives the same young Abraham pleading with the same idol-keeping father over the same lifeless wares.$n$,$n$And he said, "What help and profit have we from those idols which you do worship, And before which you do bow yourself?$n$,false),
(48691,'T',12,17,12,17,$n$Jubilees 12:17–19 — *All the signs of the stars, and the signs of the moon and of the sun are all in the hand of Yahuah (God)... You alone are my Elohim (God)... And You have created all things, And all things that are are the work of Your hands.* The parallel restored record gives Abraham's own confession of the Creator that meets the "I am He" of Apocalypse of Abraham 8:3.$n$,$n$And a word came into his heart and he said: "All the signs of the stars, and the signs of the moon and of the sun are all in the hand of Yahuah (God). Why do I search (them) out?$n$,false),
(49297,'T',10,8,10,8,$n$Jubilees 10:8–9 — *And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say to them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men; for these are for corruption and leading astray before my judgment, for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.” And He said: “Let the tenth part of them remain before him, and let nine parts descend into the place of condemnation.”* The chief-of-spirits left to rule is the same Sammael with his hosts envying in the firmament in Ascension of Isaiah 7:9.$n$,$n$And the chief of the spirits, Mastêmâ, came and said: “Yahuah (God), Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say to them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men; for these are for corruption and leading astray before my judgment, for great is the wickedness of the sons of men.”$n$,false),
(49862,'T',16,12,16,12,$n$*And in the middle of the sixth month Yahuah (God) visited Sarah and did to her as He had spoken, and she conceived. And she bare a son in the third month... at the time of which Yahuah (God) had spoken to Abraham, on the festival of the first-fruits of the harvest, Isaac was born* (Jubilees 16:12–13). The restored witness carries *Yahuah visited Sarah as he had said* (Genesis 21:1) and the *set time* (Genesis 21:2) verse-for-verse — and binds the birth to an appointed time, the festival of the first-fruits of the harvest.$n$,$n$And in the middle of the sixth month Yahuah (God) visited Sarah and did to her as He had spoken, and she conceived.$n$,false),
(49868,'T',17,6,17,6,$n$*And Elohim (God) said to Abraham "Let it not be grievous in your sight, because of the child and because of the bondwoman; in all that Sarah has said to you, hearken to her words and do (them); for in Isaac shall your name and seed be called* (Jubilees 17:6). The restored witness carries the word entire — *in Isaac shall your name and seed be called* — matching *in Isaac shall thy seed be called* (Genesis 21:12), the elect line named through the son of promise.$n$,$n$And Elohim (God) said to Abraham “Let it not be grievous in your sight, because of the child and because of the bondwoman; in all that Sarah has said to you, hearken to her words and do (them); for in Isaac shall your name and seed be called.$n$,false),
(49885,'T',16,20,16,20,$n$*And he built there an altar to Yahuah (God) who had delivered him, and who was making him rejoice in the land of his sojourning, and he celebrated a festival of joy in this month seven days, near the altar which he had built at the Well of the Oath. And he built booths for himself and for his servants on this festival, and he was the first to celebrate the feast of tabernacles on the earth.* (Jubilees 16:20–21). *And Abraham planted a grove in Beer-sheba, and called there on the name of Yahuah (LORD), El Olam (the everlasting God).* (Genesis 21:33). Genesis says Abraham planted a grove at Beer-sheba and called there on the name of Yahuah (LORD), El Olam (the everlasting God). Beer-sheba is the Well of the Oath, and Jubilees tells what the calling looked like there: an altar built to the one who had delivered him, seven days of a festival of joy beside it, booths for himself and his servants, and the first feast of tabernacles ever kept on the earth. The grove and the altar and the name all belong to one appointed time. Abraham called on the everlasting name by keeping the feast, long before Sinai wrote the feast down.$n$,$n$And he built there an altar to Yahuah (God) who had delivered him, and who was making him rejoice in the land of his sojourning, and he celebrated a festival of joy in this month seven days, near the altar which he had built at the Well of the Oath.$n$,true),
(49888,'T',18,2,18,2,$n$*And Elohim (God) said to him, "Abraham, Abraham"; and he said, "Behold, (here) am I." And He said, "Take your beloved son whom you love, (even) Isaac, and go to the high country, and offer him on one of the mountains which I will point out to you."* (Jubilees 18:1–2). The restored witness keeps the call verse-for-verse with *Take now thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest... offer him there for a burnt offering upon one of the mountains* (Genesis 22:2) — *your beloved son whom you love*, the only son named for the offering.$n$,$n$And He said, “Take your beloved son whom you love, (even) Isaac, and go to the high country, and offer him on one of the mountains which I will point out to you.”$n$,false),
(49935,'T',19,21,19,21,$n$*Let your hands be strong And let your heart rejoice in your son Jacob; For I have loved him far beyond all my sons. He will be blessed for ever, And his seed will fill the whole earth. If a man can number the sand of the earth, His seed also will be numbered* (Jubilees 19:21–22). The restored witness carries the same innumerable-seed blessing on the next carrier of the line — *his seed will fill the whole earth... If a man can number the sand of the earth, His seed also will be numbered* — the very promise laid on the bride in *be thou the mother of thousands of millions, and let thy seed possess the gate of those which hate them* (Genesis 24:60), the seed beyond counting passed on through Rebekah's womb.$n$,$n$Let your hands be strong And let your heart rejoice in your son Jacob; For I have loved him far beyond all my sons. He will be blessed for ever, And his seed will fill the whole earth.$n$,false),
(50851,'T',26,23,26,23,$n$*"Behold, the smell of my son is as the smell of a (full) field which Yahuah (God) has blessed. And may Yahuah (God) give you of the dew of heaven And of the dew of the earth, and plenty of corn and oil: Let nations serve you, And peoples bow down to you."* (Jubilees 26:22–23). The restored witness keeps the blessing — *the dew of heaven And of the dew of the earth, and plenty of corn and oil* — matching *Therefore Elohim (God) give thee of the dew of heaven, and the fatness of the earth, and plenty of corn and wine* (Genesis 27:28). The bounty of heaven and earth is poured on the chosen seed.$n$,$n$And may Yahuah (God) give you of the dew of heaven And of the dew of the earth, and plenty of corn and oil: Let nations serve you, And peoples bow down to you.$n$,false),
(50880,'T',27,22,27,22,$n$*And behold, Yahuah (God) stood upon it. And He spake to Jacob and said: "I am Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) of Abraham, your father, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac; the land whereon you are sleeping, to you shall I give it, and to your-seed after you."* (Jubilees 27:21–22). The restored witness keeps the One atop the ladder who bears the Name — *Yahuah stood upon it... I am Yahuah Elohim of Abraham, your father, and the Elohim of Isaac* — matching *behold, Yahuah (LORD) stood above it, and said, I am Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) of Abraham thy father, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac* (Genesis 28:13): the Formed Yahuah who appears and speaks, renewing the land to Jacob and his seed.$n$,$n$And He spake to Jacob and said: “I am Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) of Abraham, your father, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac; the land whereon you are sleeping, to you shall I give it, and to your-seed after you.$n$,false),
(51765,'T',28,11,28,11,$n$*And Yahuah (God) opened the womb of Leah, and she conceived and bare Jacob a son, and he called his name Reuben... But the womb of Rachel was closed, for Yahuah (God) saw that Leah was hated and Rachel loved* (Jubilees 28:11–12). The restored witness keeps the turn whole — *Yahuah opened the womb of Leah... for Yahuah saw that Leah was hated* — matching *when Yahuah (LORD) saw that Leah was hated, he opened her womb: but Rachel was barren* (Genesis 29:31). The unloved wife is the one Yahuah visits first; the foundation of the tribes is laid in her affliction.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) opened the womb of Leah, and she conceived and bare Jacob a son, and he called his name Reuben, on the fourteenth day of the ninth month, in the first year of the third week.$n$,false),
(51767,'T',28,15,28,15,$n$*And again Jacob went in to Leah, and she conceived, and bare him a third son, and he called his name Levi... And again Jacob went in to her, and she conceived, and bare him a fourth son, and he called his name Yahudah (Judah)* (Jubilees 28:14–15). The restored witness carries Levi and Judah together — the priestly tribe and the kingly tribe born back to back — matching *she called his name Levi* (Genesis 29:34) and *she called his name Yahudah (Judah)* (Genesis 29:35). The two tribes that will carry the altar and the scepter come from the wife Yahuah remembered.$n$,$n$And again Jacob went in to her, and she conceived, and bare him a fourth son, and he called his name Yahudah (Judah), on the fifteenth of the third month, in the first year of the fourth week.$n$,false),
(51799,'T',28,29,28,29,$n$*And Jacob's possessions multiplied exceedingly, and he possessed oxen and sheep and asses and camels, and menservants and maidservants. And Laban and his sons envied Jacob, and Laban took back his sheep from him, and he observed him with evil intent* (Jubilees 28:29–30). The restored witness carries the increase whole — *Jacob's possessions multiplied exceedingly* — matching *the man increased exceedingly, and had much cattle, and maidservants, and menservants, and camels, and asses* (Genesis 30:43); and it names what follows, Laban's envy, the strife of ch31 already gathering.$n$,$n$And Jacob’s possessions multiplied exceedingly, and he possessed oxen and sheep and asses and camels, and menservants and maidservants.$n$,false),
(51844,'T',29,13,29,13,$n$*And Jacob sent away Laban, and he departed into Mesopotamia, the land of the East, and Jacob returned to the land of Gilead... And on that day Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents* (Jubilees 29:12–13). The restored witness names the meeting outright — *Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him* — the dread of the four hundred men of Genesis 33:1 turned to the embrace of *Esau ran to meet him, and embraced him, and fell on his neck* (Genesis 33:4).$n$,$n$And he passed over the Jabbok in the ninth month, on the eleventh thereof. And on that day Esau, his brother, came to him, and he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents.$n$,false),
(52820,'T',30,12,30,12,$n$*And do you, Moses, command the children of Yashar'el (Israel) and exhort them not to give their daughters to the nations, and not to take for their sons any of the daughters of the nations, for this is abominable before Yahuah (God). For this reason I have written for you in the words of the Law all the deeds of the Shechemites, which they wrought against Dinah, and how the sons of Jacob spake, saying: "We shall not give our daughter to a man who is uncircumcised; for that were a reproach to us."* (Jubilees 30:11–12). The restored witness quotes the sons' own answer back — *We shall not give our daughter to a man who is uncircumcised; for that were a reproach to us* — the very words of *We cannot do this thing, to give our sister to one that is uncircumcised; for that were a reproach unto us* (Genesis 34:14). The covenant-sign that marks the seed is here turned into the terms of a snare.$n$,$n$For this reason I have written for you in the words of the Law all the deeds of the Shechemites, which they wrought against Dinah, and how the sons of Jacob spake, saying: “We shall not give our daughter to a man who is uncircumcised; for that were a reproach to us.”$n$,false),
(52853,'T',32,19,32,19,$n$*I shall give to your seed all the earth which is under heaven, and they will judge all the nations according to their desires, and after that they will get possession of the whole earth and inherit it for ever.”* (Jubilees 32:19). *And the land which I gave Abraham and Isaac, to thee I will give it, and to thy seed after thee will I give the land.* (Genesis 35:12). At Bethel the promise to Jacob in Genesis is the land, the land given to Abraham and Isaac, now given to him and to his seed after him. Jubilees records the same promise at the same place and lets it run to its full length: all the earth which is under heaven, the nations judged by his seed, and the whole earth possessed and inherited for ever. The land promised to the fathers was never the ceiling. It was the first instalment of an inheritance that widens to the whole earth, held by the seed of Jacob gathered home, and both books are describing the one promise at its two ends.$n$,$n$Ana I shall give to your seed all the earth which is under heaven, and they will judge all the nations according to their desires, and after that they will get possession of the whole earth and inherit it for ever.”$n$,false),
(52860,'T',31,18,31,18,$n$*And to Yahudah (Judah) he said: May Yahuah (God) give you strength and power To tread down all that hate you; A prince shall you be, you and one of your sons, over the sons of Jacob... In you shall be the help of Jacob, And in you be found the salvation of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Jubilees 31:18–19). Among the twelve sons numbered at Bethel — *the sons of Leah; Reuben, Jacob's firstborn, and Simeon, and Levi, and Yahudah (Judah)* (Genesis 35:23) — the kingly office falls to Yahudah, and the restored witness sings it: *A prince shall you be... In you be found the salvation of Yashar'el*. The sceptre that goes to Judah carries the seed-promise toward the King to come.$n$,$n$And to Yahudah (Judah) he said: May Yahuah (God) give you strength and power To tread down all that hate you; A prince shall you be, you and one of your sons, over the sons of Jacob; May your name and the name of your sons go forth and traverse every land and region. Then will the nations fear before your face, And all the nations will quake And all the peoples will quake.$n$,false),
(52861,'T',31,14,31,14,$n$*And he turned to Levi first, and began to bless him first, and said to him: "May the Elohim (God) of all... bless you and your children throughout all the ages... and cause you and your seed, from among all flesh, to approach Him to serve in His sanctuary as the angels of the presence and as the holy ones."* (Jubilees 31:13–14). Of the twelve sons (Genesis 35:23), the priesthood falls to Levi, and the restored witness gives Isaac's blessing upon him — *to approach Him to serve in His sanctuary*. The tribe set apart to draw near is marked here at Bethel, the priestly office settled among the sons of Jacob.$n$,$n$And may Yahuah (God) give to you and to your seed greatness and great glory, and cause you and your seed, from among all flesh, to approach Him to serve in His sanctuary as the angels of the presence and as the holy ones. (Even) as they, will the seed of your sons be for glory and greatness and holiness, and may He make them great to all the ages.$n$,false),
(52862,'T',38,15,38,15,$n$*And the sons of Edom have not got quit of the yoke of servitude which the twelve sons of Jacob had imposed on them until this day. And these are the kings that reigned in Edom before there reigned any king over the children of Yashar'el (Israel) until this day in the land of Edom* (Jubilees 38:14–15). The restored witness keeps the same formula verbatim — *these are the kings that reigned in Edom before there reigned any king over the children of Yashar'el* — matching Genesis 36:31, and frames it under the servitude of Edom to the twelve sons of Jacob: the adversary-house subdued, the covenant line awaiting its own crown.$n$,$n$And these are the kings that reigned in Edom before there reigned any king over the children of Yashar’el (Israel) until this day in the land of Edom.$n$,false),
(52887,'T',34,11,34,11,$n$*And they dealt treacherously with him, and formed a plot against him to slay him, but changing their minds, they sold him to Ishmaelite merchants, and they brought him down into Egypt, and they sold him to Potiphar, the eunuch of Pharaoh... And the sons of Jacob slaughtered a kid, and dipped the coat of Joseph in the blood, and sent (it) to Jacob their father* (Jubilees 34:11–12). The restored witness carries the counsel of *Come, and let us sell him to the Ishmeelites... for he is our brother and our flesh* (Genesis 37:27): the plot to slay turned to a sale, *they sold him to Ishmaelite merchants, and they brought him down into Egypt* — the brother sold rather than slain.$n$,$n$And they dealt treacherously with him, and formed a plot against him to slay him, but changing their minds, they sold him to Ishmaelite merchants, and they brought him down into Egypt, and they sold him to Potiphar, the eunuch of Pharaoh, the chief of the cooks, priest of the city of ’Êlêw.$n$,false),
(53739,'T',41,11,41,11,$n$*And she said to him: "Give me my hire"; and he said to her: "I have nothing in my hand save my ring that is on my finger, and my necklace, and my staff which is in my hand."... and he gave them to her, (and he went in to her,) and she conceived by him* (Jubilees 41:11–12). The restored witness keeps the pledge entire — *my ring... my necklace... my staff* given for the hire — matching *Thy signet, and thy bracelets, and thy staff that is in thine hand. And he gave it her, and came in unto her, and she conceived by him* (Genesis 38:18). The tokens handed over in the dark will become the very witnesses that convict and acquit.$n$,$n$And she said to him: “Give me my hire”; and he said to her: “I have nothing in my hand save my ring that is on my finger, and my necklace, and my staff which is in my hand.”$n$,false),
(53765,'T',39,12,39,12,$n$*And the Egyptian saw the garment of Joseph and the broken door, and heard the words of his wife, and cast Joseph into prison into the place where the prisoners were kept whom the king imprisoned. And he was there in the prison; and Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour in the sight of the chief of the prison guards and compassion before him, for he saw that Yahuah (God) was with him* (Jubilees 39:11–12). The restored witness keeps both the condemning and the comfort — *cast Joseph into prison... and Yahuah gave Joseph favour* — matching *put him into the prison, a place where the king's prisoners were bound* (Genesis 39:20), and already turning toward the mercy that meets him there: the innocent lowered, but not abandoned.$n$,$n$And he was there in the prison; and Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour in the sight of the chief of the prison guards and compassion before him, for he saw that Yahuah (God) was with him, and that Yahuah (God) made all that he did to prosper.$n$,false),
(53788,'T',40,6,40,6,$n$*And he appointed him the second in all his kingdom and gave him authority over all Egypt, and caused him to ride in the second chariot of Pharaoh. And he clothed him with byssus garments, and he put a gold chain upon his neck... and he placed a ring on his hand and made him ruler over all his house, and magnified him, and said to him: "Only on the throne shall I be greater than you."* (Jubilees 40:6–7). The restored witness keeps the regalia entire — *the second chariot... byssus garments... a gold chain upon his neck... a ring on his hand... ruler over all his house* — matching *Pharaoh took off his ring... and arrayed him in vestures of fine linen, and put a gold chain about his neck* (Genesis 41:42) and *only in the throne will I be greater than thou* (41:40). The lowered one robed and ringed and set over all.$n$,$n$And he appointed him the second in all his kingdom and gave him authority over all Egypt, and caused him to ride in the second chariot of Pharaoh.$n$,false),
(54973,'T',42,6,42,6,$n$*And after that he set them free again, and detained Simeon alone and sent off his nine brothers. And he filled their sacks with corn, and he put their gold in their sacks, and they did not know* (Jubilees 42:6–7). The restored witness keeps the binding and the secret restoring together — *detained Simeon alone... filled their sacks with corn... put their gold in their sacks, and they did not know* — matching *took from them Simeon, and bound him before their eyes* (Genesis 42:24) and the money restored into the sack (42:25); the brother held, the gold returned unknown, the hand of Elohim moving behind the fear.$n$,$n$And after that he set them free again, and detained Simeon alone and sent off his nine brothers.$n$,false),
(55013,'T',43,11,43,11,$n$*...and your servant our father loves him, and his life also is bound up with the life of this (lad). And it will come to pass, when we go to your servant our father, and the lad is not with us, that he will die, and we shall bring down our father with sorrow to death* (Jubilees 43:11–12). The restored witness keeps the bond word-for-word — *his life also is bound up with the life of this (lad)* — exactly as *his life is bound up in the lad's life* (Genesis 44:30); the father's soul knit to the youngest so that the lad's loss is the old man's grave.$n$,$n$And Yahudah (Judah) said: "O my lord, let your servant, I pray you, speak a word in my lord’s ear; two brothers did your servant’s mother bear to our father; one went away and was lost, and has not been found, and he alone is left of his mother, and your servant our father loves him, and his life also is bound up with the life of this (lad).$n$,false),
(60478,'T',5,29,5,24,$n$*And Yahuah (God) opened seven flood-gates of heaven, And the mouths of the fountains of the great deep, seven mouths in number. And the flood-gates began to pour down water from the heaven forty days and forty nights, And the fountains of the deep also sent up waters, until the whole world was full of water.* (Jubilees 5:24–25). *Whereby the world that then was, being overflowed with water, perished:* (2 Peter 3:6). Peter says the scoffers of the last days willingly forget one thing: that the world that then was perished, overflowed with water. Jubilees does not let it be forgotten. It counts the flood-gates and the mouths of the deep, forty days and forty nights, until the whole world was full of water. The same picture Genesis gives stands here in the restored library with the numbers attached. The judgment the scoffers deny is written in more than one book, and it is not new.$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) opened seven flood-gates of heaven, And the mouths of the fountains of the great deep, seven mouths in number.$n$,true),
(69521,'T',4,17,4,17,$n$Jubilees 4:17–18 — *And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven... And he was the first to write a testimony.* Enoch the first writer is the pattern of Eve's charge — the antediluvian life set down in a testimony that outlasts the generations.$n$,$n$And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book, that men might know the seasons of the years according to the order of their separate months.$n$,false),
(104148,'T',47,12,47,12,$n$*And on the second day you did find two of the children of Yashar'el (Israel) striving together, and you did say to him who was doing the wrong: "Why do you smite your brother?" And he was angry and indignant, and said "Who made you a prince and a judge over us? Thinkest you to kill me as you killedst the Egyptian yesterday?" And you did fear and flee on account of these words* (Jubilees 47:11–12). Jubilees carries *Who made thee a prince and a judge over us?* and the flight of Exodus 2:14-15 — the rejected deliverer driven out.$n$,$n$And he was angry and indignant, and said “Who made you a prince and a judge over us? Thinkest you to kill me as you killedst the Egyptian yesterday?” And you did fear and flee on account of these words.$n$,false),
(104188,'T',48,8,48,8,$n$*ten great and terrible judgments came on the land of Egypt that you might execute vengeance on it for Yashar’el (Israel). And Yahuah (God) did everything for Yashar’el’s (Israel’s) sake, and according to His covenant, which He had ordained with Abraham that He would take vengeance on them as they had brought them by force into bondage* (Jubilees 48:7–8). Jubilees grounds *I will redeem you with a stretched out arm, and with great judgments* (Exodus 6:6) in the covenant with Abraham — the deliverance is Yahuah keeping the oath He swore, *I have remembered my covenant* (Exodus 6:5).$n$,$n$And Yahuah (God) did everything for Yashar’el’s (Israel’s) sake, and according to His covenant, which He had ordained with Abraham that He would take vengeance on them as they had brought them by force into bondage.$n$,false),
(119919,'T',31,19,31,19,$n$*And to Yahudah (Judah) he said: ... A prince shall you be ... In you shall be the help of Jacob, And in you be found the salvation of Yashar’el (Israel)* (Jubilees 31:18–19). Isaac blesses Judah with the very office Matthew's genealogy delivers — the prince in whom the salvation of Israel is found.$n$,$n$In you shall be the help of Jacob, And in you be found the salvation of Yashar’el (Israel).$n$,false),
(165948,'T',1,20,1,20,$n$*Let Your mercy, O Yahuah (God), be lifted up upon Your people... and create in them an upright spirit, and let not the spirit of Beliar rule over them... But they are Your people and Your inheritance...* (Jubilees 1:20–21). The jealousy of Deuteronomy 32:21 bends toward this: a clean heart created in the people who are still *Your inheritance*, the new-covenant cure for the apostasy the Song laments.$n$,$n$Let Your mercy, O Yahuah (God), be lifted up upon Your people, and create in them an upright spirit, and let not the spirit of Beliar rule over them to accuse them before You, and to ensnare them from all the paths of righteousness, so that they may perish from before Your face.$n$,false),
(172794,'T',1,11,1,11,$n$*And they will make to themselves high places and groves and graven images, and they will worship, each his own (graven image), so as to go astray, and they will sacrifice their children to demons, and to all the works of the error of their hearts.* (Jubilees 1:11). *And he ordained him priests for the high places, and for the devils, and for the calves which he had made.* (2 Chronicles 11:15). What Yahuah (God) told Moses on the mountain they would do, Jeroboam did in Bethel and Dan: high places, devils, and calves of his own making, with priests ordained to serve them. Jubilees calls the devils demons and adds the worst of it, children sacrificed to them. The golden calf was not a one-time fall at the foot of Sinai; it came back as a state religion with its own priesthood and its own altars. Going astray always builds a high place and ordains someone to tend it, and the error of the heart ends in the blood of the children.$n$,$n$And they will make to themselves high places and groves and graven images, and they will worship, each his own (graven image), so as to go astray, and they will sacrifice their children to demons, and to all the works of the error of their hearts.$n$,true);
INSERT INTO _s463bm VALUES
(5172,25565,$n$Malachi 3:16 — *Then they that feared Yahuah (LORD) spake often one to another: and Yahuah (LORD) hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared Yahuah (LORD), and that thought upon his name.* Jubilees 1:26's charge to write the first and the last echoes the book of remembrance written before Yahuah.$n$),
(5176,25591,$n$Genesis 2:3 — *And Elohim (God) blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it: because that in it he had rested from all his work which Elohim (God) created and made.* Jubilees 2:17 gives this same blessed, sanctified seventh day as the great sign over creation.$n$),
(5179,25610,$n$Matthew 19:5 — *And said, For this cause shall a man leave father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife: and they twain shall be one flesh?* Yahusha appeals to the same creation ordinance Jubilees 3:7 records as given from the second week — "man and wife be one."$n$),
(5181,25616,$n$Genesis 2:8 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) planted a garden eastward in Eden; and there he put the man whom he had formed.* The garden Jubilees 3:12 calls "holier than all the earth besides" is this planted Eden of Genesis.$n$),
(5182,25617,$n$Genesis 3:1 — *Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath Elohim (God) said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?* Jubilees 3:17 quotes the serpent's opening question almost word for word.$n$),
(5184,25626,$n$Genesis 3:21 — *Unto Adam also and to his wife did Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) make coats of skins, and clothed them.* The covering Jubilees 3:31 prescribes "on the heavenly tables" — that they not uncover themselves as the nations do — flows from this first God-given covering.$n$),
(5188,25631,$n$Jude 1:14 — *And Enoch also, the seventh from Adam, prophesied of these, saying, Behold, Yahuah (Lord) cometh with ten thousands of his saints.* The canon itself names Enoch the testifying prophet Jubilees 4:18 calls the first to write a testimony.$n$),
(5186,25637,$n$Genesis 4:15 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto him, Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold. And Yahuah (LORD) set a mark upon Cain, lest any finding him should kill him.* The Genesis judgment on Cain matches the heavenly-tablet curse of Jubilees 4:5.$n$),
(5194,25665,$n$Leviticus 16:29 — *And this shall be a statute for ever unto you: that in the seventh month, on the tenth day of the month, ye shall afflict your souls, and do no work at all.* Jubilees 5:17 promises Israel forgiveness if they turn — the Torah fixes the 'statute for ever' on which that yearly pardon hangs.$n$),
(5195,25667,$n$Genesis 6:14 — *Make thee an ark of gopher wood; rooms shalt thou make in the ark, and shalt pitch it within and without with pitch.* The ark Noah is commanded to make in Jubilees 5:21 is Genesis' ark of gopher wood.$n$),
(5195,25669,$n$Genesis 7:12 — *And the rain was upon the earth forty days and forty nights.* The forty days and forty nights of Jubilees 5:25 are Genesis' forty-day rain.$n$),
(5195,25670,$n$Genesis 7:24 — *And the waters prevailed upon the earth an hundred and fifty days.* The 'five months-one hundred and fifty days' of Jubilees 5:27 is Genesis' hundred and fifty days exactly.$n$),
(5198,25679,$n$Genesis 9:4 — *But flesh with the life thereof, which is the blood thereof, shall ye not eat.* The blood prohibition Jubilees 6:7 gives Noah is taken straight from Genesis' covenant with Noah.$n$),
(5198,25681,$n$Leviticus 17:10 — *And whatsoever man there be of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), or of the strangers that sojourn among you, that eateth any manner of blood; I will even set my face against that soul that eateth blood, and will cut him off from among his people.* The Torah carries the Noahic no-blood law forward to Israel, with the same cutting-off Jubilees 6:12,13 commands.$n$),
(5198,25682,$n$Leviticus 17:11 — *For the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement for your souls: for it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul.* Jubilees 6:7's reason — the life of all flesh is in the blood — is the Torah's own reason, and it is why the blood belongs on the altar (Jubilees 6:14).$n$),
(5198,25683,$n$Leviticus 17:14 — *For it is the life of all flesh; the blood of it is for the life thereof: therefore I said unto the children of Yashar'el (Israel), Ye shall eat the blood of no manner of flesh: for the life of all flesh is the blood thereof: whosoever eateth it shall be cut off.* Word for word the rationale of Jubilees 6:7 — the life of all flesh is the blood — binding the same eternal commandment.$n$),
(5204,25699,$n$Genesis 9:21 — *And he drank of the wine, and was drunken; and he was uncovered within his tent:* the Genesis source Jubilees 7:7 retells almost verbatim.$n$),
(5206,25708,$n$Genesis 6:4 — *There were giants in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the sons of Elohim (God) came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown:* the giants Jubilees 7:22 calls the Nâphîdîm who devoured one another.$n$),
(5206,25709,$n$1 Enoch 7:2 — *And they became pregnant, and they bare great giants, whose height was three thousand ells:* the live Enoch witness to the giants Jubilees 7:22 names from the Watchers.$n$),
(5206,25710,$n$1 Enoch 7:3 — *Who consumed all the acquisitions of men. And when men could no longer sustain them, the giants turned against them and devoured mankind:* the same mutual devouring Jubilees 7:22 describes among the Nâphîdîm.$n$),
(5212,25721,$n$1 Enoch 26:1 — *And I went from thence to the middle of the earth, and I saw a blessed place in which there were trees with branches abiding and blooming [of a dismembered tree].* Enoch's vision of the middle of the earth — the holy mountain and Zion — is the same centre Jubilees 8:19 sets at the navel of the world.$n$),
(5212,25722,$n$Ezekiel 38:12 — *To take a spoil, and to take a prey; to turn thine hand upon the desolate places that are now inhabited, and upon the people that are gathered out of the nations, which have gotten cattle and goods, that dwell in the midst of the land.* The regathered seed dwelling in the navel of the earth — the centre Shem inherits in Jubilees 8:19.$n$),
(5212,25723,$n$Ezekiel 5:5 — *Thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); This is Jerusalem: I have set it in the midst of the nations and countries that are round about her.* Yahuah Himself names Jerusalem the midst of the earth that Jubilees 8:19-19 calls Zion the navel.$n$),
(5267,25919,$n$Genesis 11:1 — *And the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech.* Jubilees 10:18 names the same moment in Peleg's days — one people, evil in purpose, before the tongues were confounded.$n$),
(5272,25932,$n$Acts 7:3 — *And said unto him, Get thee out of thy country, and from thy kindred, and come into the land which I shall shew thee.* The call out of the idolatrous house Jubilees 11:16-17 shows Abraham already straining toward as a child.$n$),
(5272,25934,$n$Joshua 24:3 — *And I took your father Abraham from the other side of the flood, and led him throughout all the land of Canaan, and multiplied his seed, and gave him Isaac.* The election Jubilees 11:16 dramatizes as the boy separating himself from his father's idols.$n$),
(5269,25941,$n$Genesis 11:26 — *And Terah lived seventy years, and begat Abram, Nahor, and Haran:* the climax of the line, Abram born to Terah exactly as Jubilees 11:15 narrates.$n$),
(5269,25943,$n$Genesis 11:22 — *And Serug lived thirty years, and begat Nahor:* matching Jubilees 11:7-8 where Serug dwells in Ur and his wife bears him Nahor.$n$),
(5277,25950,$n$Genesis 12:3 — *And I will bless them that bless thee, and curse him that curseth thee: and in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed.* The blessing-and-cursing and all-families promise of Jubilees 12:23 is Genesis 12:3 set side by side.$n$),
(5276,25952,$n$1 Enoch 10:16 — *Destroy all wrong from the face of the earth and let every evil work come to an end: and let the plant of righteousness and truth appear: and it shall prove a blessing; the works of righteousness and truth' shall be planted in truth and joy for evermore.* Abram's plea to stablish him and his seed for ever in Jubilees 12:20 is the planting of the righteous seed against the seed-war that 1 Enoch 10 promises after the Watchers are bound.$n$),
(5275,25955,$n$Genesis 11:31 — *And Terah took Abram his son, and Lot the son of Haran his son's son, and Sarai his daughter in law, his son Abram's wife; and they went forth with them from Ur of the Chaldees, to go into the land of Canaan; and they came unto Haran, and dwelt there.* The family's departure from Ur toward Canaan in Jubilees 12:15 is the same journey Genesis records.$n$),
(5282,25968,$n$Genesis 14:1 — *And it came to pass in the days of Amraphel king of Shinar, Arioch king of Ellasar, Chedorlaomer king of Elam, and Tidal king of nations.* Jubilees 13:22 names the same coalition of kings.$n$),
(5279,25978,$n$Genesis 12:8 — *And he removed from thence unto a mountain on the east of Beth-el, and pitched his tent, having Beth-el on the west, and Hai on the east: and there he builded an altar unto Yahuah (LORD), and called upon the name of Yahuah (LORD).* Jubilees 13:8 has Abram between Bethel and Ai building the altar and calling on the Name.$n$),
(5279,25979,$n$Genesis 12:7 — *And Yahuah (LORD) appeared unto Abram, and said, Unto thy seed will I give this land: and there builded he an altar unto Yahuah (LORD), who appeared unto him.* The land-and-seed promise and the altar of Jubilees 13:3,4 are Genesis verbatim.$n$),
(5287,25987,$n$Genesis 15:17 — *And it came to pass, that, when the sun went down, and it was dark, behold a smoking furnace, and a burning lamp that passed between those pieces.* The smoking furnace and flame passing between the pieces (Jubilees 14:17) is the sign of Yahuah cutting the covenant alone.$n$),
(5286,25990,$n$Genesis 17:7 — *And I will establish my covenant between me and thee and thy seed after thee in their generations for an everlasting covenant, to be a Elohim (God) unto thee, and to thy seed after thee.* The "to you and to your seed after you" of Jubilees 14:7 is the everlasting seed-covenant Genesis 17 seals with circumcision.$n$),
(5286,25993,$n$Genesis 15:7 — *And he said unto him, I am Yahuah (LORD) that brought thee out of Ur of the Chaldees, to give thee this land to inherit it.* The self-naming by the deliverance out of Ur (Jubilees 14:7) is Abraham's election out of the idolatrous nations.$n$),
(5290,26009,$n$Genesis 17:19 — *And Elohim (God) said, Sarah thy wife shall bear thee a son indeed; and thou shalt call his name Isaac: and I will establish my covenant with him for an everlasting covenant, and with his seed after him.* Jubilees 15:19 names Isaac and establishes the everlasting covenant in him exactly as Genesis does.$n$),
(5289,26012,$n$Romans 4:17 — *(As it is written, I have made thee a father of many nations,) before him whom he believed, even Elohim (God), who quickeneth the dead, and calleth those things which be not as though they were.* Paul takes the "father of many nations" of Jubilees 15:7 as the seal of Abraham's faith in the God who raises the dead.$n$),
(5289,26014,$n$Genesis 17:7 — *And I will establish my covenant between me and thee and thy seed after thee in their generations for an everlasting covenant, to be a Elohim (God) unto thee, and to thy seed after thee.* Jubilees 15:9 names the same everlasting covenant to Abraham and his seed throughout their generations.$n$),
(5289,26015,$n$Genesis 17:5 — *Neither shall thy name any more be called Abram, but thy name shall be Abraham; for a father of many nations have I made thee.* Jubilees 15:7 carries the renaming word for word — Abram becomes Abraham, father of many nations forever.$n$),
(5296,26027,$n$1 Peter 2:9 — *But ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people; that ye should shew forth the praises of him who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light:* The portion of the Most High, the kingdom and priests of Jubilees 16:18, is the same calling the apostle lays on the regathered remnant.$n$),
(5296,26028,$n$Exodus 19:6 — *And ye shall be unto me a kingdom of priests, and an holy nation. These are the words which thou shalt speak unto the children of Yashar'el (Israel).* The kingdom and priests and holy nation Jubilees 16:18 sees in Abraham's holy seed is Sinai's own charter for Israel.$n$),
(5295,26032,$n$Genesis 21:2 — *For Sarah conceived, and bare Abraham a son in his old age, at the set time of which Elohim (God) had spoken to him.* The set time of which Yahuah had spoken to Abraham is the same set time Jubilees 16:13 records for Isaac's birth.$n$),
(5294,26035,$n$Genesis 19:29 — *And it came to pass, when Elohim (God) destroyed the cities of the plain, that Elohim (God) remembered Abraham, and sent Lot out of the midst of the overthrow, when he overthrew the cities in the which Lot dwelt.* Lot is saved because Elohim remembered Abraham — Jubilees 16:7 keeps this Torah reason verbatim.$n$),
(5470,26670,$n$Genesis 21:20 — *And Elohim (God) was with the lad; and he grew, and dwelt in the wilderness, and became an archer.* The same growth into an archer that Jubilees 17:13 records.$n$),
(5476,26681,$n$Hebrews 11:17 — *By faith Abraham, when he was tried, offered up Isaac: and he that had received the promises offered up his only begotten son,* The offered son of Jubilees 18:8 is Hebrews' first witness of tried faith — the one who had the promise yet offered the heir.$n$),
(5476,26683,$n$James 2:21 — *Was not Abraham our father justified by works, when he had offered Isaac his son upon the altar?* The altar-offering of Jubilees 18:8 is James's proof that Abraham was justified by works, not faith alone.$n$),
(5475,26685,$n$Genesis 22:17 — *That in blessing I will bless thee, and in multiplying I will multiply thy seed as the stars of the heaven, and as the sand which is upon the sea shore; and thy seed shall possess the gate of his enemies;* The seed multiplied as the stars and the sand in Jubilees 18:15 is the Genesis oath verbatim — the covenant kept in the fathers.$n$),
(5475,26686,$n$Genesis 22:16 — *And said, By myself have I sworn, saith Yahuah (LORD), for because thou hast done this thing, and hast not withheld thy son, thine only son:* The self-sworn oath of Jubilees 18:15 is the Torah's own — Yahuah binds Himself because the son was not withheld.$n$),
(5474,26687,$n$Genesis 22:14 — *And Abraham called the name of that place Yahuah Yireh (Jehovah-jireh): as it is said to this day, In the mount of Yahuah (LORD) it shall be seen.* Jubilees 18:13 names the same place — “Yahuah has seen” — and locates it as Mount Sion, the mountain of provision.$n$),
(5472,26694,$n$Genesis 22:2 — *And he said, Take now thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest, and get thee into the land of Moriah; and offer him there for a burnt offering upon one of the mountains which I will tell thee of.* The command Jubilees 18:2 retells — the beloved son offered on a mountain Elohim will point out — is the very word of Genesis.$n$),
(5482,26698,$n$Genesis 13:16 — *And I will make thy seed as the dust of the earth: so that if a man can number the dust of the earth, then shall thy seed also be numbered.* Abraham's blessing on Jacob in Jubilees 19:22 — seed numberless as the sand — is the very promise spoken to Abraham himself.$n$),
(5481,26702,$n$Genesis 25:28 — *And Isaac loved Esau, because he did eat of his venison: but Rebekah loved Jacob.* Jubilees 19:15 carries the same divided love — Abraham loved Jacob, Isaac loved Esau.$n$),
(5480,26707,$n$Genesis 24:67 — *And Isaac brought her into his mother Sarah's tent, and took Rebekah, and she became his wife; and he loved her: and Isaac was comforted after his mother's death.* This is the marriage Jubilees 19:10 records — Abraham takes a wife for his son Isaac.$n$),
(5480,26708,$n$Genesis 24:15 — *And it came to pass, before he had done speaking, that, behold, Rebekah came out, who was born to Bethuel, son of Milcah, the wife of Nahor, Abraham's brother, with her pitcher upon her shoulder.* Jubilees 19:10 names her by the same lineage — Rebecca daughter of Bethuel, son of Nahor, brother of Abraham.$n$),
(5478,26716,$n$Genesis 23:1 — *And Sarah was an hundred and seven and twenty years old: these were the years of the life of Sarah.* Jubilees 19:7 keeps the same count to the year — one hundred and twenty-seven years of Sarah's life.$n$),
(5489,26717,$n$Genesis 25:6 — *But unto the sons of the concubines, which Abraham had, Abraham gave gifts, and sent them away from Isaac his son, while he yet lived, eastward, unto the east country.* Jubilees 20:11's sending of Ishmael and Keturah's sons away with gifts, the inheritance reserved to Isaac, is the same event Genesis records.$n$),
(5497,26763,$n$Genesis 28:4 — *And give thee the blessing of Abraham, to thee, and to thy seed with thee; that thou mayest inherit the land wherein thou art a stranger, which Elohim (God) gave unto Abraham.* The blessing of Abraham passing to Jacob's seed in Jubilees 22:10 is the very blessing Isaac renews over Jacob at his sending.$n$),
(5512,26785,$n$Exodus 32:32 — *Yet now, if thou wilt forgive their sin—; and if not, blot me, I pray thee, out of thy book which thou hast written.* The heavenly tables of Jubilees 24:33, engraved for the day of judgment, are the very book Moses pleads over.$n$),
(5512,26786,$n$Malachi 3:16 — *Then they that feared Yahuah (LORD) spake often one to another: and Yahuah (LORD) hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared Yahuah (LORD), and that thought upon his name.* Jubilees 24:33's “written and engraved on the heavenly tables” is the same pre-written book of remembrance the canon names.$n$),
(5511,26788,$n$Genesis 26:24 — *And Yahuah (LORD) appeared unto him the same night, and said, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham thy father: fear not, for I am with thee, and will bless thee, and multiply thy seed for my servant Abraham’s sake.* Jubilees 24:22 quotes the Beer-sheba theophany verbatim, adding only its new-moon dating.$n$),
(5507,26798,$n$Romans 9:12 — *It was said unto her, The elder shall serve the younger.* Jubilees 24:7 makes the election plain — “Jacob became the elder” — the seed kept by Yahuah's choice, not by merit.$n$),
(5507,26799,$n$Hebrews 12:17 — *For ye know how that afterward, when he would have inherited the blessing, he was rejected: for he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears.* Jubilees 24:7 says Esau was “brought down from his dignity” — the rejection Hebrews carries forward.$n$),
(5650,27279,$n$Genesis 27:27 — *And he came near, and kissed him: and he smelled the smell of his raiment, and blessed him, and said, See, the smell of my son is as the smell of a field which Yahuah (LORD) hath blessed:* Jubilees 26:22 opens the blessing with the same smell-of-the-field that Yahuah has blessed.$n$),
(5650,27280,$n$Genesis 27:28 — *Therefore Elohim (God) give thee of the dew of heaven, and the fatness of the earth, and plenty of corn and wine:* The dew of heaven and plenty of corn of Jubilees 26:23 is the Genesis blessing word for word.$n$),
(5650,27281,$n$Genesis 27:29 — *Let people serve thee, and nations bow down to thee: be lord over thy brethren, and let thy mother’s sons bow down to thee: cursed be every one that curseth thee, and blessed be he that blesseth thee.* Jubilees 26:23,24 carries the same nations-serve-you, mother's-sons-bow, cursed/blessed formula.$n$),
(5652,27288,$n$Genesis 27:36 — *And he said, Is not he rightly named Jacob? for he hath supplanted me these two times: he took away my birthright; and, behold, now he hath taken away my blessing. And he said, Hast thou not reserved a blessing for me?* Jubilees 26:30 has Esau name the two supplantings — birthright then blessing — in the same words.$n$),
(5653,27293,$n$Genesis 27:41 — *And Esau hated Jacob because of the blessing wherewith his father blessed him: and Esau said in his heart, The days of mourning for my father are at hand; then will I slay my brother Jacob.* Jubilees 26:35 ends with the identical murderous resolve in Esau's heart.$n$),
(5659,27295,$n$Leviticus 27:30 — *And all the tithe of the land, whether of the seed of the land, or of the fruit of the tree, is the LORD’S: it is holy unto Yahuah (LORD).* The Torah engraves as holy the very tithe Jacob vows to Yahuah at Bethel in Jubilees 27:27.$n$),
(5659,27296,$n$Genesis 14:20 — *And blessed be the El Elyon (most high God), which hath delivered thine enemies into thy hand. And he gave him tithes of all.* Abraham tithed before Jacob ever did — the tenth Jacob vows in Jubilees 27:27 is the fathers' practice, not a new thing.$n$),
(5655,27307,$n$Genesis 28:5 — *And Isaac sent away Jacob: and he went to Padan-aram unto Laban, son of Bethuel the Syrian, the brother of Rebekah, Jacob’s and Esau’s mother.* The sending to Mesopotamia/Padan-aram that Jubilees 27:12 records in its own words.$n$),
(5663,27316,$n$Hebrews 7:14 — *For it is evident that our Lord sprang out of Juda; of which tribe Moses spake nothing concerning priesthood.* The kingly tribe born in Jubilees 28:15 is the tribe of the Messiah — Judah's sceptre fulfilled.$n$),
(5663,27317,$n$Numbers 24:17 — *...there shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Yashar'el (Israel), and shall smite the corners of Moab, and destroy all the children of Sheth.* The Sceptre of Judah's line, first dated at his birth in Jubilees 28:15, is the Star Balaam foresaw.$n$),
(5663,27318,$n$Genesis 49:10 — *The sceptre shall not depart from Yahudah (Judah), nor a lawgiver from between his feet, until Shiloh come; and unto him shall the gathering of the people be.* The Yahudah born in Jubilees 28:15 carries the kingship and the Messianic sceptre.$n$),
(5668,27336,$n$Genesis 36:8 — *Thus dwelt Esau in mount Seir: Esau is Edom.* The Torah seals Esau's settling in Seir that Jubilees 29:13 marks as his departure.$n$),
(5668,27337,$n$Genesis 33:16 — *So Esau returned that day on his way unto Seir.* Genesis says exactly what Jubilees 29:13 says: Esau departed to the land of Seir.$n$),
(5668,27338,$n$Genesis 33:3 — *And he passed over before them, and bowed himself to the ground seven times, until he came near to his brother.* Jacob's bowing approach is the reconciliation Jubilees 29:13 names in a phrase.$n$),
(5668,27339,$n$Genesis 33:1 — *And Jacob lifted up his eyes, and looked, and, behold, Esau came, and with him four hundred men. And he divided the children unto Leah, and unto Rachel, and unto the two handmaids.* Genesis fills out the meeting Jubilees 29:13 records as Esau coming to Jacob.$n$),
(5666,27343,$n$Genesis 31:52 — *This heap be witness, and this pillar be witness, that I will not pass over this heap to thee, and that thou shalt not pass over this heap and this pillar unto me, for harm.* This is the exact non-crossing oath Jubilees 29:7 compresses into a single line.$n$),
(5666,27346,$n$Genesis 31:44 — *Now therefore come thou, let us make a covenant, I and thou; and let it be for a witness between me and thee.* Laban's proposal is the covenant-oath Jubilees 29:7 says the two men swore.$n$),
(5674,27351,$n$Revelation 3:5 — *He that overcometh, the same shall be clothed in white raiment; and I will not blot out his name out of the book of life, but I will confess his name before my Father, and before his angels.* The Messiah keeps the same book of life Jubilees 30:22 says the unfaithful are blotted from.$n$),
(5674,27354,$n$Exodus 32:33 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Moses, Whosoever hath sinned against me, him will I blot out of my book.* The blotting from the book is exactly the destruction out of the book of life threatened in Jubilees 30:22.$n$),
(5673,27356,$n$Deuteronomy 33:9 — *Who said unto his father and to his mother, I have not seen him; neither did he acknowledge his brethren, nor knew his own children: for they have observed thy word, and kept thy covenant.* Levi's setting of covenant above kin is the same zeal reckoned for righteousness in Jubilees 30:17,18.$n$),
(5677,27378,$n$Hebrews 7:14 — *For it is evident that our Lord sprang out of Juda; of which tribe Moses spake nothing concerning priesthood.* The deliverer found in Judah (Jubilees 31:19) is the Lord the New Testament says sprang out of Judah.$n$),
(5677,27379,$n$Numbers 24:17 — *I shall see him, but not now… there shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Yashar'el (Israel), and shall smite the corners of Moab, and destroy all the children of Sheth.* The salvation of Israel in Judah (Jubilees 31:19) is the Sceptre that rises out of Israel.$n$),
(5676,27383,$n$Numbers 18:2 — *And thy brethren also of the tribe of Levi, the tribe of thy father, bring thou with thee, that they may be joined unto thee, and minister unto thee: but thou and thy sons with thee shall minister before the tabernacle of witness.* The Levites' joining to minister is the drawing-near to serve Isaac foretells in Jubilees 31:14.$n$),
(5676,27384,$n$Deuteronomy 10:8 — *At that time Yahuah (LORD) separated the tribe of Levi, to bear the ark of the covenant of Yahuah (LORD), to stand before Yahuah (LORD) to minister unto him, and to bless in his name, unto this day.* Levi's seed approaching to serve in the sanctuary (Jubilees 31:14) is the separated tribe that stands before Yahuah.$n$),
(5676,27386,$n$Deuteronomy 33:8 — *And of Levi he said, Let thy Thummim and thy Urim be with thy holy one, whom thou didst prove at Massah, and with whom thou didst strive at the waters of Meribah;* Moses' blessing of Levi as Yahuah's holy one matches Isaac drawing Levi's seed near to serve in the sanctuary (Jubilees 31:14).$n$),
(5860,28023,$n$Deuteronomy 27:20 — *Cursed be he that lieth with his father's wife; because he uncovereth his father's skirt. And all the people shall say, Amen.* The very 'second time' curse Jubilees 33:12 quotes, with the people's answering 'So be it; so be it.'$n$),
(5868,28030,$n$Genesis 28:1 — *And Isaac called Jacob, and blessed him, and charged him, and said unto him, Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Canaan.* Isaac's charge to Jacob is the seed-kept concern that the wife-roster of Jubilees 34:20 quietly measures.$n$),
(5868,28031,$n$Genesis 24:3 — *And I will make thee swear by Yahuah (LORD), the Elohim (God) of heaven, and the Elohim (God) of the earth, that thou shalt not take a wife unto my son of the daughters of the Canaanites, among whom I dwell.* Abraham's oath frames Jubilees 34:20's careful naming of which wives were Canaanite and which were of the line of Terah.$n$),
(5866,28036,$n$Genesis 37:35 — *And all his sons and all his daughters rose up to comfort him; but he refused to be comforted; and he said, For I will go down into the grave unto my son mourning. Thus his father wept for him.* Jacob's vow in Jubilees 34:17, 'Let me go down to the grave mourning for my son,' echoes Genesis word for word.$n$),
(5866,28039,$n$Genesis 37:31 — *And they took Joseph's coat, and killed a kid of the goats, and dipped the coat in the blood.* The slaughtered kid and the dipped coat of Jubilees 34:12 match Genesis stitch for stitch.$n$),
(5864,28044,$n$Genesis 48:21 — *And Yashar'el (Israel) said unto Joseph, Behold, I die: but Elohim (God) shall be with you, and bring you again unto the land of your fathers.* The same Jacob who slew the Amorites in the pastures of Shechem (Jubilees 34:7) blesses Joseph with the portion he won there.$n$),
(5875,28075,$n$Deuteronomy 6:5 — *And thou shalt love Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy might.* Isaac binds his sons to fear and worship Yahuah in Jubilees 36:7 — the first of the two great commandments.$n$),
(5887,28094,$n$Genesis 36:31 — *And these are the kings that reigned in the land of Edom, before there reigned any king over the children of Yashar’el (Israel)* — Jubilees 38:15 carries Genesis’ own heading word for word, the kings of Edom before Israel had a king.$n$),
(5885,28098,$n$Malachi 1:3 — *And I hated Esau, and laid his mountains and his heritage waste for the dragons of the wilderness* — the love-of-Jacob, hatred-of-Esau election that grounds Jubilees 38:12 is the same Yahuah declares to Israel through Malachi.$n$),
(5885,28099,$n$Numbers 24:18 — *And Edom shall be a possession, Seir also shall be a possession for his enemies; and Yashar’el (Israel) shall do valiantly* — Balaam’s oracle of Edom-as-possession is the standing yoke of tribute laid on Esau’s sons in Jubilees 38:12.$n$),
(5892,28110,$n$Genesis 39:21 — *But Yahuah (LORD) was with Joseph, and shewed him mercy, and gave him favour in the sight of the keeper of the prison.* The very favour-in-prison Jubilees 39:12 records — Yahuah with Joseph before the chief of the prison guards.$n$),
(5895,28133,$n$Genesis 41:42 — *And Pharaoh took off his ring from his hand, and put it upon Joseph’s hand, and arrayed him in vestures of fine linen, and put a gold chain about his neck.* The ring, the byssus garments, and the gold chain of Jubilees 40:7 are the regalia of Genesis 41:42.$n$),
(5895,28134,$n$Genesis 41:40 — *Thou shalt be over my house, and according unto thy word shall all my people be ruled: only in the throne will I be greater than thou.* Pharaoh's words in Jubilees 40:7 (“made him ruler over all his house… Only on the throne shall I be greater than you”) are Genesis 41:40 exactly.$n$),
(6113,28873,$n$Ruth 4:17 — *And the women her neighbours gave it a name, saying, There is a son born to Naomi; and they called his name Obed: he is the father of Jesse, the father of David.* The seed “not rooted out” in Jubilees 41:27 runs through Perez down to David.$n$),
(6120,28890,$n$Genesis 50:20 — *But as for you, ye thought evil against me; but Elohim (God) meant it unto good, to bring to pass, as it is this day, to save much people alive.* The feast Joseph spreads in Jubilees 42:22 foreshadows his final word: the evil done him was meant by Elohim for good, to save much people alive.$n$),
(6118,28898,$n$Genesis 42:35 — *And it came to pass as they emptied their sacks, that, behold, every man’s bundle of money was in his sack: and when both they and their father saw the bundles of money, they were afraid.* The returned money that makes Jacob fear to send Benjamin in Jubilees 42:12 is the silver discovered in Genesis.$n$),
(6117,28901,$n$Genesis 42:25 — *Then Joseph commanded to fill their sacks with corn, and to restore every man’s money into his sack, and to give them provision for the way: and thus did he unto them.* The gold put back in the sacks unknown in Jubilees 42:7 is Genesis' restored money.$n$),
(6121,28927,$n$Genesis 44:12 — *And he searched, and began at the eldest, and left at the youngest: and the cup was found in Benjamin’s sack* — the same search order and discovery that Jubilees 43:7 records.$n$),
(6134,28953,$n$Genesis 49:1 — *And Jacob called unto his sons, and said, Gather yourselves together, that I may tell you that which shall befall you in the last days.* Jubilees 45:14 says Israel made known to his sons what would come upon them in the last days — this is that deathbed prophecy.$n$),
(6133,28955,$n$Genesis 47:26 — *And Joseph made it a law over the land of Egypt unto this day, that Pharaoh should have the fifth part; except the land of the priests only, which became not Pharaoh’s.* Jubilees 45:12 calls it an ordinance for Egypt "until this day" — the very phrasing of Genesis.$n$),
(6133,28956,$n$Genesis 47:24 — *And it shall come to pass in the increase, that ye shall give the fifth part unto Pharaoh, and four parts shall be your own, for seed of the field, and for your food, and for them of your households, and for food for your little ones.* The fifth-for-the-king, four-parts-for-the-people split of Jubilees 45:12 is taken straight from Joseph's decree here.$n$),
(6138,28974,$n$Exodus 1:8 — *Now there arose up a new king over Egypt, which knew not Joseph.* The stronger new king of Jubilees 46:7 is Exodus' king who knew not Joseph.$n$),
(6145,28981,$n$Hebrews 11:27 — *By faith he forsook Egypt, not fearing the wrath of the king: for he endured, as seeing him who is invisible.* The flight from Pharaoh's wrath in Jubilees 47:12 is the faith Hebrews credits to Moses' forsaking of Egypt.$n$),
(6145,28982,$n$Acts 7:25 — *For he supposed his brethren would have understood how that Elohim (God) by his hand would deliver them: but they understood not.* The brother who turns on Moses in Jubilees 47:12 is Stephen's portrait of the deliverer rejected by his own.$n$),
(6145,28983,$n$Exodus 2:14 — *And he said, Who made thee a prince and a judge over us? intendest thou to kill me, as thou killedst the Egyptian? And Moses feared, and said, Surely this thing is known.* The rebuke and the fearful flight in Jubilees 47:12 quote Exodus 2:14 nearly verbatim.$n$),
(6149,29006,$n$Exodus 14:21 — *And Moses stretched out his hand over the sea; and Yahuah (LORD) caused the sea to go back by a strong east wind all that night, and made the sea dry land, and the waters were divided.* This is the sea crossed 'as if it were dry land' in Jubilees 48:13.$n$),
(6149,29007,$n$Exodus 14:19 — *And the angel of Elohim (God), which went before the camp of Yashar'el (Israel), removed and went behind them; and the pillar of the cloud went from before their face, and stood behind them.* This is the 'I stood between the Egyptians and Yashar'el' of Jubilees 48:13.$n$),
(42,4306,$n$Jubilees 10:1 — *the unclean demons began to lead astray the children of the sons of Noah.* The leading astray that outlived the flood wears the robes John calls a generation of vipers (Matthew 3:7).$n$),
(118,4463,$n$Jubilees 23:27 — *Till their days draw nigh to one thousand years.* The healing of the last days, tasted first in the blind seeing and the dead raised (Matthew 11:5).$n$),
(120,4466,$n$Jubilees 1:23 — *I shall circumcise the foreskin of their heart and the foreskin of the heart of their seed.* What John announced from outside, the least in the kingdom carries inside, a heart circumcised and a holy spirit created (Matthew 11:11).$n$),
(120,4467,$n$Jubilees 1:24 — *their souls will cleave to Me and to all My commandments, and they will fulfil My commandments.* The new heart keeps the commandments and the Father calls them children; this is what makes the least in the kingdom greater than the prophet (Matthew 11:11).$n$),
(120,4468,$n$Jubilees 1:25 — *they will all be called children of the living Elohim (God).* Hosea's name for the house once called Not My People, known in heaven as His children; the least of them is greater than John (Matthew 11:11).$n$),
(124,4478,$n$Jubilees 16:5 — *He burned them with fire and brimstone, and destroyed them until this day.* The measuring rod of judgment; Capernaum, which saw the mighty works, will fare worse than Sodom (Matthew 11:23).$n$),
(124,4479,$n$Jubilees 16:6 — *Elohim (God) will execute judgment on the places where they have done according to the uncleanness of the Sodomites.* Sodom's judgment as a pattern still in use; Yahusha (Jesus) lays it over Capernaum (Matthew 11:23).$n$),
(183,4493,$n$Jubilees 2:21 — *to eat and to drink, and to bless Him who has created all things.* The Sabbath as a day for eating and blessing the Creator; the Lord of the Sabbath defends his disciples' eating (Matthew 12:8).$n$),
(183,4494,$n$Jubilees 50:12 — *whoever fasts or makes war on the Sabbaths.* Jubilees' list of what the Sabbath forbids; doing well was never on it (Matthew 12:12).$n$),
(188,4510,$n$Jubilees 10:11 — *a tenth part of them we left that they might be subject before Satan on the earth.* Nine parts bound and a tenth left loose; the strong man is bound before his house is spoiled (Matthew 12:29).$n$),
(189,4511,$n$Jubilees 15:34 — *there will no more be pardon or forgiveness to them.* The deliberate forsaking of the covenant and the blasphemy against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) at Matthew 12:31 end in the same place, past forgiveness.$n$),
(191,4519,$n$Jubilees 23:30 — *Yahuah (God) will heal His servants, And they will rise up and see great peace.* The servants rise because the Master rose first, three days and three nights in the heart of the earth (Matthew 12:40).$n$),
(192,4521,$n$Jubilees 1:23 — *I shall create in them a holy spirit, and I shall cleanse them.* What must fill the swept house, or the seven return (Matthew 12:45).$n$),
(193,4525,$n$Jubilees 1:24 — *I shall be their Father and they will be My children.* The Father's children are the ones who cleave to His commandments; the same mark names the King's family (Matthew 12:50).$n$),
(195,4530,$n$Jubilees 11:11 — *the prince Mastêmâ sent ravens and birds to devour the seed which was sown in the land.* The birds that devour the seed were sent; the fowls by the way side are the wicked one (Matthew 13:4, 19).$n$),
(197,4538,$n$Jubilees 5:1 — *they took themselves wives of all whom they chose, and they bare to them sons and they were giants.* The first tares sown in the field while men slept (Matthew 13:25).$n$),
(197,4539,$n$Jubilees 7:21 — *the Watchers against the law of their ordinances went a whoring after the daughters of men.* The seed that was never the Father's; the tares are the children of the wicked one (Matthew 13:38).$n$),
(203,4558,$n$Jubilees 4:2 — *Cain slew Abel because Elohim (God) accepted the sacrifice of Abel.* The first of the children of the wicked one, *who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother* (1 John 3:12; Matthew 13:38).$n$),
(222,4590,$n$Jubilees 15:30 — *he chose them not because they are the children of Abraham, because He knew them, but He chose Yashar’el (Israel).* Not every child of Abraham is the chosen seed; the woman on the coast of Canaan who knew the Son of David is one of the lost sheep he was sent to (Matthew 15:22).$n$),
(222,4591,$n$Jubilees 15:31 — *over all has He placed spirits in authority to lead them astray from Him.* The nations under spirits, Yashar'el (Israel) gathered out to be ruled directly; the children's bread (Matthew 15:26).$n$),
(222,4592,$n$Jubilees 15:32 — *over Yashar’el (Israel) He did not appoint any angel or spirit, for He alone is their ruler.* The flock the Father shepherds Himself; the children's bread is theirs, and the woman on the coast of Canaan was one of the children (Matthew 15:26).$n$),
(222,4593,$n$Jubilees 16:17 — *from the sons of Isaac one should become a holy seed, and should not be reckoned among the nations.* The holy seed not reckoned among the nations; the children's bread (Matthew 15:26).$n$),
(223,4594,$n$Jubilees 50:1 — *I made known to you the days of the Sabbaths in the desert of Sinai.* The Sabbaths built on seven; the second feeding leaves seven baskets (Matthew 15:37).$n$),
(223,4595,$n$Jubilees 50:2 — *I told you of the jubilee years in the sabbaths of years.* The jubilee counted in sevens; seven baskets left over at the second feeding (Matthew 15:37).$n$),
(223,4596,$n$Jubilees 50:4 — *there are forty-nine jubilees from the days of Adam until this day.* The world counted in sevens from Adam; seven baskets over when the jubilee has a face (Matthew 15:37).$n$),
(376,4699,$n$Jubilees 3:4 — *It is not good that the man should be alone: let us make a helpmeet for him.* The beginning the King reads from before he reads Moses (Matthew 19:4).$n$),
(376,4700,$n$Jubilees 3:6 — *This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she will be called my wife.* One flesh because she was taken from him; the *for this cause* of Matthew 19:5.$n$),
(380,4716,$n$Jubilees 1:15 — *I shall gather them from amongst all the nations.* The gathering that puts twelve tribes under twelve thrones (Matthew 19:28).$n$),
(380,4717,$n$Jubilees 31:15 — *they will be princes and judges, and chiefs of all the seed of the sons of Jacob.* Levi's blessing of judgeship over Jacob's seed; twelve thrones over the twelve tribes (Matthew 19:28).$n$),
(401,4809,$n$Jubilees 5:2 — *lawlessness increased on the earth and all flesh corrupted its way.* The days of Noah were days of lawlessness; so will the coming of the Son of Adam be (Matthew 24:37).$n$),
(401,4810,$n$Jubilees 5:19 — *no man’s person was accepted save that of Noah alone.* The one accepted was the one left; the flood took the rest (Matthew 24:38).$n$),
(424,4905,$n$Jubilees 1:15 — *they will turn to Me from amongst the nations with all their heart and with all their soul.* The gathering from among all nations, carried out by sending to all nations (Matthew 28:19).$n$),
(424,4906,$n$Jubilees 1:23 — *I shall cleanse them so that they shall not turn away from Me from that day to eternity.* The new heart that keeps what the disciples are sent to teach (Matthew 28:20).$n$),
(424,4907,$n$Jubilees 1:25 — *they will know that these are My children, and that I am their Father.* Hosea's sons of the living Elohim (God), sought where they were scattered (Matthew 28:19).$n$),
(188,7916,$n$Jubilees 10:11 — *all the malignant evil ones we bound in the place of condemnation.* The binding begun at Noah's prayer, finished by the stronger one who divides the spoils (Luke 11:22).$n$),
(649,7942,$n$Jubilees 5:2 — *every imagination of the thoughts of all men (was) thus evil continually.* What lay under the eating and drinking of Noah's days (Luke 17:27).$n$),
(3781,20338,$n$Jubilees 14:14 — *after that they will come forth thence with much substance.* The exodus promised to Abram in the same breath as the bondage (Genesis 15:14).$n$),
(5172,25564,$n$Jubilees 1:26 — *the first and the last, which shall come to pass in all the divisions of the days.* Written before it happens, as David's members were written in the book before there was one of them (Psalm 139:16).$n$),
(5193,25658,$n$Jubilees 5:13 — *the judgment of all is ordained and written on the heavenly tables in righteousness.* The same pre-written book David saw in Psalm 139:16, opened to the page of judgment.$n$),
(5679,27374,$n$Jubilees 31:32 — *they record it as an eternal testimony to them on the heavenly tables how Isaac blessed them.* The blessing written in the same book where David's members were written (Psalm 139:16).$n$),
(5683,27398,$n$Jubilees 32:21 — *an angel descended from heaven with seven tablets in his hands, and he gave them to Jacob.* Jacob read what would befall his sons through all the ages, already written, as David's days were written before they came (Psalm 139:16).$n$),
(5681,27403,$n$Jubilees 32:10 — *in the place where it is chosen that His name should dwell, and to this law there is no limit.* The tithe law of Deuteronomy 14:23 was on the heavenly tables before Sinai, with no limit of days.$n$),
(5861,28021,$n$Jubilees 33:16 — *there had not been revealed the ordinance and judgment and law in its completeness for all.* Written before it was revealed, the way David's days were written before they came (Psalm 139:16).$n$),
(5897,28126,$n$Jubilees 40:7 — *made him ruler over all his house, and magnified him.* Pharaoh did the clothing and the speaking, but Psalm 105:21 says who made Joseph lord: *He made him lord of his house*, and the He is Yahuah (God).$n$),
(5894,28136,$n$Jubilees 40:3 — *seven years of famine, such a famine as has not been in all the land.* The same reading Joseph gives Pharaoh in Genesis 41:30.$n$),
(6126,28944,$n$Jubilees 44:6 — *I shall go down with you, and I shall bring you up (again).* The oath of Genesis 15:14, in and judged and out, spoken to Jacob at the door of Egypt.$n$),
(6188,29154,$n$Jubilees 5:8 — *Your spirit will not always abide on man; for they also are flesh.* The hundred and twenty years that Jasher 5:8 calls a space to turn before the flood.$n$),
(6191,29167,$n$Jubilees 5:22 — *Noah made the ark in all respects as He commanded him.* The obedient building Jasher 5:33 records, here given its date.$n$),
(6199,29198,$n$Jubilees 8:10 — *they divided the earth into three parts, for Shem and Ham and Japheth.* The three sons Jasher 7:1 names become the three portions of the earth.$n$),
(6203,29214,$n$Jubilees 8:8 — *for in the days when he was born the children of Noah began to divide the earth amongst themselves.* The same reason for the name that Jasher 7:19 gives.$n$),
(6204,29219,$n$Jubilees 10:19 — *in his days they built the city and the tower, saying, “Go to, let us ascend thereby into heaven.”* The city Jasher 7:42 says Nimrod's counselors advised him to build.$n$),
(6269,29441,$n$Jubilees 9:3 — *for Asshur came forth the second portion, all the land of Asshur and Nineveh and Shinar.* The land Jasher 10:32 says Asshur went out and built four cities in.$n$),
(6267,29447,$n$Jubilees 9:1 — *Ham divided amongst his sons, and the first portion came forth for Cush towards the east.* The four sons of Ham that Jasher 10:19 names, receiving their lots.$n$),
(6266,29451,$n$Jubilees 9:8 — *the first portion came forth for Gomer to the east from the north side to the river Tînâ.* The sons of Japheth Jasher 10:7 lists, Gomer and Magog, given their borders.$n$),
(6265,29454,$n$Jubilees 10:25 — *from thence they were dispersed into their cities, each according to his language and his nation.* The scattering from the tower that Jasher 10:2 opens with.$n$),
(6264,29459,$n$Jubilees 8:8 — *in the days when he was born the children of Noah began to divide the earth amongst themselves.* The birth of the Peleg whose death Jasher 10:1 records.$n$),
(6281,29507,$n$Jubilees 12:28 — *he would leave Haran to go into the land of Canaan to see it and return to him.* Leaving the Haran stop that Jasher 13:1 says Terah settled into.$n$),
(6283,29515,$n$Jubilees 13:3 — *“To you and to your seed will I give this land.”* The land grant at Abram's entry that Jasher 13:7 records.$n$),
(6408,29977,$n$Jubilees 16:10 — *Abraham moved from Hebron, and departed and dwelt between Kadesh and Shur in the mountains of Gerar.* The move to Gerar that opens Jasher 20:1.$n$),
(6422,29999,$n$Jubilees 17:17 — *Yahuah (God) knew that Abraham was faithful in all his afflictions.* The trials already passed before the one Jasher 22:45 says Yahuah (the Lord) thought to try him with.$n$),
(6418,30010,$n$Jubilees 16:11 — *he moved from thence, and dwelt at the Well of the Oath.* Beersheba, named for the oath Jasher 22:9 records.$n$),
(6423,30029,$n$Jubilees 18:2 — *Take your beloved son whom you love, (even) Isaac, and go to the high country.* The command that followed the "Here I am" of Jasher 23:1.$n$),
(6431,30037,$n$Jubilees 19:2 — *the days of the life of Sarah were accomplished, and she died in Hebron.* The death Jasher 24:14 says Shem and Eber followed to the bier.$n$),
(6429,30043,$n$Jubilees 19:8 — *This is the tenth trial wherewith Abraham was tried, and he was found faithful, patient in spirit.* Burying Sarah (Jasher 24:1) counted as Abraham's tenth trial.$n$),
(6575,30534,$n$Jubilees 22:1 — *Isaac and Ishmael came from the Well of the Oath to celebrate the feast of weeks.* The last feast of the year that Jasher 26:31 closes with Abraham's burial.$n$),
(6573,30541,$n$Jubilees 19:13 — *Rebecca bare to Isaac two sons, Jacob and Esau, and Jacob was a smooth and upright man.* The twins whose birth Jasher 26:15 records, already divided.$n$),
(6609,30651,$n$Jubilees 29:13 — *he was reconciled to him, and departed from him to the land of Seir, but Jacob dwelt in tents.* The parting Jasher 32:73 shows from the road, Esau back to Seir.$n$),
(6803,31356,$n$Jubilees 32:1 — *Levi dreamed that they had ordained and made him the priest of the El Elyon (Most High) Elohim (God).* The Bethel night that Jasher 36:1 sent Jacob to.$n$),
(6804,31358,$n$Jubilees 32:30 — *Deborah Rebecca’s nurse died, and they buried her beneath the city under the oak of the river.* The mourning oak Jasher 36:6 names Allon-bachuth.$n$),
(6805,31363,$n$Jubilees 32:17 — *“Your name shall not be called Jacob, but Yashar’el (Israel) shall they name your name.”* The renaming at Bethel that Jasher 36:8 records.$n$),
(6806,31369,$n$Jubilees 33:2 — *Reuben saw Bilhah, Rachel’s maid, the concubine of his father, bathing in water in a secret place.* The sin Jasher 36:14 tells from the side of Reuben's anger.$n$),
(6807,31372,$n$Jubilees 33:22 — *these were the names of the sons of Jacob: the first-born Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Yahudah (Judah).* The twelve that Jasher 36:16 counts, here named by mother.$n$),
(7062,32259,$n$Jubilees 34:11 — *they dealt treacherously with him, and formed a plot against him to slay him.* The sale that the bloodied coat of Jasher 43:13 was sent to hide.$n$),
(7071,32268,$n$Jubilees 39:11 — *cast Joseph into prison into the place where the prisoners were kept whom the king imprisoned.* The king's own prison, where Jasher 44:76 keeps him twelve years and where the road to Pharaoh began.$n$),
(7069,32275,$n$Jubilees 39:6 — *he remembered Yahuah (God) and the words which Jacob, his father, used to read from amongst the words of Abraham.* The commandment Joseph kept in Jasher 44:78 was read aloud in his father's house before Sinai wrote it on stone.$n$),
(7067,32280,$n$Jubilees 39:3 — *the blessing of Yahuah (God) came upon the house of the Egyptian on account of Joseph.* The overseer of Jasher 44:11 brought the blessing of Abraham's house into a stranger's house.$n$),
(7076,32288,$n$Jubilees 41:19 — *Tamar is more righteous than I am. And therefore let them burn her not.* Yahudah's (Judah's) confession at the end of the road Tamar sat down on in Jasher 45:31.$n$),
(7075,32292,$n$Jubilees 41:5 — *Onan knew that the seed would not be his, (but) his brother’s only.* Why the work of Jasher 45:26 was evil: he would not raise a seed for his dead brother.$n$),
(7094,32350,$n$Jubilees 40:3 — *he said before Pharaoh that his two dreams were one.* The same seven and seven that Jasher 48:55 unfolds, one reading for two dreams.$n$),
(7093,32355,$n$Jubilees 40:5 — *for the spirit of Yahuah (God) is with him.* Pharaoh's confession of the Spirit that Jasher 48:52 says clothed Joseph before the king.$n$),
(7092,32358,$n$Jubilees 40:2 — *the chief butler remembered Joseph and spake of him to the king.* The remembrance that Jasher 48:33 puts into the butler's own words before Pharaoh.$n$),
(7090,32365,$n$Jubilees 40:1 — *Pharaoh dreamed two dreams in one night concerning a famine which was to be in all the land.* The dreams Jasher 48:2 begins at the river, and the magicians who could not read them.$n$),
(7366,33322,$n$Jubilees 40:8 — *he had no respect of persons, and did not accept gifts, but he judged in uprightness.* The ruler under whom the seven years of plenty in Jasher 50:7 came to the land.$n$),
(7371,33342,$n$Jubilees 42:4 — *he sent his ten sons that they should procure food for him in Egypt; but Benjamin he did not send.* The descent of the ten that Jasher 51:1 opens, with Benjamin kept at home.$n$),
(7389,33396,$n$Jubilees 43:14 — *Joseph saw that they were all accordant in goodness one with another, and he could not refrain himself.* The moment Joseph could hold back no longer, and the words he said in Jasher 54:69.$n$),
(7388,33400,$n$Jubilees 43:13 — *let me, your servant, abide instead of the boy as a bondsman to my lord.* The surety plea of Jasher 54:57, with Yahudah (Judah) offering himself in Benjamin's place.$n$),
(7717,34520,$n$Jubilees 38:16 — *Bâlâq, the son of Beor, reigned in Edom, and the name of his city was Danâbâ.* The first king of Edom, whose crowning Jasher 57:42 shows with the shout, May the king live.$n$),
(7716,34523,$n$Jubilees 38:10 — *the sons of Jacob pressed hard upon the sons of Esau in the mountains of Seir, and bowed their necks.* Esau's sons bowed under Jacob's sons, and in Jasher 57:35 bowing the Horites under themselves.$n$),
(7714,34532,$n$Jubilees 38:8 — *they slew of Edom and of the Horites four hundred men, stout warriors.* The war in which Esau fell; Jasher 57:2 is the later one where Joseph took Zepho in chains.$n$),
(7734,34588,$n$Jubilees 46:1 — *they became a great nation, and they were of one accord in heart, so that brother loved brother.* The multiplying that Jasher 62:1 counts by the deaths of the fathers, Reuben first.$n$),
(7732,34595,$n$Jubilees 46:8 — *they buried him in the land of Egypt, and his brethren died after him.* The brethren dying after Joseph, counted one by one in Jasher 62; here Yahudah (Judah) at 129.$n$),
(7740,34627,$n$Jubilees 46:8 — *Joseph died in the forty-sixth jubilee, in the sixth week, in the second year.* The death that Zepho was waiting for in Jasher 64:8 before he came against the children.$n$),
(8095,35854,$n$Jubilees 46:15 — *the more they dealt evilly with them, the more they increased and multiplied.* The rule Jasher 67:59 proves with ploughs that could not hurt the infants.$n$),
(8092,35863,$n$Jubilees 47:2 — *they should cast all their male children which were born into the river.* The decree Balaam counselled in Jasher 67:49 to wipe away their name.$n$),
(8099,35877,$n$Jubilees 47:5 — *Tharmuth, the daughter of Pharaoh, came to bathe in the river, and she heard your voice crying.* Pharaoh's daughter at the river, named Bathia in Jasher 68:18, lifting the child out of the decree.$n$),
(8096,35889,$n$Jubilees 47:2 — *Pharaoh, king of Egypt, issued a command regarding them.* The decree that Jasher 68:11 shows carried out by Pharaoh's officers all the days.$n$),
(8108,35915,$n$Jubilees 47:9 — *Amram your father taught you writing, and after you had completed three weeks they brought you into the royal court.* The son of Pharaoh's daughter in Jasher 70:33, who was taught by Amram before the court had him.$n$),
(8116,35953,$n$Jubilees 47:10 — *did see an Egyptian smiting your friend who was of the children of Yashar’el (Israel), and you did slay him.* The slaying that sent Moses out of Egypt in Jasher 72:21.$n$),
(8498,37310,$n$Jubilees 14:13 — *your seed shall be a stranger in a land (that is) not theirs, and they will bring them into bondage.* The appointed years that the sons of Ephraim in Jasher 75:2 counted finished before they were.$n$),
(8503,37325,$n$Jubilees 48:1 — *you did depart and dwell in the land of Midian five weeks and one year.* The length of the sojourn that Jasher 76:13 begins at a well.$n$),
(8515,37347,$n$Jubilees 48:7 — *ten great and terrible judgments came on the land of Egypt that you might execute vengeance on it.* The answer to the cries Yahuah (the Lord) heard in Jasher 78:15.$n$),
(8512,37356,$n$Jubilees 48:1 — *you did return into Egypt in the second week in the second year in the fiftieth jubilee.* The dated sojourn in which Jasher 78:7 places the marriage to Zipporah.$n$),
(8940,38882,$n$Jubilees 48:19 — *we did not lead forth the children of Yashar’el (Israel) from Egypt empty handed.* The going out that Jasher 81:4 sums up with a strong hand after two hundred and ten years.$n$),
(8997,39065,$n$Jubilees 46:9 — *brought forth all the bones of the children of Jacob save the bones of Joseph.* The bones of the brethren going home; Jasher 90:38 buries them later, each in his own inheritance.$n$),
(9001,39088,$n$Jubilees 13:20 — *all the land which you see I shall give to you and to your seed for ever.* The oath to Abram that Jasher 91:15 shows kept to his seed.$n$),
(11049,46049,$n$Jubilees 3:26 — *He made for them coats of skin, and clothed them, and sent them forth from the Garden of Eden.* The expulsion that 1 Adam and Eve 54:3 guards with a cherub at the gate.$n$),
(42186,172794,$n$Jubilees 1:11 — *they will make to themselves high places and groves and graven images.* The pattern foretold to Moses, which Jeroboam built and staffed in 2 Chronicles 11:15.$n$),
(30,4268,$n$Jubilees 46:1 — *the children of Yashar’el (Israel) multiplied in the land of Egypt, and they became a great nation.* Egypt sheltered the nation under one Joseph and the child under another (Matthew 2:14).$n$),
(34,4279,$n$Jubilees 2:20 — *have written him down as My firstborn son, and have sanctified him to Myself for ever and ever.* The son called out of Egypt (Matthew 2:15) is the firstborn son written down at creation.$n$),
(36,4284,$n$Jubilees 32:34 — *she was buried in the land of Ephrath, the same is Bethlehem.* Rachel weeps at Matthew 2:18 from a grave on the Bethlehem road.$n$),
(43,4308,$n$Jubilees 10:8 — *let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice.* The devil who tempted Yahusha (Jesus) in the wilderness (Matthew 4:1) works inside a permission he asked for.$n$),
(43,4309,$n$Jubilees 11:11 — *the prince Mastêmâ sent ravens and birds to devour the seed which was sown in the land.* The enemy who stole the bread from the fields is the tempter who held out bread in the wilderness (Matthew 4:3).$n$),
(43,4310,$n$Jubilees 17:16 — *bid him offer him as a burnt-offering on the altar, and You will see if he will do this command.* The accuser who proposed Abraham's trial proposes the Son's on the pinnacle (Matthew 4:5).$n$),
(2740,16266,$n$Jubilees 5:12 — *He made for all His works a new and righteous nature.* The plant of righteousness and truth of 1 Enoch 10:16 is this new nature made after the flood.$n$),
(2870,16776,$n$Jubilees 4:26 — *Mount Zion (which) will be sanctified in the new creation for a sanctification of the earth.* The mountain of 1 Enoch 25:3, whose summit is His throne, stands among the four holy places of the earth.$n$),
(3772,20284,$n$Jubilees 13:15 — *he journeyed to the place where he had pitched his tent at the beginning, to the place of the altar.* The return of Genesis 13:4, with the words of the calling on the name given in the next verse.$n$),
(3779,20326,$n$Jubilees 14:6 — *he believed in Yahuah (God), and it was counted to him for righteousness.* Genesis 15:6 word for word, spoken inside the covenant that names the land and the seed in the next breath.$n$),
(4731,23895,$n$Jubilees 5:24 — *the mouths of the fountains of the great deep, seven mouths in number.* The fountains Enoch saw opened on the surface of the enclosure (1 Enoch 89:3).$n$),
(5190,25648,$n$Jubilees 5:2 — *they began to devour each other.* The devouring has a face: the giants *Who consumed all the acquisitions of men* (1 Enoch 7:3).$n$),
(5665,27347,$n$Jubilees 27:27 — *so that I come again to my father’s house in peace, then shall Yahuah (God) be my Elohim (God).* The dream at Jubilees 29:3 that sends him back to his father's house answers this vow.$n$),
(7364,33300,$n$Jubilees 40:10 — *the king called Joseph’s name Sĕphânṭîphâns.* The new name Jasher 49:21 gives as Zaphnath Paaneah.$n$),
(8082,35824,$n$Jubilees 46:14 — *they built strong cities for Pharaoh, Pithom and Raamses.* The same two cities the Egyptians resolved to fortify at Jasher 65:8.$n$),
(11025,45959,$n$Jubilees 3:22 — *he took fig-leaves and sewed (them) together, and made an apron for himself, and covered his shame.* The serpent cursed in the next line is the Satan who calls his hosts against the man at 1 Adam and Eve 48:1.$n$),
(12089,49885,$n$Jubilees 16:20 — *he celebrated a festival of joy in this month seven days.* The calling on the name at Beer-sheba (Genesis 21:33) was the first feast of tabernacles on the earth.$n$),
(12782,52853,$n$Jubilees 32:19 — *they will get possession of the whole earth and inherit it for ever.* The land of Genesis 35:12 widened to the whole earth for Jacob's seed.$n$),
(14883,60478,$n$Jubilees 5:24 — *opened seven flood-gates of heaven, And the mouths of the fountains of the great deep.* The world Peter says *being overflowed with water, perished* (2 Peter 3:6).$n$),
(659,7988,$n$Jubilees 1:25 — *they will all be called children of the living Elohim (God)... that these are My children, and that I am their Father.* *Ye must be born again* (John 3:7) is the doorway into the children-of-the-living the Hebrew library names as the destination of the heart-circumcision — the sonship promised the gathered who were once *not my people.*$n$),
(1902,12765,$n$Jubilees 16:13 — *at the time of which Yahuah (God) had spoken... on the festival of the first-fruits of the harvest, Isaac was born* the promise-child born on an appointed feast of the Most High (Galatians 4:23).$n$),
(5675,27387,$n$Jubilees 27:26 — *And Jacob arose early in the morning, and took the stone which he had put under his head and set it up as a pillar for a sign, and he poured oil upon the top of it.* The pillar and the sacrifice of Jubilees 31:3 pay the vow Jubilees first recorded at the ladder.$n$),
(5896,28131,$n$Jubilees 39:12 — *Yahuah (God) gave Joseph favour in the sight of the chief of the prison guards and compassion before him, for he saw that Yahuah (God) was with him, and that Yahuah (God) made all that he did to prosper.* The upright Joseph of Jubilees 40:8 is the same man who kept his purity in Jubilees 39:12 — “Yahuah was with him” ties the pit to the throne.$n$),
(6174,29083,$n$Jubilees 4:15 — *Mahalalel took to him to wife Dînâh, the daughter of Barâkî’êl the daughter of his father’s brother, and she bare him a son... and he called his name Jared; for in his days the angels of Yahuah (God) descended on the earth, those who are named the Watchers, that they should instruct the children of men.* Jubilees carries the same Mahalaleel-Jared generation of Jasher 2:37 and marks Jared's days as when the Watchers came down — the corruption Jasher's next chapters and 1 Enoch unfold.$n$),
(6176,29110,$n$Jubilees 4:17 — *And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book.* the wisdom Jasher 3:8 says the spirit of Elohim gave Enoch to teach all his men.$n$),
(6184,29137,$n$Jubilees 5:19 — *...no man’s person was accepted save that of Noah alone; for his person was accepted in behalf of his sons... for his heart was righteous in all his ways...* the same righteous Noah Jasher 4:15 says walked perfect and upright with Elohim while all flesh corrupted.$n$),
(6210,29225,$n$Jubilees 12:4 — *Worship the Elohim (God) of heaven, Who causes the rain and the dew to descend on the earth, And does everything upon the earth, And has created everything by His word.* Jubilees gives the same father — an idolater Abram must rebuke — that Jasher shows bargaining with Nimrod.$n$),
(8941,38878,$n$Jubilees 48:12 — *the prince of the Mastêmâ... cried to the Egyptians to pursue after you with all the powers of the Egyptians, with their chariots, and with their horses, and with all the hosts of the peoples of Egypt.* Jubilees tells the same pursuit that overtakes Israel at the sea in Jasher 81:24.$n$),
(11081,46157,$n$Jubilees 10:9 — *Let the tenth part of them remain before him, and let nine parts descend into the place of condemnation.* As mercy curbs Mastema to a permitted tenth here, so mercy in 1 Adam & Eve 62:3 lets the buried figs live rather than perish.$n$),
(11240,46723,$n$Jubilees 4:17 — *And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book.* Enoch who "wrote a celebrated book" (22:2) is Jubilees' first man to write a testimony.$n$),
(11771,48691,$n$Jubilees 12:19 — *You alone are my Elohim (God), And You and Your dominion have I chosen. And You have created all things, And all things that are are the work of Your hands.* The parallel restored record gives Abraham's own confession of the Creator that meets the "I am He" of Apocalypse of Abraham 8:3.$n$),
(17390,69521,$n$Jubilees 4:17 — *And he was the first among men that are born on earth who learnt writing and knowledge and wisdom and who wrote down the signs of heaven according to the order of their months in a book.* Enoch the first writer is the pattern of Eve's charge — the antediluvian life set down in a testimony that outlasts the generations.$n$),
(35119,141652,$n$Jubilees 5:1 — *the angels of Elohim (God) saw them... and they took themselves wives* — the Genesis 6 fall recorded; the servants Yahuah put no trust in.$n$);
